window.ENG_READING_ADVANCED = [{"no": 1, "passages": [{"title": "Artificial Intelligence in Medical Diagnosis", "text": "The integration of artificial intelligence into medical diagnosis represents one of the most profound transformations in contemporary healthcare, yet its promise is accompanied by considerable uncertainty. Machine learning systems, trained on vast datasets of medical images and patient records, can now identify patterns that often escape human observation, detecting early signs of cancer, retinal disease, and neurological disorders with remarkable accuracy. Proponents argue that such tools will reduce diagnostic errors, alleviate the burden on overworked physicians, and extend high-quality care to regions where specialists are scarce. Nevertheless, the technology raises fundamental questions about accountability, transparency, and the nature of clinical judgment. When an algorithm recommends a treatment that proves harmful, it remains unclear whether responsibility lies with the developer, the hospital, or the doctor who accepted the suggestion. Moreover, many advanced models operate as black boxes, producing conclusions without offering a comprehensible explanation, a feature that conflicts with the traditional expectation that medical decisions should be traceable and justifiable. Critics also warn that datasets may reflect historical biases, causing systems to perform less reliably for underrepresented groups and thereby deepening existing health inequalities. Despite these concerns, most experts do not advocate abandoning the technology. Instead, they propose a collaborative model in which artificial intelligence serves as an assistant rather than a replacement, flagging suspicious cases for human review while leaving final decisions to clinicians. Such an approach, they contend, preserves professional expertise while exploiting computational power. The challenge, therefore, is not merely technical but institutional: hospitals must establish clear protocols, regulators must define standards for validation, and medical education must prepare future doctors to interpret algorithmic outputs critically. Only through such deliberate governance can the benefits of artificial intelligence be realized without compromising patient trust or safety. Looking ahead, the most promising path may involve continuous monitoring of AI performance after deployment, so that errors are detected early and models are updated as medical knowledge evolves. Such vigilance, combined with honest communication with patients about how algorithms assist their care, could turn a powerful but imperfect tool into a trustworthy partner in medicine.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["AI will soon replace doctors in all diagnostic work.", "AI in diagnosis offers great benefits but raises serious questions that require careful governance.", "Medical AI has already solved the problem of diagnostic errors.", "Hospitals should avoid using AI until it becomes fully transparent."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章主张人机协作而非取代医生；B 正确，概括了益处与问责、透明、偏见等问题及治理需求；C 错误，文章说错误仍存在且有不确定性；D 错误，专家并不主张弃用，而是规范使用。"}, {"prompt": "According to the passage, why is the “black box” nature of AI a problem?", "options": ["It makes AI systems too slow for clinical use.", "It prevents doctors from understanding and justifying medical decisions.", "It increases the cost of medical equipment.", "It makes AI unable to process medical images."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章未提速度问题；B 正确，黑箱结论缺乏可理解的解释，与可追溯、可辩护的医疗决策要求冲突；C 错误，未提成本；D 错误，AI 能处理图像。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author imply about biased datasets?", "options": ["They are irrelevant to AI performance.", "They may worsen health inequalities for underrepresented groups.", "They only affect research, not clinical practice.", "They have already been completely eliminated."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，偏见会影响性能；B 正确，数据集可能反映历史偏见，导致对代表性不足群体的表现较差；C 错误，临床也会受影响；D 错误，文章说这是警告而非已解决。"}, {"prompt": "What model do most experts support?", "options": ["AI as an assistant that flags cases for human review.", "AI as the sole decision-maker in hospitals.", "A complete ban on AI in medicine.", "AI used only in regions without specialists."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，专家提倡协作模式，AI 辅助、医生做最终决定；B 错误，与主张相反；C 错误，专家不主张放弃；D 错误，应用范围不限于缺医地区。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward AI in medicine?", "options": ["Cautiously optimistic, stressing governance and human oversight.", "Entirely negative and distrustful.", "Uncritically enthusiastic.", "Indifferent to its effects."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，作者肯定益处同时强调制度、监管与教育；B 错误，作者未全盘否定；C 错误，作者列举诸多担忧；D 错误，作者态度明确。"}]}, {"title": "The Hidden Cost of Digital Convenience", "text": "Modern life is increasingly defined by convenience: groceries arrive within an hour, messages receive instant replies, and entertainment is available at the touch of a screen. This frictionless existence, however, conceals a subtle psychological cost that researchers are only beginning to understand. When every desire can be satisfied immediately, the capacity to tolerate delay, uncertainty, and effort gradually weakens. Psychologists describe this phenomenon as a shrinking of frustration tolerance, a trait closely linked to perseverance, creativity, and long-term achievement. Children who grow up surrounded by instant rewards may find it harder to persist through difficult homework, boring practice, or ambiguous social situations, not because they lack intelligence but because they have rarely needed to wait. Adults are not immune either. The habit of switching tasks at the first sign of boredom, checking phones during conversations, and abandoning books after a few pages reflects a mind trained to expect constant stimulation. Some scholars argue that convenience itself is not the enemy; rather, the problem lies in the absence of deliberate friction. Activities that require sustained attention, such as learning a musical instrument, mastering a language, or building a meaningful relationship, inherently involve periods of discomfort and slow progress. Removing all obstacles from daily life may therefore deprive people of the very experiences that build resilience. The solution, according to recent studies, is not to reject technology but to design intentional pauses into everyday routines: leaving phones in another room during meals, choosing slower modes of travel occasionally, or committing to one demanding hobby. Such practices do not restore an imagined past; they simply preserve the psychological muscles that convenience tends to atrophy. In a society that prizes speed above all else, the willingness to wait may become a rare and valuable skill. Schools and families can play a part by protecting time for slow activities, such as reading a long book or practicing an instrument, and by praising persistence rather than only speed. Over time, these small choices help young people discover that waiting is not wasted time but the space in which attention, creativity, and character quietly grow.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the central argument of the passage?", "options": ["Technology should be abandoned to restore patience.", "Instant convenience may weaken the ability to tolerate delay and effort.", "Modern people are naturally less patient than previous generations.", "Entertainment is the main cause of social problems."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者主张有意设置停顿而非抛弃技术；B 正确，核心是即时满足削弱忍耐力；C 错误，文章归因于环境训练而非天性；D 错误，娱乐只是例子之一。"}, {"prompt": "The phrase “psychological muscles” most likely refers to ______.", "options": ["physical strength gained from exercise", "mental capacities such as patience and resilience", "the ability to use digital devices quickly", "memory skills for academic tests"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，非指身体力量；B 正确，比喻忍耐、坚持等心理能力；C 错误，与设备操作无关；D 错误，范围过窄。"}, {"prompt": "According to the passage, why do some children struggle with difficult tasks?", "options": ["Because they are less intelligent than earlier generations.", "Because they have seldom needed to wait for rewards.", "Because schools no longer teach perseverance.", "Because their parents forbid all technology."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章明确说不是智力问题；B 正确，即时奖励环境使他们很少需要等待；C 错误，未提学校教学；D 错误，未提禁止技术。"}, {"prompt": "What solution does the author suggest?", "options": ["Banning smartphones completely.", "Designing intentional pauses and friction into daily life.", "Returning to life without any modern technology.", "Avoiding all demanding hobbies."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者反对彻底拒绝技术；B 正确，建议吃饭不带手机、偶尔慢行、坚持一项费力的爱好；C 错误，非回到过去；D 错误，作者鼓励坚持 demanding hobby。"}, {"prompt": "What can be inferred about the author's view of speed?", "options": ["Speed is always beneficial in modern society.", "The willingness to wait may become valuable precisely because speed is overvalued.", "Speed has no effect on human psychology.", "Only children are affected by speed."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者批判唯速度论；B 正确，结尾指出在崇尚速度的社会里，愿意等待会成为稀缺而宝贵的技能；C 错误，文章论证速度有心理影响；D 错误，成人同样受影响。"}]}]}, {"no": 2, "passages": [{"title": "Can Cities Learn to Live with Rising Water?", "text": "As sea levels rise and extreme rainfall becomes more frequent, coastal cities face a dilemma that cannot be solved by concrete alone. For decades, the dominant response to flooding was to build higher walls, stronger pumps, and deeper drains, an approach rooted in the belief that nature could be held back indefinitely. Recent disasters, however, have exposed the limits of this strategy: when barriers fail, the damage is often catastrophic because development behind them assumed absolute safety. In response, a growing number of planners advocate a more adaptive philosophy known as living with water. This approach combines engineered defenses with natural buffers such as restored wetlands, permeable pavements, and parks designed to flood safely during storms. Rotterdam, for example, has created public squares that serve as playgrounds in dry weather and reservoirs during heavy rain, while Singapore integrates drainage channels into landscaped corridors. Advocates argue that such designs not only reduce risk but also improve urban life, providing green space, cooling neighborhoods, and supporting biodiversity. Critics caution that adaptation is not a panacea. Retrofitting dense cities is expensive, land is scarce, and poorer districts often receive investment last, raising concerns about environmental justice. Moreover, adaptation without reducing greenhouse gas emissions merely postpones the inevitable, as rising seas will eventually overwhelm even flexible systems. Nevertheless, most experts agree that neither pure resistance nor pure retreat is sufficient. The future likely involves a hybrid strategy: protecting critical infrastructure, allowing some areas to flood temporarily, and gradually relocating the most vulnerable communities with fair compensation. Such decisions require not only engineering expertise but also political courage, since they challenge property values, cultural attachment to place, and short-term economic interests. Cities that begin this difficult conversation early, scholars suggest, will suffer less than those that wait for the next disaster to force it. Insurance and planning rules will also need to change, so that new homes are not built in the most dangerous places and residents understand the risks they face. When governments, insurers, and communities share information honestly, adaptation becomes a collective project rather than a burden carried alone by those living closest to the water.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To argue that flood walls are the only effective solution.", "To introduce an adaptive approach to urban flooding and discuss its challenges.", "To describe the history of Rotterdam's architecture.", "To prove that climate change will not affect coastal cities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章指出单纯筑墙有局限；B 正确，介绍与水共存的适应策略并讨论代价与公平问题；C 错误，鹿特丹只是例子；D 错误，与文章前提相反。"}, {"prompt": "Why did traditional flood defenses sometimes cause greater damage?", "options": ["Because they were built too cheaply.", "Because people behind them assumed they were completely safe.", "Because they increased rainfall.", "Because they were never tested."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，未提造价；B 正确，屏障后的开发假定绝对安全，一旦失效损失惨重；C 错误，防洪设施不增加降雨；D 错误，未提未测试。"}, {"prompt": "What is an example of “living with water”?", "options": ["Building higher concrete walls everywhere.", "Squares that act as playgrounds normally and reservoirs in storms.", "Banning all construction near coasts.", "Pumping water permanently out to sea."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，属于旧式抵抗策略；B 正确，鹿特丹广场干时游乐、雨时蓄水；C 错误，未提禁建；D 错误，未提永久抽水。"}, {"prompt": "What concern do critics raise about adaptation?", "options": ["It is too simple to implement.", "Poorer districts may receive investment last.", "It will eliminate all green space.", "It requires no political decisions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，改造密集城市昂贵复杂；B 正确，贫困地区常最后得到投资，涉及环境公正；C 错误，适应策略增加绿地；D 错误，需要政治勇气。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author suggest about the future strategy?", "options": ["A hybrid of protection, temporary flooding, and fair relocation.", "Complete retreat from all coastal areas immediately.", "Relying solely on emission cuts.", "Ignoring flooding until disasters occur."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，保护关键设施、允许部分地区暂时淹水、公平搬迁最脆弱社区；B 错误，非立即全部撤离；C 错误，减排 alone 不够；D 错误，作者主张尽早讨论。"}]}, {"title": "Why We Remember Stories Better Than Facts", "text": "Human memory is notoriously selective: people forget lists, statistics, and instructions with remarkable speed, yet they can recall stories heard years ago in vivid detail. This asymmetry has long puzzled psychologists, but recent research offers a compelling explanation rooted in evolution. For most of human history, knowledge was transmitted orally through narratives that encoded survival information: which plants were poisonous, where water could be found, and how ancestors navigated danger. Stories, unlike isolated facts, provide causal structure, emotional engagement, and social context, three features that make information easier to encode and retrieve. A fact presented alone has few mental hooks; embedded in a narrative, it becomes part of a sequence in which each event makes the next one plausible. Emotion further strengthens this effect. When listeners feel suspense, empathy, or surprise, the brain releases neurochemicals that enhance consolidation, effectively marking the experience as important. Modern educators have begun to exploit this principle, using case studies and personal anecdotes to teach abstract subjects such as economics and medicine. However, the power of narrative also carries risks. A compelling story can make weak evidence seem convincing, leading audiences to overweight a single vivid example while ignoring broader statistical trends. Political campaigns, advertisements, and misinformation often rely on precisely this vulnerability, crafting emotionally resonant tales that bypass critical scrutiny. The challenge, therefore, is not to abandon stories but to use them wisely: to recognize when a narrative illuminates a truth and when it merely disguises a generalization. Teaching students to ask for the data behind the story, researchers argue, may be one of the most important literacy skills of the digital age, where narratives circulate faster than verification ever can. Journalists and teachers can use this insight responsibly by pairing vivid examples with representative data, so that audiences feel the human stakes without losing sight of the broader pattern. In the end, a good story should open a door to the evidence, not close it, inviting listeners to care enough to check what is true.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why stories are remembered better than isolated facts and the risks this creates.", "Why human memory is completely unreliable.", "How to write political campaign advertisements.", "Why statistics are more persuasive than stories."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，文章解释故事易记的进化与心理原因及被利用的风险；B 错误，记忆有选择性但非完全不可靠；C 错误，竞选只是例子；D 错误，与文章结论相反。"}, {"prompt": "According to the passage, why did stories aid survival?", "options": ["They were entertaining during long winters.", "They encoded practical knowledge in causal, emotional form.", "They replaced the need for language.", "They were written down early in history."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，娱乐非主要原因；B 正确，故事编码了有毒植物、水源等生存信息；C 错误，故事依赖语言；D 错误，早期是口头传播。"}, {"prompt": "How does emotion help memory?", "options": ["It distracts the brain from facts.", "It triggers chemicals that strengthen consolidation.", "It makes people forget unpleasant events.", "It reduces the need for repetition."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，情绪是强化而非干扰；B 正确，悬念、共情等促使神经化学物质增强记忆巩固；C 错误，未提遗忘；D 错误，未提减少重复。"}, {"prompt": "What danger of narrative does the author warn about?", "options": ["Stories are always false.", "A vivid story can make weak evidence seem convincing.", "Stories cannot teach abstract subjects.", "People no longer enjoy stories."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，故事不总虚假；B 正确，生动个例会让人忽视统计趋势；C 错误，教育正利用故事教抽象学科；D 错误，未提人们不喜欢故事。"}, {"prompt": "What skill does the author recommend?", "options": ["Ignoring all stories.", "Asking for the data behind a story.", "Memorizing more statistics.", "Avoiding emotional responses."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者说不应抛弃故事；B 正确，教学生追问故事背后的数据是重要素养；C 错误，非单纯背统计；D 错误，未提避免情绪。"}]}]}, {"no": 3, "passages": [{"title": "The Paradox of Choice in Higher Education", "text": "Today's university students face a paradox that previous generations could scarcely imagine: an abundance of choice that was supposed to liberate them often leaves them anxious and indecisive. With hundreds of majors, thousands of online courses, and endless career paths advertised as equally promising, the act of choosing itself becomes a source of stress. Psychologists call this phenomenon choice overload, a condition in which additional options, beyond a certain point, reduce satisfaction rather than increase it. When students select one major, they become acutely aware of all the alternatives they have rejected, and this awareness can breed regret even when the chosen path is objectively sound. Social media intensifies the problem by constantly displaying peers who appear to have made better decisions, secured more prestigious internships, or discovered more exciting fields. The result is a pervasive fear of missing out, which encourages frequent switching of courses and majors, delaying the deep engagement that genuine expertise requires. Some educators argue that universities should therefore limit options in the first year, requiring a common core curriculum that allows students to sample disciplines without committing prematurely. Others contend that restriction merely postpones the dilemma and that the real solution lies in teaching decision-making skills: helping students clarify values, tolerate uncertainty, and understand that most career paths are not linear. Research supports the latter view to some extent, showing that students who reflect on their long-term goals before choosing report higher satisfaction regardless of the specific major selected. Yet reflection alone cannot eliminate structural pressures, such as family expectations and labor market volatility, which make any choice feel high-stakes. Ultimately, the paradox suggests that freedom without guidance is not genuine freedom. Universities that combine breadth of opportunity with structured mentoring may best prepare students not merely to choose, but to live with their choices.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students today have too few choices in university.", "Abundant choice can cause anxiety and regret, so guidance matters.", "Online courses have ruined higher education.", "Students should never change their majors."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章说选择过多；B 正确，概括选择过载、后悔与指导的必要性；C 错误，网课只是背景之一；D 错误，文章未禁止换专业。"}, {"prompt": "What does “choice overload” mean in the passage?", "options": ["Having no options at all.", "Too many options reducing satisfaction.", "Choosing too quickly.", "Being forced to choose by parents."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，过载是过多而非没有；B 正确，超过一定程度选项越多满意度越低；C 错误，未提速度；D 错误，家庭期望是另一压力。"}, {"prompt": "How does social media worsen the problem?", "options": ["By hiding information about careers.", "By showing peers who seem to have made better choices.", "By banning discussion of majors.", "By making courses more expensive."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，社交媒体展示而非隐藏；B 正确，同龄人看似选择更好，加剧错失恐惧；C 错误，未提禁止讨论；D 错误，未提费用。"}, {"prompt": "What solution do some educators propose?", "options": ["A common core curriculum in the first year.", "Eliminating all majors.", "Requiring students to choose in high school.", "Closing online courses."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，部分教育者主张第一年设共同核心课程；B 错误，未提取消专业；C 错误，未提高中选择；D 错误，未提关闭网课。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's conclusion?", "options": ["Freedom without guidance is not genuine freedom.", "Students should avoid reflection.", "Universities should offer unlimited majors.", "Family expectations are irrelevant."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，结尾指出没有指导的自由不是真正的自由；B 错误，研究支持反思；C 错误，作者倾向结构化指导；D 错误，家庭期望是结构性压力。"}]}, {"title": "The Economics of Attention", "text": "In the modern economy, attention has become one of the scarcest and most valuable resources, traded, measured, and fought over with an intensity once reserved for oil or gold. Technology companies design products not merely to be useful but to capture and retain human focus for as long as possible, because every additional minute of attention can be converted into advertising revenue or data. This transformation has profound implications for how societies allocate a resource that is, by definition, finite: each person has only twenty-four hours a day, and attention devoted to one activity is necessarily withdrawn from another. Economists describe this as an opportunity cost that is rarely made explicit. When a teenager spends four hours scrolling through short videos, the cost is not only the time itself but also the homework undone, the sleep lost, and the conversations not held. Yet markets, left to themselves, have little incentive to reveal these hidden costs, since the firms profiting from attention are not the ones who bear them. Some policymakers have proposed interventions, from limiting persuasive design features aimed at children to requiring platforms to disclose how much time users spend and how algorithms prioritize content. Critics of regulation argue that individuals should exercise self-control and that government interference would stifle innovation. Supporters counter that the playing field is not level: companies employ teams of psychologists and data scientists to exploit cognitive biases, while ordinary users, especially adolescents, lack comparable defenses. The debate echoes earlier disputes over tobacco and sugary drinks, where industries initially insisted on personal responsibility before evidence of systemic harm prompted regulation. Whether attention deserves similar treatment remains contested, but few deny that the stakes are high. A society that cannot protect its collective capacity to focus, critics warn, may find it increasingly difficult to sustain democratic deliberation, deep learning, and even meaningful personal relationships.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the passage mainly concerned with?", "options": ["How attention became a valuable economic resource and its hidden costs.", "How to make advertising more profitable.", "Why teenagers should not use phones.", "The history of oil and gold markets."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，文章论述注意力成为稀缺资源、机会成本与监管争议；B 错误，盈利只是背景；C 错误，青少年只是例子；D 错误，石油黄金只是类比。"}, {"prompt": "What is meant by “opportunity cost” here?", "options": ["The money paid for advertisements.", "What is lost when attention is given to one activity instead of another.", "The price of a smartphone.", "The cost of building apps."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，非广告费；B 正确，注意力用于一事就意味着放弃作业、睡眠、交谈等；C 错误，非手机价格；D 错误，非开发成本。"}, {"prompt": "Why do markets fail to reveal hidden costs?", "options": ["Because users do not care about time.", "Because firms profiting from attention do not bear those costs.", "Because attention is unlimited.", "Because economists refuse to study it."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，未提用户不在乎；B 正确，获利企业不承担隐性成本；C 错误，注意力有限；D 错误，经济学家正在研究。"}, {"prompt": "What argument do supporters of regulation make?", "options": ["Users and companies have equal power.", "Companies exploit cognitive biases while users lack defenses.", "Regulation will increase innovation.", "Children should design apps themselves."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，支持者认为力量不对等；B 正确，公司雇心理学家利用认知偏差，用户缺乏防御；C 错误，批评者才谈创新受抑；D 错误，未提儿童设计应用。"}, {"prompt": "What comparison does the author draw?", "options": ["Attention regulation resembles earlier debates over tobacco and sugary drinks.", "Attention is exactly the same as oil.", "Democracy has nothing to do with attention.", "Advertising revenue is declining."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，作者类比烟草和含糖饮料的监管争议；B 错误，只是类比珍贵程度；C 错误，作者认为注意力影响民主 deliberation；D 错误，未提收入下降。"}]}]}, {"no": 4, "passages": [{"title": "The Rise of Remote Work and Its Discontents", "text": "Remote work, once a rare privilege, became a widespread experiment during the pandemic and has since evolved into a permanent feature of many industries. Its advocates celebrate the elimination of commuting, greater autonomy over schedules, and access to talent regardless of geography. Employees report higher job satisfaction when they can design their workdays around family responsibilities and personal rhythms, while employers benefit from reduced office costs and a broader recruitment pool. Yet beneath this optimistic narrative lies a more complicated reality. Many remote workers describe feelings of isolation, blurred boundaries between work and home, and difficulty disconnecting at the end of the day. Without spontaneous conversations in hallways or shared lunches, informal learning and mentorship suffer, particularly for younger employees who learn as much from observation as from formal training. Managers, meanwhile, struggle to evaluate performance fairly, sometimes resorting to surveillance software that monitors keystrokes and screen activity, a practice that erodes trust and morale. The debate also has distributional consequences: remote work is largely available to well-paid knowledge workers, while service, manufacturing, and healthcare employees must still appear in person, deepening a divide between those who enjoy flexibility and those who do not. Cities face their own dilemmas as downtown offices empty, threatening local businesses that depended on commuter traffic. Some companies have responded with hybrid models, requiring office attendance two or three days a week in an attempt to capture the benefits of both worlds. Whether hybrid arrangements resolve these tensions or merely create new ones, such as resentment over unequal schedules, remains uncertain. What is clear is that remote work is not simply a logistical change; it is a renegotiation of how work, identity, and community intersect in modern life. Policymakers may need to update labor protections for this new reality, ensuring that flexibility does not become a justification for insecurity. If remote and hybrid work are to fulfill their promise, they must be paired with fair pay, clear boundaries, and genuine opportunities for connection and advancement.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Remote work is entirely beneficial for everyone.", "Remote work brings benefits but also creates isolation, inequality, and management problems.", "All companies should return to full-time office work.", "Remote work has no effect on cities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章列举诸多问题；B 正确，概括灵活性的好处与孤立、不平等、管理难题；C 错误，作者未主张全面回办公室；D 错误，市中心商业受影响。"}, {"prompt": "Why do younger employees suffer particularly in remote work?", "options": ["They prefer working alone.", "They learn much from observation and informal mentorship.", "They are less skilled with technology.", "They dislike flexible schedules."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，未提偏好独处；B 正确，年轻人多靠观察和非正式指导学习；C 错误，与技术能力无关；D 错误，他们受益于灵活。"}, {"prompt": "What problem does surveillance software create?", "options": ["It improves trust between managers and staff.", "It erodes trust and morale.", "It reduces office costs.", "It helps employees disconnect."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，监控侵蚀信任；B 正确，监控击键和屏幕会损害信任与士气；C 错误，降成本是远程办公本身；D 错误，监控无助于下班断开。"}, {"prompt": "What distributional issue does the passage mention?", "options": ["Remote work is available mainly to well-paid knowledge workers.", "All workers can work remotely equally.", "Service workers earn more than office workers.", "Remote work eliminates income gaps."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，远程多属高薪知识工作者，加深与必须到岗者的鸿沟；B 错误，与事实相反；C 错误，未提收入比较；D 错误，文章说加深分化。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's view of hybrid models?", "options": ["They definitely solve all problems.", "Their effectiveness remains uncertain.", "They are worse than full remote work.", "They have been abandoned by all companies."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者说是否解决紧张仍不确定；B 正确，可能产生新的不满；C 错误，未作此比较；D 错误，许多公司正采用混合模式。"}]}, {"title": "Nudges: Gentle Pushes or Hidden Manipulation?", "text": "Governments and companies increasingly use nudges, subtle changes in how choices are presented, to influence behavior without restricting freedom. Placing healthy foods at eye level, making organ donation the default option, or sending reminders to save for retirement are classic examples. The appeal is obvious: nudges are inexpensive, preserve individual autonomy in theory, and can produce significant improvements in public health and financial security. Yet the concept, popularized by behavioral economists, has attracted growing criticism. Skeptics argue that nudges exploit cognitive biases rather than respecting rational deliberation, steering people toward outcomes chosen by policymakers rather than by individuals themselves. When a default option is set, many people stick with it not because they have evaluated it carefully but because inertia and confusion make switching costly. This raises a democratic question: who decides what counts as a good choice, and how transparent should that decision be? Defenders respond that choice architecture is unavoidable; every menu, form, and website already frames decisions in some way, so the only question is whether the framing is designed thoughtfully or left to chance. They also point out that nudges can be disclosed and that people remain free to opt out. Critics remain unconvinced, noting that disclosure is often buried in fine print and that vulnerable populations, such as the elderly or those with limited literacy, are least able to resist subtle influence. The debate ultimately reflects deeper disagreements about human nature: whether people are best seen as rational agents who need only information, or as predictably irrational beings who benefit from guidance. As nudges expand from cafeterias to algorithms that shape news feeds and credit offers, the stakes grow. A nudge that encourages exercise differs profoundly from one that nudges consumers toward high-interest debt, even if both use the same psychological mechanisms. Transparency offers a partial remedy: when people know they are being nudged and understand why, they can reflect on whether the suggested choice matches their own goals. A nudge that survives such scrutiny is far more defensible than one that works only because its influence remains hidden from view.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is a nudge as described in the passage?", "options": ["A law that bans unhealthy behavior.", "A subtle change in choice presentation that influences behavior without forbidding options.", "A large financial penalty.", "A medical treatment."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，助推不禁止选择；B 正确，改变呈现方式引导行为；C 错误，非罚款；D 错误，非医疗。"}, {"prompt": "Why do critics object to nudges?", "options": ["They are too expensive.", "They exploit cognitive biases and steer people toward policymakers' preferred outcomes.", "They have no effect on behavior.", "They require people to read long documents."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，助推成本低；B 正确，批评者认为其利用认知偏差；C 错误，助推确实有效；D 错误，未提长文档。"}, {"prompt": "What do defenders argue about choice architecture?", "options": ["It can be completely avoided.", "It is unavoidable because every menu or form frames decisions.", "It should never be designed thoughtfully.", "It only affects the elderly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，辩护者说无法避免；B 正确，任何菜单、表格都在框定选择；C 错误，他们主张精心设计；D 错误，影响所有人。"}, {"prompt": "Why are vulnerable groups mentioned?", "options": ["They benefit most from nudges.", "They are least able to resist subtle influence.", "They design most nudges.", "They never encounter defaults."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章说他们最难抵制；B 正确，老年人和低 literacy 者最难抵抗微妙影响；C 错误，非设计者；D 错误，他们也会遇到默认选项。"}, {"prompt": "What broader issue does the debate reflect?", "options": ["Whether humans are rational agents or predictably irrational beings.", "Whether economics is a science.", "Whether governments should exist.", "Whether advertising should be banned."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，争论反映对人性的不同看法；B 错误，非经济学科学性；C 错误，非政府存废；D 错误，非广告禁令。"}]}]}, {"no": 5, "passages": [{"title": "The Future of Food: Lab-Grown Meat", "text": "Cultivated meat, grown from animal cells in bioreactors rather than raised on farms, is often presented as a solution to some of the most pressing problems of modern agriculture. Conventional livestock production consumes vast amounts of land and water, generates significant greenhouse gas emissions, and raises persistent concerns about animal welfare. By producing meat directly from cells, proponents argue, society could dramatically reduce its environmental footprint while still satisfying the global demand for protein. Early tastings suggest that cultivated chicken and beef can closely resemble their conventional counterparts in flavor and texture, although production costs remain high and scaling up poses formidable engineering challenges. Despite the technological optimism, public acceptance remains uncertain. Surveys reveal that many consumers are intrigued by the environmental benefits but uneasy about eating something grown in a laboratory, describing it as unnatural or artificial. This reaction, sometimes called the yuck factor, is not merely irrational; it reflects deep-seated cultural associations between food, nature, and authenticity. Regulators face the additional task of deciding how to label such products and what safety standards should apply, questions that have already sparked lobbying battles between traditional meat producers and cultivated meat startups. Economists also caution that cultivated meat may not automatically benefit small farmers or developing countries. If production becomes concentrated in a few high-tech firms, the gains could flow primarily to investors rather than to rural communities. Nevertheless, supporters maintain that the technology deserves serious investment because the alternative, continuing to expand conventional livestock production to meet rising demand, is ecologically unsustainable. The ultimate success of cultivated meat, therefore, will depend not only on scientific breakthroughs but also on transparent regulation, honest communication, and a food system willing to share its benefits broadly. Consumer education will matter as well, because people are more willing to try unfamiliar foods when they understand how they are made and what benefits they offer. If producers are honest about costs, limitations, and environmental trade-offs, cultivated meat may earn the trust it needs to move from laboratory curiosity to everyday option.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main argument of the passage?", "options": ["Lab-grown meat will definitely replace all farming soon.", "Cultivated meat offers environmental promise but faces technical, cultural, and economic hurdles.", "Consumers already fully accept cultivated meat.", "Traditional meat production has no environmental impact."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，文章说成本和规模化仍是挑战；B 正确，概括环境前景与技术、文化、经济障碍；C 错误，消费者仍有疑虑；D 错误，传统畜牧业耗地耗水排温室气体。"}, {"prompt": "What is the “yuck factor”?", "options": ["A scientific measure of taste.", "Consumers' unease about eating lab-grown food as unnatural.", "A type of food poisoning.", "A marketing slogan."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，非科学度量；B 正确，消费者觉得实验室培育的食物不自然；C 错误，非食物中毒；D 错误，非营销口号。"}, {"prompt": "What challenge do regulators face?", "options": ["Deciding labeling and safety standards amid lobbying.", "Banning all new foods.", "Setting the price of beef.", "Teaching farmers to use bioreactors."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，监管需决定标签与安全标准，已引发游说之争；B 错误，未提禁令；C 错误，未提定价；D 错误，未提培训农民。"}, {"prompt": "What economic concern is raised?", "options": ["Cultivated meat will make all food free.", "Production may concentrate in a few firms, benefiting investors over rural communities.", "Small farmers will automatically become rich.", "Developing countries need no protein."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，未提免费；B 正确，若生产集中于少数高科技公司，收益流向投资者；C 错误，文章警告未必惠及小农；D 错误，未提不需要蛋白质。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author conclude?", "options": ["Success depends only on science.", "Success depends on science, regulation, communication, and fair benefit-sharing.", "Cultivated meat should be abandoned.", "Conventional farming can expand indefinitely."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者强调多因素；B 正确，成功取决于科学突破、透明监管、诚实沟通与利益共享；C 错误，作者支持认真投资；D 错误，继续扩张生态上不可持续。"}]}, {"title": "Why Lifelong Learning Is No Longer Optional", "text": "The idea that education ends with graduation is rapidly becoming obsolete. Technological change, shifting labor markets, and longer working lives mean that skills acquired in one's twenties may be outdated within a decade. Workers who once expected a single career now anticipate several transitions, each requiring new knowledge and capabilities. In this context, lifelong learning is no longer a luxury for the ambitious but a necessity for remaining employable. Governments have responded with initiatives such as subsidized online courses, tax incentives for training, and recognition of informal learning. Employers, too, increasingly offer upskilling programs, recognizing that retraining existing staff is often cheaper than hiring new ones. Yet significant barriers remain. Time is the most obvious constraint: adults juggling jobs, childcare, and household responsibilities often find it difficult to commit to regular study. Motivation poses another challenge, as learning after a long break can feel daunting, particularly for those whose earlier educational experiences were negative. Financial costs, even when subsidized, can deter low-income workers who cannot afford to reduce their hours. Moreover, the quality of available courses varies widely, and learners may struggle to identify which credentials employers actually value. Psychologists suggest that the most effective approach is not to treat learning as an occasional, intensive effort but to integrate small, regular habits into daily life: reading industry news, practicing a skill for twenty minutes, or discussing new ideas with colleagues. Such micro-learning, they argue, builds momentum and reduces the psychological barrier to starting. Ultimately, societies that make continuous learning accessible, affordable, and socially valued will be better equipped to adapt to change. Those that treat education as a one-time event risk leaving large segments of the population behind as the economy transforms. Employers can support this shift by recognizing informal learning, offering paid time for training, and rewarding employees who mentor others. When learning becomes part of ordinary working life rather than an extra burden, workers are far more likely to keep growing throughout their careers.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the central claim of the passage?", "options": ["Education should end at graduation.", "Lifelong learning is now necessary to stay employable.", "Only young people need to learn new skills.", "Online courses are useless."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者说毕业即结束的观念已过时；B 正确，终身学习是保持就业能力的必需；C 错误，成年人同样需要；D 错误，政府正补贴网课。"}, {"prompt": "Why is retraining existing staff attractive to employers?", "options": ["It is often cheaper than hiring new workers.", "It requires no time.", "It guarantees promotion.", "It eliminates all training costs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，再培训现有员工常比招聘新人便宜；B 错误，仍需时间；C 错误，未提保证晋升；D 错误，未提消除成本。"}, {"prompt": "What barrier is described as most obvious?", "options": ["Lack of internet access.", "Time constraints from jobs and family.", "Poor eyesight.", "Lack of interest in any subject."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，未提网络；B 正确，时间是最明显限制，成年人兼顾工作与育儿；C 错误，未提视力；D 错误，动机是另一障碍。"}, {"prompt": "What does “micro-learning” involve?", "options": ["Studying intensively once a year.", "Small, regular habits integrated into daily life.", "Quitting a job to study full-time.", "Only learning from colleagues."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，非一年一次集中学习；B 正确，每天读行业新闻、练20分钟等小习惯；C 错误，未提辞职；D 错误，同事讨论只是方式之一。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward societies that ignore lifelong learning?", "options": ["They will adapt easily.", "They risk leaving many people behind.", "They will save money.", "They are more innovative."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者警告会落后；B 正确，把教育当一次性事件会使大批人掉队；C 错误，未提省钱；D 错误，与结论相反。"}]}]}, {"no": 6, "passages": [{"title": "The Psychology of Procrastination", "text": "Procrastination is often misunderstood as a simple failure of time management or laziness, but psychological research reveals a far more complex picture. At its core, procrastination is not about poor planning; it is about emotion regulation. People delay tasks not because they do not know how to do them, but because the tasks provoke unpleasant feelings such as boredom, anxiety, self-doubt, or frustration. By postponing the work, individuals obtain immediate relief, even though they know the delay will create greater stress later. This pattern, known as temporal discounting, reflects the brain's tendency to prioritize present comfort over future consequences. Chronic procrastinators are not necessarily less capable; many are perfectionists who fear that their work will not meet their own high standards, so avoiding the task temporarily protects their self-image. Others struggle with unclear goals or tasks that feel overwhelming, leading them to avoid starting altogether. The consequences extend beyond missed deadlines. Research links chronic procrastination to higher levels of stress, poorer physical health, and lower life satisfaction. Fortunately, interventions based on this emotional understanding are more effective than traditional advice to simply make a schedule. Strategies such as breaking tasks into tiny first steps, practicing self-compassion after a setback, and focusing on the next action rather than the final outcome help reduce the emotional barrier to beginning. Equally important is changing the environment: removing distractions, setting clear deadlines, and working alongside others can make starting easier. The key insight is that willpower alone is rarely sufficient. Lasting change comes from understanding why a task feels aversive and addressing that feeling directly, rather than blaming oneself for a supposed character flaw. Teachers and managers can help by making the first step as small and clear as possible, so that starting feels manageable rather than overwhelming. A single sentence written, a single page read, or a single email drafted can break the inertia that makes a task seem impossible.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Procrastination is caused by laziness.", "Procrastination is mainly an emotion-regulation problem, not poor time management.", "Perfectionists never procrastinate.", "Making a schedule always cures procrastination."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者否定懒惰解释；B 正确，核心是情绪调节；C 错误，完美主义者常拖延；D 错误，单纯做计划效果有限。"}, {"prompt": "What is “temporal discounting”?", "options": ["Planning tasks far in advance.", "Prioritizing present comfort over future consequences.", "Discounting prices over time.", "Forgetting deadlines."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，非提前规划；B 正确，大脑倾向当下舒适而非未来后果；C 错误，非价格折扣；D 错误，非遗忘。"}, {"prompt": "Why might perfectionists procrastinate?", "options": ["They enjoy missing deadlines.", "They fear their work will not meet high standards.", "They have no standards.", "They are not capable."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，非享受错过期限；B 正确，害怕达不到高标准，回避可暂时保护自我形象；C 错误，他们标准很高；D 错误，他们未必能力差。"}, {"prompt": "What strategy does the author recommend?", "options": ["Blaming oneself for a character flaw.", "Breaking tasks into tiny first steps and practicing self-compassion.", "Relying only on willpower.", "Avoiding all difficult tasks."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者反对归咎性格缺陷；B 正确，拆成小步骤、自我关怀、关注下一步；C 错误，仅靠意志力很少足够；D 错误，未提回避困难任务。"}, {"prompt": "What can be inferred about the author's view of willpower?", "options": ["Willpower alone is rarely enough.", "Willpower is the only solution.", "Willpower is irrelevant.", "Willpower always works."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，作者明确说仅靠意志力很少足够；B 错误，与观点相反；C 错误，意志力并非无关；D 错误，未说总有效。"}]}, {"title": "Greenwashing and Consumer Trust", "text": "As environmental awareness grows, companies face increasing pressure to present themselves as sustainable. Many respond with genuine changes: reducing emissions, switching to renewable energy, and redesigning packaging. Others, however, engage in greenwashing, the practice of making misleading claims about environmental benefits while changing little in substance. A product labeled natural or eco-friendly may contain only a small proportion of sustainable ingredients, while an airline advertising carbon offset programs may continue to expand its most polluting routes. Greenwashing is effective because consumers, overwhelmed by complex supply chains, often rely on simple signals such as green packaging or vague slogans. The consequences are serious. When misleading claims are exposed, public trust in all sustainability efforts declines, making it harder for genuinely responsible companies to distinguish themselves. Moreover, greenwashing can delay real action by creating the illusion of progress, allowing policymakers and consumers to feel that the problem is being addressed when it is not. Regulators in several countries have begun to respond with stricter rules on environmental advertising, requiring specific evidence for claims and imposing fines for deception. Yet enforcement remains difficult, partly because sustainability is inherently complex: a product may be better in one dimension, such as energy use, but worse in another, such as water consumption. Consumers, for their part, can protect themselves by looking for independent certifications, questioning vague language, and considering a company's overall record rather than a single advertisement. Ultimately, the fight against greenwashing is not merely about accurate labeling; it is about preserving the credibility of the entire sustainability movement. If the public concludes that green claims are mostly marketing, the motivation to support meaningful change may evaporate. Journalists and independent watchdogs also have a role, investigating claims, comparing companies over time, and celebrating firms that make verifiable progress. When honesty is rewarded and deception is exposed, the market gradually learns that genuine sustainability is worth more than a persuasive slogan.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is greenwashing?", "options": ["Genuinely reducing emissions.", "Making misleading environmental claims while changing little.", "Using green packaging for all products.", "Certifying products independently."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，那是真正的改变；B 正确，误导性宣称环保益处而实质少变；C 错误，绿包装只是手段之一；D 错误，独立认证是防范手段。"}, {"prompt": "Why is greenwashing effective?", "options": ["Consumers carefully study supply chains.", "Consumers rely on simple signals like green packaging.", "All claims are verified by regulators.", "Products are always sustainable."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，消费者被复杂供应链压倒；B 正确，依赖简单信号；C 错误，监管核查有限；D 错误，并非都可持续。"}, {"prompt": "What is a serious consequence of greenwashing?", "options": ["It increases trust in all companies.", "It creates an illusion of progress and delays real action.", "It reduces all emissions.", "It makes certification unnecessary."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，曝光后信任下降；B 正确，制造进步幻觉、延误真正行动；C 错误，未减少排放；D 错误，认证反而重要。"}, {"prompt": "Why is enforcement difficult?", "options": ["Sustainability is simple to measure.", "A product may be better in one aspect but worse in another.", "Regulators have unlimited resources.", "Companies never advertise."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，可持续性本身复杂；B 正确，某维度好、另一维度差；C 错误，未提无限资源；D 错误，公司常做广告。"}, {"prompt": "What advice does the author give consumers?", "options": ["Trust all green slogans.", "Look for independent certifications and question vague language.", "Ignore a company's overall record.", "Only buy products with green packaging."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，作者警告误导宣称；B 正确，寻找独立认证、质疑含糊语言、看整体记录；C 错误，应考虑整体记录；D 错误，绿包装正是误导信号。"}]}]}, {"no": 7, "passages": [{"title": "The Gig Economy: Freedom or Precarity?", "text": "The gig economy promises workers unprecedented freedom: choose your hours, be your own boss, and work from anywhere. For millions of drivers, couriers, designers, and tutors, platform work offers flexibility that traditional employment rarely provides, allowing students to earn between classes and parents to work around childcare. Yet behind the rhetoric of independence lies a growing debate about precarity. Gig workers typically lack minimum wage guarantees, paid sick leave, health insurance, and protection against unfair dismissal. Their income can fluctuate dramatically from week to week, depending on algorithms that assign tasks opaquely and customer ratings that may reflect bias rather than performance. Supporters of the model argue that flexibility is precisely what workers want and that imposing traditional employment rules would destroy the opportunities platforms create. They point to surveys in which many gig workers say they value autonomy over security. Critics counter that such preferences are shaped by constrained choices: when stable jobs are scarce, people may accept insecure work not because they prefer it but because they have few alternatives. The legal classification of gig workers has become a central battleground, with courts and legislatures in different countries reaching opposite conclusions. Some jurisdictions have created a third category, granting limited benefits without full employee status, while others insist platforms must treat workers as employees. Economists note that the outcome will affect not only gig workers but also the broader labor market, as platform models spread to healthcare, education, and professional services. Ultimately, the gig economy forces societies to reconsider what security should mean in an era when the traditional link between a job and a safety net is weakening, and whether flexibility can be preserved without abandoning protection. Some cities are already experimenting with portable benefits that follow workers from one platform to another, decoupling protection from a single employer. Such innovations suggest that flexibility and security need not be opposites, provided that societies are willing to redesign safety nets for a more fragmented labor market.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main debate about the gig economy?", "options": ["Whether platforms should use algorithms at all.", "Whether students should work part-time.", "Whether flexibility is worth the loss of security and benefits.", "Whether couriers should drive cars."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，算法只是背景而非争论核心；B 错误，学生兼职只是例子；C 正确，核心是灵活性与保障缺失的权衡；D 错误，与主题无关。"}, {"prompt": "What risk do customer ratings pose?", "options": ["They always reflect true performance.", "They increase wages automatically.", "They are never used by platforms.", "They may reflect bias rather than performance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，评分未必真实；B 错误，评分不自动涨薪；C 错误，平台确实使用评分；D 正确，评分可能反映偏见而非表现。"}, {"prompt": "What do supporters claim gig workers value?", "options": ["Autonomy over security.", "Full employee benefits.", "Fixed working hours.", "Union membership above all."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，支持者称许多零工工人更看重自主而非保障；B 错误，支持者反对强制雇员待遇；C 错误，固定工时与灵活相反；D 错误，未提工会优先。"}, {"prompt": "What is the “third category” mentioned?", "options": ["A ban on all platform work.", "A new type of algorithm.", "A status granting limited benefits without full employee status.", "A tax exemption for drivers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，非禁令；B 错误，非算法；C 正确，第三类给予有限福利但非完整雇员身份；D 错误，非税收减免。"}, {"prompt": "What broader question does the passage raise?", "options": ["Whether all jobs should become gig work.", "Whether parents should not work.", "Whether algorithms should be secret forever.", "Whether flexibility can be preserved without abandoning protection."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未主张全部零工化；B 错误，未提父母不应工作；C 错误，未提算法永远保密；D 正确，结尾提出能否保留灵活又不放弃保障。"}]}, {"title": "Sleep: The Neglected Pillar of Health", "text": "In discussions of healthy living, diet and exercise receive most of the attention, while sleep is often treated as a luxury that can be sacrificed for productivity. Yet a growing body of research suggests that sleep is not merely rest but an active biological process essential for memory, emotional regulation, immune function, and metabolic health. During deep sleep, the brain consolidates memories, clears metabolic waste, and recalibrates emotional responses, processes that cannot be fully compensated for by caffeine or willpower. Chronic sleep deprivation, defined as regularly sleeping fewer than seven hours, has been linked to increased risks of obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and depression. It also impairs attention, creativity, and decision-making, effects that are particularly dangerous in professions such as medicine, transport, and aviation. Despite this evidence, modern culture often celebrates sleeplessness as a sign of dedication, with entrepreneurs boasting about four-hour nights and students pulling all-nighters before exams. Some researchers argue that this attitude reflects a fundamental misunderstanding: sleep is not the opposite of productivity but its foundation. Schools and workplaces are beginning to respond, experimenting with later start times for teenagers, whose biological clocks naturally shift later, and with nap rooms for shift workers. Critics caution that individual behavior change is insufficient when economic structures reward long hours and constant availability. Nevertheless, experts agree on practical steps: maintaining a regular sleep schedule, limiting screens before bed, and treating seven to nine hours as non-negotiable. In a society obsessed with doing more, the most radical act, some suggest, may be to sleep enough. Employers who respect sleep, by limiting late-night messages and discouraging a culture of constant availability, may find that well-rested workers are more creative and make fewer costly mistakes. In this sense, protecting sleep is not a concession to comfort but an investment in the quality of work itself.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why diet is more important than sleep.", "How to cure insomnia with medicine.", "Why sleep is essential for health and productivity, yet undervalued.", "Why teenagers should wake up earlier."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，作者认为睡眠被忽视而非饮食更重要；B 错误，未提药物治失眠；C 正确，主旨是睡眠对健康与效率至关重要却被低估；D 错误，作者主张青少年晚点上学。"}, {"prompt": "What happens during deep sleep?", "options": ["The body stops all activity.", "People dream only about work.", "Memory is permanently erased.", "The brain consolidates memories and clears waste."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，深睡是大脑活跃过程；B 错误，未提只梦工作；C 错误，记忆是巩固而非抹除；D 正确，深睡时大脑巩固记忆、清除代谢废物。"}, {"prompt": "What is a consequence of chronic sleep deprivation?", "options": ["Increased risk of obesity and diabetes.", "Improved creativity.", "Better decision-making.", "Stronger immunity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，长期睡眠不足增加肥胖、糖尿病等风险；B 错误，创造力受损；C 错误，决策受损；D 错误，免疫受损。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some people celebrate sleeplessness?", "options": ["Because it is medically recommended.", "Because it improves grades.", "Because doctors advise it.", "Because it is seen as a sign of dedication."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，非医学建议；B 错误，通宵未必提高成绩；C 错误，医生不建议；D 正确，文化把少睡视为敬业的标志。"}, {"prompt": "What practical advice is given?", "options": ["Sleeping only four hours a night.", "Using caffeine instead of sleep.", "Keeping a regular schedule and treating 7–9 hours as non-negotiable.", "Working longer hours."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，四小时睡眠被批判；B 错误，咖啡因不能补偿睡眠；C 正确，建议规律作息、把7至9小时视为不可妥协；D 错误，长时间工作正是问题所在。"}]}]}, {"no": 8, "passages": [{"title": "Cultural Heritage in the Digital Age", "text": "Digital technology has transformed how cultural heritage is preserved, accessed, and understood. Museums now offer virtual tours, ancient manuscripts are scanned in high resolution, and endangered languages are recorded by community members with smartphones. For people who cannot travel to distant sites, these tools provide unprecedented access to art, history, and tradition. A student in a rural village can examine a masterpiece in detail, while diaspora communities can reconnect with rituals and songs that migration had interrupted. Yet digitization also raises profound questions about authenticity, ownership, and meaning. A photograph of a temple, however detailed, cannot convey the smell of incense, the sound of chanting, or the social experience of visiting with family. Critics worry that digital copies may create a false sense of preservation, leading societies to neglect the physical sites and living practices that give heritage its vitality. Who owns a digital scan of a sacred object, and who profits when it is licensed for commercial use? Indigenous communities have increasingly demanded control over how their cultural materials are digitized and shared, arguing that open access without consent can amount to a new form of appropriation. Technology companies, meanwhile, often frame digitization as a neutral act of preservation, overlooking the power relations involved. Scholars suggest that the most promising approach treats digital tools not as replacements for physical heritage but as complements that support living traditions: recording elders, training young practitioners, and funding the maintenance of sites. In this view, the goal is not merely to store the past but to keep it alive in the present, ensuring that future generations inherit not only images but also the knowledge and practices that make those images meaningful. Funding should therefore support not only scanning and storage but also the people who carry traditions forward, from master craftspeople to young apprentices. A digital archive is most valuable when it is linked to living practice, helping the next generation learn skills that no photograph alone can teach.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What benefit of digitization is mentioned?", "options": ["It replaces all physical museums.", "It makes heritage less interesting.", "It gives distant people unprecedented access to heritage.", "It eliminates the need for travel forever."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，作者主张数字工具是补充而非替代；B 错误，数字化使遗产更可及；C 正确，无法亲临的人获得前所未有的接触机会；D 错误，未说永远无需旅行。"}, {"prompt": "What limitation of digital copies is discussed?", "options": ["They are too expensive to make.", "They are always inaccurate.", "They damage original objects.", "They cannot convey smell, sound, or social experience."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未提太贵；B 错误，未说总不准确；C 错误，未提损坏原件；D 正确，再详细的照片也无法传达气味、声音与社会体验。"}, {"prompt": "What concern do Indigenous communities raise?", "options": ["They demand control over digitization and sharing of their materials.", "They want all heritage kept secret.", "They oppose all technology.", "They want to sell all sacred objects."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，原住民要求控制其文化材料的数字化与分享；B 错误，未要求全部保密；C 错误，未反对一切技术；D 错误，未提出售圣物。"}, {"prompt": "What approach do scholars suggest?", "options": ["Abandoning digitization completely.", "Storing images without practices.", "Letting companies decide everything.", "Using digital tools as complements supporting living traditions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未主张放弃数字化；B 错误，目标是传承知识与实践；C 错误，不应由公司全权决定；D 正确，学者建议把数字工具作为支持活态传统的补充。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main point?", "options": ["Digital copies are superior to real sites.", "Heritage should not be shared.", "Digitization alone cannot preserve heritage; living practices matter.", "Technology has no role in culture."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，作者否定数字优于实地；B 错误，作者支持分享但要有同意与控制；C 正确，仅数字化不够，活态实践才是关键；D 错误，技术有补充作用。"}]}, {"title": "The Ethics of Gene Editing", "text": "The development of CRISPR and related gene-editing tools has given humanity an unprecedented ability to alter the genetic code of living organisms, including, potentially, future generations. In medicine, the promise is extraordinary: correcting mutations that cause sickle cell disease, cystic fibrosis, and certain forms of blindness could transform lives. In agriculture, edited crops might resist drought and disease, helping to feed a growing population under climate stress. Yet the same power raises ethical questions that societies have barely begun to answer. Editing somatic cells, which affects only the individual patient, is widely regarded as less controversial than editing germline cells, which would be inherited by descendants who cannot consent. The prospect of designer babies, selected for intelligence, appearance, or athletic ability, evokes fears of a new eugenics, in which wealth determines genetic advantage and inequality becomes biologically entrenched. International governance remains fragmented: some countries ban germline editing outright, others permit research under strict oversight, and still others have ambiguous rules that invite medical tourism. Scientists themselves are divided. Some argue for a temporary moratorium until safety and ethical frameworks mature, while others warn that overly restrictive bans could drive research underground, where it would proceed without transparency. Public engagement, many ethicists argue, is essential but difficult, because the science is complex and the implications unfold over generations. The debate is not merely technical; it forces societies to articulate what they value: health, equality, autonomy, and the meaning of being human. How those values are balanced will shape not only medicine but the future of the species. Public deliberation, including voices from patients, ethicists, and communities most affected, should therefore precede any decision about heritable editing. Science can tell us what is possible, but only democratic debate can decide what is permissible, and that conversation must include those who will live with its consequences.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What distinguishes germline editing from somatic editing?", "options": ["Somatic editing affects future generations.", "They are identical.", "Germline changes are inherited by descendants.", "Germline editing is only for plants."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，体细胞编辑只影响患者本人；B 错误，两者不同；C 正确，生殖系改变会由后代继承；D 错误，生殖系编辑涉及人类后代。"}, {"prompt": "What fear is associated with “designer babies”?", "options": ["That everyone will become identical.", "That gene editing will cure all diseases.", "That parents will lose interest in children.", "That wealth could determine genetic advantage and entrench inequality."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未提人人相同；B 错误，未说包治百病；C 错误，未提父母失去兴趣；D 正确，担忧财富决定基因优势、固化不平等。"}, {"prompt": "Why is international governance described as fragmented?", "options": ["Countries differ: some ban, some permit with oversight, some are ambiguous.", "All countries have the same law.", "No country cares about gene editing.", "Scientists all agree on rules."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，各国做法不同，有的禁、有的严管下允许、有的含糊；B 错误，各国法律不同；C 错误，各国都在关注；D 错误，科学家有分歧。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some scientists oppose strict bans?", "options": ["Bans make research cheaper.", "Bans guarantee safety.", "Bans increase public engagement.", "Bans could drive research underground without transparency."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，禁令不使研究更便宜；B 错误，禁令不保证安全；C 错误，禁令不增加公众参与；D 正确，过于严格的禁令可能把研究推向地下、失去透明。"}, {"prompt": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How CRISPR works technically.", "Why agriculture needs no innovation.", "The medical promise and ethical dilemmas of gene editing.", "How to ban all science."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章重伦理而非技术细节；B 错误，农业是应用之一；C 正确，主旨是基因编辑的医学前景与伦理困境；D 错误，未主张禁绝科学。"}]}]}, {"no": 9, "passages": [{"title": "Urban Loneliness in a Crowded World", "text": "Cities are often celebrated as places of connection, where millions of people live side by side and opportunities for interaction seem endless. Yet paradoxically, urban residents frequently report higher levels of loneliness than those in smaller communities. Researchers attribute this paradox to several factors. In dense neighborhoods, interactions tend to be brief, transactional, and anonymous: exchanging a few words with a barista or nodding to a neighbor in an elevator rarely develops into meaningful friendship. High mobility further undermines stability, as people move for jobs, education, or cheaper rent, leaving social networks repeatedly disrupted. Economic pressures also play a role. Long working hours, long commutes, and the high cost of housing leave little time or energy for community life, while the pursuit of career success can make vulnerability, the foundation of close relationships, feel like a weakness. Technology compounds the problem by offering superficial substitutes for presence: hundreds of online contacts can coexist with a profound sense of being unknown. Some urban planners argue that design can help. Mixed-use neighborhoods, public squares, community gardens, and third places such as libraries and cafes create opportunities for repeated, informal encounters that gradually build trust. Others emphasize policy: affordable housing near workplaces, shorter commutes, and support for community organizations can free time for social life. Psychologists add that individual skills matter too, including the willingness to initiate conversations, accept invitations, and tolerate the awkwardness of new friendships. Loneliness, they stress, is not a personal failing but a signal, much like hunger, indicating an unmet human need. Addressing it, therefore, requires both structural change and personal courage, and cities that ignore it may pay a price in mental health, productivity, and social cohesion. Neighborhood associations, volunteer groups, and local festivals can also create the repeated encounters from which friendships grow. A city becomes less lonely not when it adds more people, but when it gives those people reasons and places to meet again and again.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What paradox does the passage describe?", "options": ["Cities have few people but much loneliness.", "Rural areas are more crowded than cities.", "Crowded cities can produce high levels of loneliness.", "Loneliness does not exist in cities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，城市人口众多；B 错误，乡村不如城市拥挤；C 正确，拥挤的城市反而孤独感更高；D 错误，城市存在孤独。"}, {"prompt": "Why are urban interactions often insufficient?", "options": ["They are too long and deep.", "People never talk to strangers.", "Cities ban socializing.", "They tend to be brief, transactional, and anonymous."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，城市互动短暂而非深入；B 错误，人们会简短交流；C 错误，城市不禁止社交；D 正确，互动多为短暂、交易性、匿名。"}, {"prompt": "How does high mobility affect loneliness?", "options": ["It repeatedly disrupts social networks.", "It strengthens social networks.", "It reduces housing costs.", "It eliminates commuting."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，高流动性反复打断社交网络；B 错误，流动破坏而非强化；C 错误，未提降低房价；D 错误，未提消除通勤。"}, {"prompt": "What design solution is suggested?", "options": ["Building only private apartments.", "Closing libraries and cafes.", "Increasing commute times.", "Creating public squares and community gardens for repeated encounters."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，只建私人公寓无助于交往；B 错误，应开放图书馆咖啡馆；C 错误，应缩短通勤；D 正确，广场与社区花园创造反复非正式相遇。"}, {"prompt": "How do psychologists view loneliness?", "options": ["As a personal failing.", "As a rare medical disease.", "As a signal of an unmet human need, like hunger.", "As something to ignore."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，作者说不是个人失败；B 错误，未说罕见疾病；C 正确，孤独是未满足需求的信号，如同饥饿；D 错误，不应忽视。"}]}, {"title": "The Changing Meaning of Privacy", "text": "Privacy was once understood primarily as the right to be left alone, to keep one's home, letters, and conversations free from intrusion. In the digital age, that definition has become increasingly inadequate. Every search, purchase, location ping, and social media interaction generates data that can be collected, combined, and analyzed to infer preferences, habits, and even emotional states. Individuals may share information willingly in one context, such as posting a photo for friends, without anticipating that it will be used in another, such as employment screening or targeted advertising. Scholars call this phenomenon context collapse, the erosion of boundaries between different spheres of life. The challenge is compounded by the fact that privacy is not only individual but also collective: data about one person can reveal information about their family, friends, and community. Genetic data, for example, implicates relatives who never consented to testing. Despite growing awareness, many people experience a privacy paradox, expressing concern about surveillance while continuing to use services that collect extensive data, partly because alternatives are inconvenient or unavailable. Some argue that stronger laws, such as data minimization and the right to deletion, are necessary. Others contend that legal solutions alone are insufficient without changes in business models that profit from surveillance. A more radical proposal is to treat personal data as labor, compensating individuals for the value their information creates. Whatever the solution, the debate reveals that privacy is not a fixed concept but a evolving social value, negotiated anew as technology reshapes what it means to be seen, known, and remembered. Education about data literacy should therefore begin early, teaching young people to read privacy settings, question default options, and understand the long-term value of their information. An informed public is far better equipped to demand laws and designs that respect privacy than one that discovers its importance only after it is lost.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is “context collapse”?", "options": ["The loss of all data.", "A type of computer virus.", "The erosion of boundaries between different spheres of life.", "The closing of social media."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，非丢失所有数据；B 错误，非病毒；C 正确，语境崩塌指不同生活领域边界的侵蚀；D 错误，非关闭社交媒体。"}, {"prompt": "Why is privacy described as collective?", "options": ["Everyone shares the same password.", "Privacy laws apply only to groups.", "Individuals have no privacy.", "Data about one person can reveal information about others."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未提相同密码；B 错误，法律也保护个人；C 错误，个人仍有隐私；D 正确，一个人的数据可揭示家人朋友的信息。"}, {"prompt": "What is the “privacy paradox”?", "options": ["People worry about surveillance but keep using data-collecting services.", "People never worry about privacy.", "People always protect their data.", "People refuse all technology."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，担忧监控却继续使用收集数据的服务；B 错误，人们确实担忧；C 错误，人们常不保护数据；D 错误，未拒绝一切技术。"}, {"prompt": "What radical proposal is mentioned?", "options": ["Banning the internet.", "Giving all data to governments.", "Deleting all personal data forever.", "Treating personal data as labor and compensating individuals."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未提禁网；B 错误，未提交给政府；C 错误，未提永久删除；D 正确，把个人数据视为劳动并给予报酬。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's conclusion about privacy?", "options": ["Privacy is a fixed, unchanging right.", "Privacy no longer matters.", "Privacy is an evolving social value negotiated as technology changes.", "Privacy only concerns letters."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，作者说隐私非固定概念；B 错误，隐私仍重要；C 正确，隐私是随技术变化而不断协商的社会价值；D 错误，隐私已远超信件。"}]}]}, {"no": 10, "passages": [{"title": "Automation and the Future of Employment", "text": "The rapid advance of automation, driven by robotics and artificial intelligence, has revived an old anxiety: that machines will render human labor obsolete. Unlike earlier waves of mechanization, which primarily replaced physical strength, contemporary systems can perform cognitive tasks once thought uniquely human, from drafting legal documents and diagnosing diseases to composing music and writing computer code. Optimists argue that history offers reassurance. Previous technological revolutions destroyed particular jobs but created new ones that were difficult to foresee, raising overall productivity and living standards. The steam engine displaced weavers yet eventually supported railways, factories, and entirely new industries. Pessimists, however, contend that this time may be different, because the pace of change is faster and the range of tasks susceptible to automation is broader. A truck driver, a paralegal, and a radiologist may all face disruption within the same decade, leaving little time for retraining. Moreover, the new jobs created by automation often require advanced technical skills, while the jobs eliminated are frequently held by workers with fewer educational opportunities, raising the prospect of widening inequality. Some economists propose bold responses, including a universal basic income, shorter working weeks, and large-scale public investment in education and care work. Others favor more incremental measures, such as portable benefits, lifelong learning accounts, and taxes on automation that fund retraining. What most experts agree on is that the outcome is not technologically determined. Societies can choose policies that share the gains from automation broadly or allow them to concentrate among owners of capital. The challenge is political as much as economic: building institutions capable of managing transition fairly, rather than assuming that markets alone will distribute the benefits of machines that can increasingly do what humans once did. In the end, the question is not whether machines can work, but whether societies can adapt their institutions quickly enough to ensure that technological progress improves life for the many rather than only for the few who own the machines.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What distinguishes current automation from earlier mechanization?", "options": ["It only replaces physical strength.", "It is slower than before.", "It can perform cognitive tasks once thought uniquely human.", "It creates no new jobs at all."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，早期机械化主要替代体力；B 错误，当代变化更快；C 正确，AI 能起草法律文件、诊断疾病、写代码等认知任务；D 错误，自动化也会创造新岗位。"}, {"prompt": "What do optimists argue?", "options": ["Machines will destroy all jobs permanently.", "Automation should be banned.", "Productivity will definitely fall.", "Previous revolutions destroyed jobs but created unforeseen new ones."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，乐观者认为新工作会出现；B 错误，未主张禁令；C 错误，乐观者认为生产率会提高；D 正确，历史表明旧岗位消失的同时会出现难以预见的新岗位。"}, {"prompt": "Why might this wave be different, according to pessimists?", "options": ["Change is faster and more kinds of tasks are automatable.", "Machines are slower now.", "Workers have more education than ever.", "Governments already solved retraining."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，变化更快、可自动化的任务范围更广；B 错误，与事实相反；C 错误，被取代者常教育机会较少；D 错误，再培训仍是难题。"}, {"prompt": "What policy is mentioned as a bold response?", "options": ["Banning all robots.", "A universal basic income.", "Ending all education.", "Closing factories."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，未提禁机器人；B 正确，全民基本收入是经济学家提出的大胆回应之一；C 错误，未提终止教育；D 错误，未提关闭工厂。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main conclusion?", "options": ["Technology alone determines employment outcomes.", "Markets alone will distribute benefits fairly.", "Societies can choose policies to share automation gains fairly.", "Inequality is unavoidable and should be ignored."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，结果并非技术决定；B 错误，作者认为不能只靠市场；C 正确，社会可通过政策广泛分享自动化收益；D 错误，作者主张通过制度公平管理转型。"}]}, {"title": "Misinformation in the Age of Social Media", "text": "Misinformation is not a new phenomenon, but social media has transformed its speed, scale, and persistence in ways that challenge democratic societies. False claims that once circulated slowly through rumor can now reach millions within hours, amplified by algorithms designed to prioritize engagement over accuracy. Emotionally charged content, whether about elections, vaccines, or natural disasters, tends to attract clicks, shares, and comments, and platforms profit from the attention it generates. Research shows that corrections rarely achieve the same reach as the original falsehood, and repeated exposure can make even implausible claims feel familiar and therefore true, a phenomenon known as the illusory truth effect. The problem is compounded by the fragmentation of the information environment. People increasingly inhabit echo chambers where their existing beliefs are reinforced and contradictory evidence is filtered out, making it harder to reach a shared understanding of basic facts. Some propose technical solutions, such as fact-checking labels, demoting false content, and deplatforming repeat offenders. Others emphasize media literacy education, teaching citizens to evaluate sources, recognize emotional manipulation, and seek diverse perspectives. Critics of platform intervention warn that private companies should not become arbiters of truth, given their commercial incentives and lack of democratic accountability. Supporters counter that inaction is also a choice, one that allows harmful falsehoods to flourish. Governments face a similar dilemma: regulation can protect public discourse but can also be abused to suppress dissent. Ultimately, addressing misinformation requires more than any single intervention. It demands a combination of transparent platform design, independent journalism, education, and a cultural commitment to valuing evidence over tribal loyalty, a combination that is easier to describe than to achieve in practice, yet essential if public debate is to remain grounded in shared facts. Local journalism deserves particular support, because trusted reporters who know their communities can correct falsehoods more effectively than distant fact-checkers. When people trust the messenger, they are more willing to reconsider a claim, even one that confirms their existing beliefs.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why does misinformation spread quickly on social media?", "options": ["People never share false claims.", "Platforms always verify content.", "False claims are boring.", "Algorithms prioritize engagement over accuracy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，人们常分享虚假内容；B 错误，平台并非总核实；C 错误，情绪化内容很吸引人；D 正确，算法优先互动而非准确性。"}, {"prompt": "What is the “illusory truth effect”?", "options": ["Repeated exposure makes implausible claims feel true.", "Truth is always obvious.", "People forget everything they read.", "Corrections always spread faster."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，反复接触使不可信的说法显得熟悉而真实；B 错误，真相并非总明显；C 错误，未提遗忘一切；D 错误，纠正传播常不及原谣言。"}, {"prompt": "What is an “echo chamber”?", "options": ["A room with good acoustics.", "A type of fact-checking tool.", "An environment where existing beliefs are reinforced and contrary evidence filtered out.", "A government agency."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，非声学房间；B 错误，非核查工具；C 正确，回音室指既有信念被强化、相反证据被过滤的环境；D 错误，非政府机构。"}, {"prompt": "What concern is raised about platform intervention?", "options": ["Platforms have no commercial incentives.", "Intervention always works perfectly.", "Users prefer censorship.", "Private companies should not become arbiters of truth."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，平台有商业动机；B 错误，干预并非总有效；C 错误，未提用户偏好审查；D 正确，批评者担心私营公司成为真相仲裁者。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author suggest is needed?", "options": ["A single technical fix.", "Banning all social media.", "A combination of platform design, journalism, education, and valuing evidence.", "Ignoring misinformation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，单一干预不够；B 错误，未主张禁网；C 正确，需要透明平台设计、独立新闻、教育与重视证据的结合；D 错误，忽视会让虚假信息蔓延。"}]}]}, {"no": 11, "passages": [{"title": "The Silent Crisis of Antibiotic Resistance", "text": "Antibiotics rank among the greatest achievements of modern medicine, transforming once-fatal infections into treatable conditions and making surgery, chemotherapy, and childbirth far safer. Yet this triumph is increasingly threatened by antibiotic resistance, a process in which bacteria evolve to survive drugs designed to kill them. The causes are well understood: overprescription by doctors, self-medication by patients, widespread use in livestock farming, and the release of pharmaceutical waste into the environment all accelerate the emergence of resistant strains. In many parts of the world, antibiotics are available without prescription, and patients often stop taking them once symptoms improve, leaving the hardiest bacteria alive to multiply. The consequences are already visible. Infections that were once routine, such as urinary tract infections and pneumonia, are becoming harder to treat, requiring more expensive drugs, longer hospital stays, and, in some cases, procedures with no effective antibiotic backup. The World Health Organization has warned that without urgent action, resistant infections could cause ten million deaths annually by 2050, surpassing cancer as a leading cause of death. Solutions exist but require coordination across sectors that rarely cooperate. Doctors must prescribe more judiciously, farmers must reduce routine antibiotic use in healthy animals, and pharmaceutical companies need incentives to develop new drugs, a market that is currently unattractive because new antibiotics are deliberately used sparingly to preserve their effectiveness. Public awareness also matters: patients who understand why they should finish a course and why antibiotics do not cure viral colds are less likely to demand them inappropriately. The crisis, in short, is not a mystery of science but a failure of stewardship, a reminder that even miraculous technologies lose their power when used carelessly and without regard for the future, and that preserving antibiotics requires collective responsibility across medicine, agriculture, industry, and everyday life. International cooperation is equally important, because resistant bacteria do not respect borders and a problem ignored in one country can quickly become a crisis in another. Sharing surveillance data and coordinating stewardship efforts may be the most cost-effective investment global health can make.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is antibiotic resistance?", "options": ["A new type of antibiotic.", "A virus that attacks antibiotics.", "Bacteria evolving to survive drugs designed to kill them.", "A shortage of hospitals."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，非新型抗生素；B 错误，非病毒攻击抗生素；C 正确，细菌进化出抵抗药物的能力；D 错误，非医院短缺。"}, {"prompt": "What behavior accelerates resistance?", "options": ["Finishing a full course of antibiotics.", "Washing hands regularly.", "Avoiding antibiotics entirely.", "Stopping antibiotics once symptoms improve."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，完成疗程是正确做法；B 错误，洗手有助于预防；C 错误，合理避免并非加速原因；D 正确，症状好转即停药会留下最顽强的细菌。"}, {"prompt": "What warning has the WHO issued?", "options": ["Resistant infections could cause ten million deaths a year by 2050.", "Antibiotics will cure all diseases.", "Resistance is no longer a problem.", "Cancer will disappear by 2050."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，世卫组织警告到2050年耐药感染每年或致一千万人死亡；B 错误，未说包治百病；C 错误，耐药仍是严重问题；D 错误，未提癌症消失。"}, {"prompt": "Why is developing new antibiotics unattractive to companies?", "options": ["They are too easy to make.", "New antibiotics are used sparingly to preserve effectiveness.", "Governments ban all new drugs.", "Doctors refuse to prescribe them."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，新药研发并不容易；B 正确，新抗生素被有意少用以保持药效，市场回报低；C 错误，未提禁新药；D 错误，医生并非拒绝开药。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main point?", "options": ["Resistance is a scientific mystery with no solution.", "Antibiotics should never be used.", "Resistance is a failure of stewardship, not a mystery of science.", "Farmers are the only cause."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，原因与对策都已知；B 错误，作者主张合理使用而非禁用；C 正确，危机是管理失当而非科学之谜；D 错误，农民只是原因之一。"}]}, {"title": "The Digital Divide in Education", "text": "The shift toward digital learning, accelerated by the pandemic, was widely celebrated as a democratizing force that would bring world-class teaching to every student with an internet connection. In practice, however, it has exposed and sometimes deepened a digital divide that mirrors broader social inequalities. Students from affluent families typically have reliable broadband, personal laptops, quiet study spaces, and parents able to help with technical problems. Their less privileged peers may share a single smartphone among siblings, rely on unstable mobile data, or study in crowded homes where concentration is difficult. These differences are not merely technical; they shape learning outcomes in profound ways. A student who cannot join a live video class, submit assignments on time, or access interactive simulations falls behind not because of ability but because of infrastructure. Teachers report that the gap is often invisible in aggregate statistics, which record enrollment but not the quality of participation. Moreover, digital literacy itself is unevenly distributed. Knowing how to search effectively, evaluate online sources, and use productivity software are skills that some children absorb at home while others must learn at school, if they learn them at all. Some policymakers have responded by distributing devices and subsidizing broadband, important steps that nonetheless fail to address deeper issues such as parental time, housing conditions, and the quality of digital pedagogy. Critics argue that technology is often presented as a substitute for investment in teachers, smaller classes, and community support, when in fact it works best as a complement to those foundations. The lesson of the digital divide is therefore cautionary: without deliberate attention to equity, innovations that promise to level the playing field may instead tilt it further in favor of those who already have the most, leaving disadvantaged learners even further behind in an increasingly digital world. Community centers and libraries can help bridge the gap by offering free devices, reliable internet, and patient tutoring for families who need them. When learning support is available close to home, students are more likely to persist, and parents are more likely to become partners in their children's education.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Digital learning has eliminated inequality.", "All students have equal internet access.", "Technology should replace teachers.", "Digital learning can deepen existing inequalities without attention to equity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，数字化并未消除不平等；B 错误，接入并不平等；C 错误，技术应是补充而非替代教师；D 正确，不关注公平时数字学习会加深不平等。"}, {"prompt": "What example illustrates the divide?", "options": ["Affluent students have broadband and laptops; poorer students may share one phone.", "All students use the same devices.", "Poor students always outperform rich students.", "Teachers prefer online classes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，富裕学生有宽带与电脑，贫困学生可能共用一部手机；B 错误，设备不同；C 错误，未提成绩反超；D 错误，未提教师偏好。"}, {"prompt": "Why is the gap often invisible in statistics?", "options": ["No data is collected.", "All students participate equally.", "Statistics record enrollment but not quality of participation.", "Teachers hide the data."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，数据有收集；B 错误，参与质量不同；C 正确，统计只记录注册人数而非参与质量；D 错误，未提教师隐瞒。"}, {"prompt": "What do critics argue about technology?", "options": ["It should replace all teachers.", "It is useless in education.", "It solves housing problems.", "It works best as a complement to teachers and smaller classes, not a substitute."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未主张取代教师；B 错误，技术并非无用；C 错误，技术不解决住房；D 正确，批评者认为技术应作为教师与小班制的补充。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward distributing devices?", "options": ["It is sufficient alone.", "It is harmful.", "It is an important step but does not address deeper issues.", "It should be stopped."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，仅发设备不够；B 错误，发设备并非有害；C 正确，发设备与补贴宽带重要，但未解决家长时间、住房等深层问题；D 错误，未主张停止。"}]}]}, {"no": 12, "passages": [{"title": "Climate Migration: A Challenge Without Borders", "text": "As climate change intensifies, millions of people are being forced to leave homes made uninhabitable by rising seas, prolonged drought, and extreme heat. The World Bank estimates that by 2050, more than two hundred million people could be displaced within their own countries, a figure that does not include those who cross international borders. Unlike refugees fleeing war, climate migrants often move gradually, first seeking work in nearby towns, then in distant cities, and only later considering another country. This slow movement makes the phenomenon harder to measure and easier for policymakers to ignore until a crisis becomes visible. Host communities face difficult choices. New arrivals can bring labor, skills, and cultural vitality, yet they also increase demand for housing, water, schools, and healthcare in places that may already be strained. When resources are scarce, competition can fuel resentment and political backlash, particularly if migrants are portrayed as outsiders rather than neighbors. International law offers little protection: the 1951 Refugee Convention does not recognize climate as a ground for asylum, leaving many displaced people in legal limbo. Some scholars propose a new framework that would grant climate migrants legal status, planned relocation assistance, and compensation from high-emitting countries, arguing that those who contributed least to warming suffer its worst consequences. Others caution that creating a separate legal category could be politically unfeasible and might divert attention from the more urgent task of reducing emissions and helping communities adapt in place. Despite these disagreements, most experts agree that climate migration is not a distant threat but a present reality, and that orderly, humane responses are far less costly, both economically and morally, than chaotic displacement followed by border closures and humanitarian emergencies. Early investment in adaptation, such as drought-resistant crops and stronger infrastructure, can also reduce the need for migration in the first place. Helping people stay safely at home, when that is what they prefer, is often more humane and less costly than managing displacement after it has already occurred.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does the World Bank estimate?", "options": ["Over 200 million people could be displaced within their countries by 2050.", "No one will migrate due to climate.", "All migration will be international.", "Climate change will stop by 2050."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，世行估计到2050年逾两亿人将在国内流离失所；B 错误，气候移民规模巨大；C 错误，多数为国内流离；D 错误，气候变化不会停止。"}, {"prompt": "How does climate migration often occur?", "options": ["Suddenly, in a single day.", "Gradually, first to nearby towns then farther away.", "Only by airplane.", "Only after a war begins."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，气候移民多为渐进；B 正确，先到附近城镇，再到远方城市；C 错误，方式不限飞机；D 错误，与战争无关。"}, {"prompt": "What legal problem do climate migrants face?", "options": ["They are all granted asylum automatically.", "They receive too much compensation.", "The Refugee Convention does not recognize climate as grounds for asylum.", "They are banned from all cities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，并非自动获庇护；B 错误，补偿不足；C 正确，1951年难民公约不以气候为庇护理由；D 错误，未被禁止进城。"}, {"prompt": "What do some scholars propose?", "options": ["Ignoring climate migration.", "Stopping all migration.", "Closing all borders permanently.", "Legal status, relocation help, and compensation from high emitters."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，学者主张行动而非忽视；B 错误，关闭边境并非提议；C 错误，未主张停止一切移民；D 正确，学者提议给予法律地位、搬迁援助并由高排放国补偿。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main point?", "options": ["Climate migration is a present reality needing orderly, humane responses.", "Climate migration will never happen.", "Host communities never benefit.", "Emission cuts alone solve migration."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，气候移民是现实，需要有序、人道的应对；B 错误，与事实相反；C 错误，新移民也可带来劳动力与活力；D 错误，减排重要但不足以单独解决。"}]}, {"title": "The Science of Happiness", "text": "For centuries, happiness was considered a subject for philosophers and poets rather than scientists, too subjective and elusive to measure. In recent decades, however, psychologists and economists have developed systematic methods to study well-being, using surveys, brain imaging, and long-term studies that follow thousands of people over decades. Their findings challenge many common assumptions. Wealth, for example, improves happiness up to a point, by relieving poverty and insecurity, but beyond a moderate income, additional money brings diminishing returns. Relationships, by contrast, consistently emerge as the strongest predictor of life satisfaction: people with close friendships, stable partnerships, and supportive communities report higher well-being regardless of income. Health, meaningful work, and a sense of autonomy also matter more than status or material possessions. Another surprising finding concerns adaptation. Humans quickly adjust to both good and bad events, a phenomenon known as the hedonic treadmill: a promotion or a new car brings a temporary lift, but people soon return to their baseline level of happiness. This does not mean that circumstances are irrelevant, but it suggests that lasting well-being depends more on daily practices than on single achievements. Gratitude, regular exercise, helping others, and spending time in nature have all been shown to produce sustained improvements. Critics caution that happiness research can be culturally biased, since definitions of a good life vary across societies, and that focusing on individual happiness may distract from structural problems such as inequality and injustice. Nevertheless, the field has shifted public debate: policymakers in several countries now measure national well-being alongside GDP, recognizing that economic growth alone does not guarantee that citizens live fulfilling lives. Workplaces can apply these findings by giving employees autonomy, recognizing their contributions, and protecting time for rest and relationships. A culture that values well-being alongside productivity may discover that happier workers are not only healthier but also more creative and committed.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does research say about wealth and happiness?", "options": ["More money always brings equal happiness.", "Money has no effect at all.", "Wealth helps up to a point, then brings diminishing returns.", "Only the rich can be happy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，财富并非越多越幸福；B 错误，金钱并非毫无作用；C 正确，财富在缓解贫困后回报递减；D 错误，幸福不只属于富人。"}, {"prompt": "What is the strongest predictor of life satisfaction?", "options": ["High income.", "Fame.", "Material possessions.", "Relationships."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，收入非最强预测因素；B 错误，名声未被提及；C 错误，物质财产不如关系重要；D 正确，人际关系是最强的生活满意度预测因素。"}, {"prompt": "What is the “hedonic treadmill”?", "options": ["People quickly adapt to good and bad events and return to baseline happiness.", "A type of exercise machine.", "A new happiness drug.", "A treadmill for athletes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，享乐适应指人们迅速适应好坏事件并回到幸福基线；B 错误，非健身器械；C 错误，非药物；D 错误，非运动员跑步机。"}, {"prompt": "What practices improve well-being?", "options": ["Working longer hours.", "Buying more cars.", "Gratitude, exercise, helping others, and time in nature.", "Avoiding all social contact."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，买车只带来短暂提升；B 错误，工作更久无助于幸福；C 正确，感恩、锻炼、助人、亲近自然能持续改善幸福感；D 错误，避免社交有害。"}, {"prompt": "What change in policy is mentioned?", "options": ["Policymakers ignore well-being.", "All countries have abandoned GDP.", "Happiness research has been banned.", "Some countries now measure national well-being alongside GDP."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未放弃 GDP；B 错误，研究未被禁止；C 错误，决策者并非忽视幸福感；D 正确，一些国家现同时衡量国民幸福感与 GDP。"}]}]}, {"no": 13, "passages": [{"title": "Space Exploration: Ambition or Extravagance?", "text": "Humanity's return to the Moon and ambitions to reach Mars have reignited a long-standing debate: is space exploration a worthy investment or an extravagant distraction from pressing problems on Earth? Supporters argue that exploration drives innovation in ways that are difficult to predict. Technologies originally developed for space, from satellite communications and GPS to water purification and medical imaging, have become integral to daily life. Moreover, they contend, exploration inspires young people to study science and engineering, fostering a skilled workforce that benefits the broader economy. The search for life beyond Earth and the study of other planets also deepen our understanding of climate, geology, and the origins of life itself. Critics respond that the benefits are overstated and unevenly distributed. The billions spent on crewed missions could, they argue, fund vaccines, schools, clean water, and climate adaptation for millions of people who need them now. They also note that private space companies, while reducing costs, raise new concerns about the commercialization of orbit, space debris, and the militarization of space. The environmental cost of frequent rocket launches, though small compared with aviation, is growing as launch rates increase. A middle position acknowledges both perspectives: robotic missions, which are cheaper and safer, may offer the best scientific return, while crewed missions should be justified by clear goals rather than national prestige alone. International cooperation, proponents of this view add, could reduce duplication and ensure that space remains a shared resource rather than a new arena for rivalry. Ultimately, the debate is not only about budgets but about values: what kind of future humanity imagines for itself, and whether looking outward helps us care better for the planet we already inhabit, or merely provides an escape from its problems. Young people inspired by space may go on to solve earthly problems, from clean energy to disease, using skills they first developed while dreaming of other worlds. In that sense, exploration is not an escape from Earth but an investment in the curiosity and capability that Earth most needs.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What benefit do supporters cite?", "options": ["Space technology has led to GPS, satellite communications, and medical imaging.", "Space exploration has no practical benefits.", "Only astronauts benefit.", "Space missions reduce all poverty."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，航天技术带来 GPS、卫星通信、医疗成像等；B 错误，支持者认为益处很多；C 错误，受益不限宇航员；D 错误，未说消除贫困。"}, {"prompt": "What do critics argue the money could fund instead?", "options": ["More rockets.", "Vaccines, schools, clean water, and climate adaptation.", "Luxury hotels.", "Military weapons only."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，批评者反对多花钱在火箭；B 正确，数十亿可用于疫苗、学校、清洁水与气候适应；C 错误，未提豪华酒店；D 错误，非仅武器。"}, {"prompt": "What concern is raised about private space companies?", "options": ["They make launches too cheap.", "They never launch rockets.", "Commercialization, debris, and militarization of space.", "They refuse all government funding."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，降成本是优点；B 错误，私营公司确实发射火箭；C 正确，担忧包括轨道商业化、太空垃圾与军事化；D 错误，未提拒绝资助。"}, {"prompt": "What middle position is suggested?", "options": ["Ban all space missions.", "Ignore international cooperation.", "Only crewed missions matter.", "Robotic missions offer best scientific return; crewed missions need clear goals."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未主张禁止任务；B 错误，仅载人任务并非中间立场；C 错误，国际合作应加强；D 正确，机器人任务性价比最高，载人任务需有明确目标。"}, {"prompt": "What is the deeper issue in the debate?", "options": ["Values: what future humanity imagines and whether space helps us care for Earth.", "Only budgets matter.", "Rocket fuel prices.", "Astronaut salaries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，争论深层是价值观：人类想象何种未来、太空探索能否帮助我们更好地爱护地球；B 错误，预算只是表层；C 错误，燃料价格非核心；D 错误，薪水非核心。"}]}, {"title": "Minimalism and the Search for Enough", "text": "In an era of relentless consumption, minimalism has emerged as both a lifestyle and a critique of modern culture. Its advocates argue that owning fewer possessions can lead to greater freedom, clarity, and well-being. Clutter, they suggest, is not merely untidy; it demands time, money, and attention to maintain, clean, and store, resources that could be devoted to relationships, experiences, or creative work. By deliberately choosing what to keep, minimalists claim, people regain control over their environments and their values. The movement has grown through books, documentaries, and social media, where images of sparse, white-walled apartments have become aspirational. Yet critics argue that minimalism, as popularly presented, is often a privilege disguised as a virtue. For affluent people, discarding possessions is easy because they can repurchase anything they need; for low-income families, owning little is not a choice but a condition imposed by scarcity. The aesthetic of minimalism can also become another form of consumption, as people buy expensive, beautifully designed objects to replace cheaper, functional ones, or purchase courses and books about owning less. Psychologists add nuance: research does show that excessive clutter is associated with stress, and that experiences tend to bring more lasting satisfaction than material goods. However, the benefits depend on motivation. Decluttering driven by genuine values can be liberating, while decluttering driven by social pressure or perfectionism can become another source of anxiety. The most thoughtful proponents therefore emphasize that minimalism is not about a specific number of possessions or a particular style, but about intentionality: asking whether each object, commitment, and habit genuinely serves a meaningful life, and having the courage to let go of what does not. Communities can support this shift by sharing tools, libraries, and spaces, so that people need not own everything they occasionally use. When access replaces ownership for many goods, life becomes lighter without becoming poorer, and neighbors gain reasons to cooperate.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What do minimalists claim about owning less?", "options": ["It is only about white walls.", "It makes people poorer.", "It leads to greater freedom and well-being.", "It requires buying more things."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，少拥有不等于更穷；B 错误，极简非只关白墙；C 正确，极简主义者认为少拥有带来更多自由与幸福感；D 错误，极简反对多买东西。"}, {"prompt": "What criticism is made of popular minimalism?", "options": ["It is too difficult for anyone.", "It bans all possessions.", "It helps only low-income families.", "It is often a privilege: the affluent can repurchase what they discard."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，非人人太难；B 错误，极简并非只帮助低收入家庭；C 错误，未主张禁止一切财产；D 正确，流行极简常是特权：富人丢掉东西后可以再买。"}, {"prompt": "How can minimalism become another form of consumption?", "options": ["By buying expensive designed objects to replace functional ones.", "By giving everything away.", "By never shopping.", "By living outdoors."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，买昂贵设计品替代便宜实用品是另一种消费；B 错误，赠送非消费；C 错误，从不购物非消费；D 错误，未提户外生活。"}, {"prompt": "What does research show about experiences vs. goods?", "options": ["Goods always bring more satisfaction.", "Neither matters.", "Experiences tend to bring more lasting satisfaction.", "Only goods matter for happiness."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，物品并非总带来更多满足；B 错误，物品非幸福唯一来源；C 正确，研究显示体验比物质带来更持久的满足；D 错误，两者并非都不重要。"}, {"prompt": "What is minimalism really about, according to thoughtful proponents?", "options": ["Owning exactly 100 items.", "A specific interior style.", "Impressing others on social media.", "Intentionality: whether each thing serves a meaningful life."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，非固定100件；B 错误，非特定室内风格；C 错误，在社交媒体上炫耀并非本质；D 正确，极简本质是 intentionality：每件物品是否真正服务于有意义的生活。"}]}]}, {"no": 14, "passages": [{"title": "The Power and Limits of Science Communication", "text": "Science shapes modern life, yet public understanding of science remains uneven, with consequences for health, climate policy, and trust in institutions. Effective science communication is therefore increasingly recognized as essential, not optional. Its challenges, however, are formidable. Scientific knowledge is often probabilistic, provisional, and expressed in technical language, while public debate tends to favor certainty, simple stories, and emotional appeal. When scientists say that a vaccine is safe based on large trials, or that warming is very likely human-caused, audiences may hear either absolute guarantee or mere opinion, depending on their prior beliefs. The deficit model, which assumed that skepticism stemmed mainly from ignorance and could be cured by more facts, has been largely discredited. Research shows that people with greater scientific literacy can be more polarized, not less, because they use their knowledge to defend positions tied to identity and community. Values, trust, and social identity often matter more than information alone. Successful communicators therefore do more than transmit facts: they listen, acknowledge uncertainty honestly, share the process of science, and connect findings to audiences' concerns and values. Storytelling, visual explanations, and trusted local messengers, such as doctors, teachers, and community leaders, can be more persuasive than distant experts. At the same time, communicators must navigate a media environment that rewards controversy and speed over nuance and accuracy. The rise of preprint servers and open data has accelerated sharing but also allowed preliminary findings to be misinterpreted as settled conclusions. Ultimately, science communication is not about winning arguments but about building a shared capacity to make informed decisions in an uncertain world, a task that requires humility from scientists and curiosity from the public alike. Training programs for young scientists should therefore include communication skills alongside research methods, so that the next generation can explain its work clearly and listen to public concerns. Science that cannot be understood by the society it serves risks losing the support it needs to continue.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is science communication difficult?", "options": ["Scientific knowledge is probabilistic and technical, while public debate favors certainty and simple stories.", "Science is always certain.", "People never care about science.", "Scientists refuse to speak."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，科学知识是概率性、暂时的、技术性的，而公众辩论偏好确定与简单故事；B 错误，科学常不确定；C 错误，人们关心科学；D 错误，科学家在沟通。"}, {"prompt": "What is the “deficit model”?", "options": ["A model of climate change.", "The idea that skepticism comes from ignorance and can be cured by more facts.", "A type of vaccine.", "A budgeting method."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，气候模型非缺失模型；B 正确，缺失模型认为怀疑源于无知、可通过更多事实治愈；C 错误，非疫苗；D 错误，非预算方法。"}, {"prompt": "What does research show about scientific literacy and polarization?", "options": ["More literacy always reduces polarization.", "Literacy has no effect.", "More literate people can be more polarized.", "Only illiterate people are polarized."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，更多知识未必减少极化；B 错误，知识并非毫无作用；C 正确，科学素养更高的人可能更极化，因用知识捍卫身份立场；D 错误，未说只有文盲极化。"}, {"prompt": "What makes communicators more persuasive?", "options": ["Ignoring audiences' values.", "Using only technical jargon.", "Hiding the process of science.", "Listening, acknowledging uncertainty, and using trusted local messengers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，应连接受众关切；B 错误，纯术语难懂；C 错误，隐藏科学过程不可取；D 正确，倾听、承认不确定性、使用可信的本地传播者更有效。"}, {"prompt": "What is the goal of science communication, according to the author?", "options": ["Building shared capacity to make informed decisions.", "Winning arguments.", "Proving scientists are always right.", "Avoiding all uncertainty."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，目标是建立共同做出明智决策的能力；B 错误，非赢得争论；C 错误，科学并非总正确；D 错误，应诚实承认不确定性。"}]}, {"title": "Why Cities Need More Trees", "text": "Urban trees are often treated as decoration, pleasant but dispensable when budgets tighten or development pressures grow. A growing body of evidence suggests that this view is profoundly mistaken: trees are essential infrastructure, providing services that are difficult and expensive to replicate by other means. Their canopies shade streets and buildings, reducing summer temperatures by several degrees and lowering demand for air conditioning. Their roots absorb stormwater, reducing flooding, while their leaves filter air pollutants and capture carbon dioxide. Beyond these physical benefits, trees support mental health. Studies consistently find that residents of greener neighborhoods report lower stress, better mood, and even faster recovery from illness. Children with access to green spaces show improved attention and academic performance, while older adults in tree-lined areas are more likely to walk and socialize. Yet the distribution of urban trees is strikingly unequal. Wealthier neighborhoods often enjoy abundant canopy, while poorer districts, frequently hotter and more polluted, have far fewer trees, a pattern sometimes called tree inequity. Planting programs that ignore this disparity can even worsen it, if new trees are concentrated where maintenance is easiest rather than where need is greatest. Moreover, planting alone is insufficient: young trees require watering, pruning, and protection from damage for years before they deliver full benefits, and many municipal programs fail to fund this long-term care. Community involvement helps, as residents who participate in planting and stewardship are more likely to protect trees and advocate for their care. The lesson is that urban forestry should be treated like any other public service, planned, funded, and maintained with equity in mind, rather than as an optional beautification project that can be cut when convenient. Residents who help choose where trees are planted and who care for them are more likely to protect them through droughts and storms. In this way, urban forestry becomes not only an environmental program but also a community project that strengthens the neighborhoods it serves.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea?", "options": ["Trees are merely decoration.", "Trees increase flooding.", "Trees are essential infrastructure providing cooling, flood control, and health benefits.", "Trees harm mental health."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，树木非仅装饰；B 错误，树木减少洪水；C 正确，树木是重要基础设施，提供降温、防洪与健康益处；D 错误，树木改善心理健康。"}, {"prompt": "How do trees reduce temperatures?", "options": ["By blocking all sunlight permanently.", "By removing all pavement.", "By increasing air conditioning use.", "By shading streets and buildings."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，非永久遮挡阳光；B 错误，树木降低空调需求；C 错误，未提移除路面；D 正确，树冠遮荫街道与建筑从而降温。"}, {"prompt": "What is “tree inequity”?", "options": ["Wealthier areas have abundant canopy while poorer, hotter districts have far fewer.", "All neighborhoods have equal trees.", "Trees grow equally everywhere.", "Poor districts have more trees."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，树木不平等指富裕地区树冠丰富、贫困炎热地区树木稀少；B 错误，分布不均；C 错误，生长并不均等；D 错误，贫困地区树更少。"}, {"prompt": "Why is planting alone insufficient?", "options": ["No one likes trees.", "Trees grow instantly.", "Young trees need years of watering, pruning, and protection.", "Planting is free."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，树木不会瞬间长大；B 错误，人们喜欢树木；C 正确，幼树需要多年浇水、修剪与保护才能发挥全部效益；D 错误，种植并非免费。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author recommend?", "options": ["Ignore long-term care.", "Cut tree programs when budgets tighten.", "Plant trees only in wealthy areas.", "Treat urban forestry as a planned, funded public service with equity in mind."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，不应随意削减树木项目；B 错误，应关注贫困地区而非只富裕地区；C 错误，不应忽视长期养护；D 正确，应把城市林业当作有规划、有资金、讲公平的公共服务。"}]}]}, {"no": 15, "passages": [{"title": "The Global Scandal of Food Waste", "text": "Roughly one third of all food produced in the world is never eaten, a staggering figure that represents both a moral failure and an environmental disaster. In wealthy countries, much of the waste occurs at the retail and household level: supermarkets discard cosmetically imperfect produce, restaurants serve oversized portions, and families throw away leftovers they forgot in the refrigerator. In poorer countries, by contrast, food is more often lost earlier in the supply chain, spoiling during harvest, storage, or transport for lack of refrigeration and decent roads. The environmental costs are enormous. Wasted food accounts for about eight percent of global greenhouse gas emissions, because producing it consumed land, water, fertilizer, and energy, and rotting food in landfills releases methane, a potent greenhouse gas. The social paradox is equally stark: while nearly eight hundred million people suffer from hunger, edible food is discarded every day in the same cities where food banks struggle to meet demand. Solutions exist at every level. Farmers can adopt better storage, retailers can sell imperfect produce at a discount and donate surplus, and households can plan meals, store food properly, and learn to use leftovers creatively. Technology helps too, with apps connecting shops to consumers willing to buy near-expiry items cheaply. Governments can set reduction targets, standardize date labels that confuse consumers, and fund infrastructure in developing regions. Yet experts caution that technical fixes alone are insufficient without a cultural shift: societies must stop treating abundance as a sign of prosperity and cheap food as an entitlement, and instead recognize that wasting food wastes the labor, water, and soil that produced it, resources that future generations will desperately need. Schools can teach children to value food by growing vegetables, cooking simple meals, and understanding where their food comes from. Habits formed early, such as taking only what one can eat and saving leftovers, often last a lifetime and shape how families shop and cook.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What proportion of food is never eaten?", "options": ["Roughly one third of all food produced.", "Almost none.", "About one tenth.", "More than half."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，约三分之一的食物从未被吃掉；B 错误，浪费规模巨大；C 错误，比例约为三分之一而非十分之一；D 错误，未说超过一半。"}, {"prompt": "How does food waste differ between rich and poor countries?", "options": ["There is no difference.", "Rich countries waste at retail and household level; poor countries lose food earlier in storage and transport.", "Poor countries waste more at home.", "Rich countries lose food during harvest."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，差异明显；B 正确，富国浪费在零售与家庭环节，穷国损失在收获、储存与运输环节；C 错误，穷国主要损失在供应链前端；D 错误，收获损失多在穷国。"}, {"prompt": "Why is rotting food in landfills harmful?", "options": ["It produces oxygen.", "It improves soil instantly.", "It releases methane, a potent greenhouse gas.", "It reduces emissions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，腐烂食物不产生氧气；B 错误，腐烂不会立即改良土壤；C 正确，垃圾填埋场腐烂食物释放甲烷这种强效温室气体；D 错误，腐烂增加排放。"}, {"prompt": "What solution is suggested for retailers?", "options": ["Throwing away all imperfect produce.", "Closing supermarkets.", "Raising prices for everyone.", "Selling imperfect produce at a discount and donating surplus."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，应打折出售而非丢弃；B 错误，未提为所有人涨价；C 错误，未提关闭超市；D 正确，零售商可打折出售品相不佳的农产品并捐赠剩余食物。"}, {"prompt": "What cultural shift do experts call for?", "options": ["Recognizing that wasting food wastes labor, water, and soil.", "Treating abundance as prosperity.", "Treating cheap food as an entitlement.", "Ignoring food waste."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，专家呼吁认识到浪费食物就是浪费生产它的劳动、水与土壤；B 错误，应停止把丰裕视为繁荣；C 错误，不应把廉价食物视为应得权利；D 错误，不应忽视浪费。"}]}, {"title": "Online Learning: Promise and Pitfalls", "text": "Massive open online courses, known as MOOCs, were once heralded as a revolution that would make elite university education free and available to everyone on Earth. A decade later, the verdict is more mixed. On the positive side, online platforms have undeniably expanded access: a learner in a remote village can now watch lectures from leading professors, practice coding, and earn certificates without relocating or paying full tuition. During the pandemic, online learning kept education going when classrooms closed, and it continues to serve working adults who need flexible schedules. Yet completion rates remain strikingly low, often below ten percent, suggesting that access alone does not guarantee learning. Without the structure of fixed class times, peer pressure, and face-to-face interaction with teachers, many learners struggle to stay motivated, especially when courses are long and demanding. The quality of interaction also suffers: discussion forums can feel impersonal, feedback is often delayed or automated, and cheating is harder to prevent. Moreover, online learning assumes a level of digital access and self-regulation that not all students possess, potentially widening gaps between advantaged and disadvantaged learners. The most promising models, researchers argue, are blended: online materials for flexible content delivery combined with in-person tutoring, group projects, and mentoring. Such hybrid approaches preserve the scale of online learning while restoring the human relationships that sustain motivation and deepen understanding. The lesson is that technology can extend the reach of good teaching, but it cannot replace the social and emotional dimensions of learning that make education transformative rather than merely informational. Employers can also recognize online credentials more consistently, so that learners who complete rigorous courses receive tangible rewards for their effort. When online learning leads to real opportunities, motivation rises and the low completion rates that plague many platforms begin to improve. Such recognition turns isolated study into a pathway toward employment and further learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What was once expected of MOOCs?", "options": ["They would be expensive for everyone.", "They would replace all schools immediately.", "They would make elite education free and available to everyone.", "They would only teach coding."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，未说立即取代所有学校；B 错误，慕课并非对所有人都昂贵；C 正确，慕课曾被期待让精英教育免费并惠及所有人；D 错误，课程不限编程。"}, {"prompt": "What is a major problem with MOOCs?", "options": ["Too many teachers.", "No one enrolls.", "Courses are too short.", "Completion rates are often below ten percent."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，教师并非过多；B 错误，课程常很长；C 错误，有很多人注册；D 正确，慕课完成率常低于10%。"}, {"prompt": "Why do many learners struggle online?", "options": ["They lack structure, peer pressure, and face-to-face interaction.", "Courses are too easy.", "They have too much free time.", "Teachers give too much feedback."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，学习者缺乏固定课时、同伴压力与面对面互动，难以坚持；B 错误，课程并不容易；C 错误，时间并非原因；D 错误，反馈常延迟或自动化。"}, {"prompt": "What model do researchers find most promising?", "options": ["Pure online learning.", "No learning at all.", "Blended learning combining online materials with in-person tutoring and mentoring.", "Only lectures."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，纯网课完成率低；B 错误，非不学习；C 正确，混合模式最有前景；D 错误，仅讲座不够。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's conclusion?", "options": ["Certificates guarantee jobs.", "Online learning is useless.", "Classrooms should be abolished.", "Technology can extend good teaching but cannot replace the social dimensions of learning."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，网课并非无用；B 错误，未主张废除教室；C 错误，证书不保证就业；D 正确，技术能扩展好教学的覆盖面，但无法取代学习的社会维度。"}]}]}, {"no": 16, "passages": [{"title": "Breaking the Stigma of Mental Illness", "text": "Despite growing awareness, mental illness remains surrounded by stigma that prevents many people from seeking help. Unlike a broken leg, depression or anxiety carries no visible cast, and sufferers are often told to simply cheer up or try harder, as if their condition were a matter of willpower. This misunderstanding has serious consequences. People may delay treatment for years, relationships may strain under secrecy, and workplaces may penalize those who disclose their struggles. Research shows that stigma operates on multiple levels: public stigma in the form of stereotypes, self-stigma when individuals internalize negative beliefs, and structural stigma when policies and institutions fail to provide adequate care. Celebrities and athletes who speak openly about their mental health have helped to normalize conversation, yet visibility alone is insufficient if services remain underfunded and inaccessible. Schools and workplaces are beginning to respond with mental health first aid training, flexible working arrangements, and employee assistance programs. However, critics warn that awareness campaigns can backfire if they present mental illness as a simple chemical imbalance curable by medication alone, ignoring social factors such as poverty, loneliness, and trauma. Effective responses, experts argue, require both cultural change and material investment: challenging stereotypes, training professionals, funding community services, and ensuring that insurance covers mental healthcare on par with physical healthcare. The goal is not merely tolerance but genuine inclusion, a society in which a person can say they are struggling without fearing that they will be judged, pitied, or excluded from opportunities that others take for granted. Peer support groups, where people share experiences without judgment, can complement professional treatment by reducing isolation and building hope. Recovery often begins not with a prescription but with the simple discovery that one is not alone in struggling. When workplaces treat mental health as seriously as physical health, employees feel safer seeking help early.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is mental illness often misunderstood?", "options": ["Sufferers are told to cheer up, as if it were willpower.", "It has visible symptoms like a broken leg.", "Everyone understands it perfectly.", "It is never serious."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，患者常被告知振作起来、仿佛只是意志力问题；B 错误，精神疾病不像骨折有可见石膏；C 错误，理解并不充分；D 错误，病情严重。"}, {"prompt": "What is “self-stigma”?", "options": ["Stereotypes held by the public.", "When individuals internalize negative beliefs about themselves.", "A government policy.", "A type of medication."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，那是公众污名；B 正确，自我污名指个人内化负面信念；C 错误，非政府政策；D 错误，非药物。"}, {"prompt": "What is a limitation of celebrity disclosure?", "options": ["It always solves the problem.", "Celebrities never speak openly.", "Visibility alone is insufficient if services remain underfunded.", "It increases stigma."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，可见性不能单独解决问题；B 错误，名人确实公开讲述；C 正确，仅提高可见性不够，若服务仍资金不足、难以获得；D 错误，公开讲述有助于去污名化。"}, {"prompt": "What warning do critics give about awareness campaigns?", "options": ["They should never happen.", "They always work.", "They are too expensive.", "They can backfire if they present illness as only a chemical imbalance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，宣传有价值；B 错误，未提费用过高；C 错误，宣传并非总有效；D 正确，批评者警告，若宣传把精神疾病说成仅是化学失衡可单靠药物治愈，可能适得其反。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author call for?", "options": ["Cultural change and material investment for genuine inclusion.", "Tolerance only.", "Ignoring mental health.", "Medication alone."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，作者呼吁文化改变与物质投入以实现真正包容；B 错误，仅容忍不够；C 错误，不应忽视心理健康；D 错误，仅药物不够。"}]}, {"title": "The Renewable Energy Transition", "text": "The transition from fossil fuels to renewable energy is often described as inevitable, driven by falling costs of solar and wind power and by the urgent need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. In many regions, new solar and wind projects are now cheaper than operating existing coal plants, a tipping point that would have seemed implausible a decade ago. Yet the transition faces formidable obstacles that go beyond price. Solar and wind are intermittent, producing power only when the sun shines or the wind blows, which requires massive investment in batteries, transmission lines, and flexible demand. Land use is another contentious issue: large solar farms and wind turbines can conflict with agriculture, wildlife habitats, and local communities that object to changes in landscape. The supply chains for batteries and solar panels also raise concerns, depending heavily on minerals mined in a small number of countries under conditions that are sometimes environmentally damaging and ethically troubling. Moreover, the transition creates winners and losers. Coal-dependent regions face job losses and economic decline, while the benefits of new energy jobs may flow to different places and require different skills. A just transition, advocates argue, must include retraining, income support, and investment in affected communities, rather than treating them as collateral damage. Despite these challenges, the direction of travel is clear, and delay carries its own costs: every year of continued fossil fuel dependence locks in more warming and more expensive adaptation later. The question is no longer whether the transition will happen, but whether societies can manage it quickly and fairly enough to limit climate damage while ensuring that no community is left behind in the shift to a cleaner energy system. International cooperation on technology transfer can help developing countries leapfrog fossil fuels, building clean energy systems from the start rather than repeating the polluting path of earlier industrialization. Such cooperation benefits everyone, because emissions reduced anywhere help slow warming everywhere.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is renewable energy now competitive?", "options": ["Renewables need no infrastructure.", "Fossil fuels became free.", "Solar and wind are now cheaper than operating coal plants in many regions.", "Coal plants closed everywhere."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，化石燃料并未免费；B 错误，可再生能源需要基础设施；C 正确，许多地区新建太阳能与风能比运营煤电更便宜；D 错误，煤电并未全部关闭。"}, {"prompt": "What problem does intermittency create?", "options": ["It makes energy too cheap.", "It makes solar panels last forever.", "It eliminates all demand.", "It requires investment in batteries and transmission."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，间歇性不会使能源过便宜；B 错误，需求并未消除；C 错误，太阳能板不会因此永用；D 正确，间歇性需要大量投资于电池与输电。"}, {"prompt": "What is a concern about supply chains?", "options": ["They depend on minerals from few countries, sometimes mined harmfully.", "They use no minerals.", "They are entirely local.", "They have no ethical issues."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，供应链依赖少数国家开采的矿物，有时环境与伦理成问题；B 错误，需要矿物；C 错误，并非完全本地；D 错误，存在伦理问题。"}, {"prompt": "What does a “just transition” require?", "options": ["Ignoring coal regions.", "Closing all mines overnight.", "Retraining, income support, and investment in affected communities.", "No support for workers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，不应忽视煤炭地区；B 错误，不应一夜关闭所有矿井；C 正确，公正转型需要再培训、收入支持与对受影响社区的投资；D 错误，需要支持工人。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main point?", "options": ["The transition will never happen.", "Delay has no cost.", "Fossil fuels should expand.", "The transition is inevitable but must be managed quickly and fairly."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，转型方向明确；B 错误，不应扩张化石燃料；C 错误，拖延有代价；D 正确，转型不可避免，但必须快速且公平地管理。"}]}]}, {"no": 17, "passages": [{"title": "When Languages Disappear", "text": "Every two weeks, according to linguists, a language dies somewhere in the world, taking with it unique ways of describing nature, kinship, and the human experience. Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, nearly half are endangered, spoken mainly by elderly people whose children have shifted to a dominant language for education and employment. Language loss is often framed as inevitable, the price of globalization and modernization, but researchers argue that it is neither natural nor unavoidable. Languages disappear when their speakers face discrimination, when schools punish children for using their mother tongue, and when media and government services operate only in majority languages. The consequences extend beyond communication. Indigenous languages often encode detailed ecological knowledge: names for plants, weather patterns, and animal behavior that have no equivalent in dominant languages and that can inform conservation and medicine. When a language falls silent, that knowledge, accumulated over centuries, can vanish with it. Efforts to revitalize languages are growing, from immersion schools in Hawaii and New Zealand to smartphone apps and community radio in remote villages. Technology, once seen as a threat, can help: recording elders, creating digital dictionaries, and connecting dispersed speakers online. Yet revitalization requires more than documentation; it needs living use in homes, schools, and public life, which in turn requires political support and funding. Critics sometimes ask why societies should invest in languages spoken by few people when resources are scarce. Defenders respond that linguistic diversity, like biodiversity, enriches humanity as a whole, and that the cost of losing a language is borne not only by its speakers but by everyone who will never hear its stories, songs, and wisdom. Governments can support revitalization by funding bilingual education, broadcasting in minority languages, and recognizing indigenous languages in official documents. These measures signal that a language is valued, encouraging parents to pass it on to their children with pride.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How often does a language die?", "options": ["About every two weeks.", "Once a century.", "Never.", "Every day exactly one."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，语言学家估计每两周有一种语言消亡；B 错误，频率远高于此；C 错误，语言正在消亡；D 错误，未说每天恰好一种。"}, {"prompt": "Why do languages disappear?", "options": ["They are naturally too difficult.", "Speakers face discrimination and schools punish mother-tongue use.", "People prefer silence.", "Languages never change."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误，消亡非自然不可避免；B 正确，歧视、学校惩罚母语、媒体只用主流语言导致消亡；C 错误，人们并非偏好沉默；D 错误，语言会变化。"}, {"prompt": "What knowledge is lost with a language?", "options": ["Only grammar rules.", "Nothing important.", "Detailed ecological knowledge about plants, weather, and animals.", "Only songs."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，损失远超语法；B 错误，损失的并非不重要；C 正确，语言消亡会带走关于植物、天气与动物的详细生态知识；D 错误，不只歌曲。"}, {"prompt": "What helps revitalization?", "options": ["Banning all technology.", "Ignoring elders.", "Forbidding mother tongues.", "Immersion schools, apps, and community radio."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，技术可帮助；B 错误，不应禁止母语；C 错误，不应忽视长者；D 正确，沉浸式学校、应用与社区广播有助于语言复兴。"}, {"prompt": "What argument do defenders make?", "options": ["Linguistic diversity enriches humanity like biodiversity.", "Only majority languages matter.", "Small languages are worthless.", "Revitalization is impossible."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，语言多样性如生物多样性丰富全人类；B 错误，非只有主流语言重要；C 错误，小语种并非无价值；D 错误，复兴是可能的。"}]}, {"title": "Rethinking Urban Transport", "text": "For most of the twentieth century, cities were designed around the private car, with wide roads, sprawling suburbs, and parking lots consuming vast amounts of space. The results are now familiar: traffic congestion, air pollution, road deaths, and neighborhoods divided by highways. A growing movement of urban planners argues that this model is unsustainable and that cities should prioritize walking, cycling, and public transport instead. Evidence from cities that have made the shift is encouraging. Amsterdam and Copenhagen, where cycling is safe and convenient, report lower emissions, healthier residents, and vibrant street life. Bus rapid transit systems in Latin America have moved millions of people efficiently at a fraction of the cost of metro systems. Yet the transition is politically difficult. Car owners often resist measures such as congestion charges, reduced parking, and dedicated bus lanes, viewing them as attacks on their freedom. Businesses worry that fewer cars will mean fewer customers, although studies frequently show that pedestrians and cyclists spend more per month in local shops than drivers do. Equity is another concern: public transport must be affordable, reliable, and safe, especially for low-income residents, women, and people with disabilities, or the shift will benefit only the privileged. Technology offers tools, from real-time transit apps to bike-sharing schemes, but it cannot substitute for political will and long-term investment. The most successful cities, researchers find, combine infrastructure with incentives and restrictions: building protected cycle lanes, improving buses and trains, and simultaneously making driving less convenient in dense centers. Such an approach does not eliminate cars entirely, but it rebalances the city in favor of people rather than vehicles, creating places that are cleaner, safer, and more pleasant to live in. Children benefit especially from safer streets, gaining independence to walk or cycle to school and play outdoors without fear of traffic. A city designed for its youngest residents often turns out to be a city that works better for everyone, from older adults to busy parents.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What problems did car-centered design cause?", "options": ["Less pollution.", "More green space.", "Congestion, pollution, road deaths, and divided neighborhoods.", "Cheaper housing."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，以车为中心加剧污染；B 错误，未提更多绿地；C 正确，以车为中心导致拥堵、污染、交通事故与社区割裂；D 错误，未提更便宜住房。"}, {"prompt": "What do Amsterdam and Copenhagen show?", "options": ["Public transport fails.", "Cars are essential everywhere.", "Cycling is dangerous.", "Cycling cities have lower emissions and healthier residents."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，汽车并非处处必需；B 错误，骑行安全便利；C 错误，公交并未失败；D 正确，阿姆斯特丹与哥本哈根表明骑行城市排放更低、居民更健康。"}, {"prompt": "What do studies show about pedestrians and cyclists?", "options": ["They spend more per month in local shops than drivers.", "They spend less than drivers.", "They never shop.", "They avoid local businesses."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，研究显示步行者与骑行者每月在本地商店花费比司机更多；B 错误，他们会购物；C 错误，他们常光顾本地商家；D 错误，未提避开本地商业。"}, {"prompt": "What equity concern is raised?", "options": ["Women should not use buses.", "Only the rich need transport.", "Public transport must be affordable and safe for low-income residents and others.", "Disabled people prefer cars."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，低收入者更需要公交；B 错误，未说女性不应乘公交；C 正确，公交必须对低收入居民、女性与残障人士负担得起、可靠且安全；D 错误，未提残疾人偏好汽车。"}, {"prompt": "What approach works best?", "options": ["Building cycle lanes only.", "Making driving easier everywhere.", "Eliminating all public transport.", "Combining infrastructure with incentives and restrictions to rebalance cities for people."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，仅建车道不够；B 错误，应使市中心开车不那么便利；C 错误，不应取消公交；D 正确，最佳做法是基础设施、激励与限制相结合，让城市回归以人为本。"}]}]}, {"no": 18, "passages": [{"title": "Trust in Institutions in a Skeptical Age", "text": "Public trust in major institutions, from governments and media to universities and corporations, has declined sharply in many countries over the past two decades, a trend that worries political scientists and sociologists alike. Trust, they argue, is the invisible glue that allows societies to function: citizens accept election results, follow public health guidance, and pay taxes partly because they believe institutions act in good faith and with reasonable competence. When that belief erodes, cooperation becomes harder, conspiracy theories flourish, and even well-designed policies face resistance. The causes of declining trust are complex and contested. Some blame the institutions themselves, pointing to corruption scandals, misleading statements, and a sense that elites serve their own interests rather than the public good. Others emphasize changes in the information environment, where social media amplifies errors and scandals while giving little attention to routine competence. Economic insecurity also plays a role: people who feel left behind by globalization and technological change are more likely to distrust the institutions associated with those forces. Rebuilding trust, experts suggest, is not a matter of better public relations. It requires demonstrable accountability, such as independent investigations, transparent decision-making, and consequences for wrongdoing, as well as genuine responsiveness to citizens' concerns. Local institutions, such as community health clinics and neighborhood schools, often retain higher trust than national bodies, suggesting that proximity and personal contact matter. Ultimately, trust cannot be demanded; it must be earned repeatedly through actions that show institutions are both capable and honest, and societies that fail to rebuild it may find it increasingly difficult to solve collective problems that require shared sacrifice and coordinated action. Journalists, too, bear responsibility, by distinguishing between legitimate criticism and cynicism, and by reporting not only failures but also instances when institutions work well. A public that hears only about scandals will find it hard to believe that reform is possible.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Declining trust makes cooperation harder and must be rebuilt through accountability and responsiveness.", "Trust has increased in recent decades.", "Institutions never make mistakes.", "Conspiracy theories are harmless."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Declining trust makes cooperation harder and must be rebuilt through accountability and responsiveness.，文章指出信任是社会合作的黏合剂，信任下降使合作更难，需通过问责与回应重建；B Trust has increased in recent decades.，与文章内容不符；C Institutions never make mistakes.，与文章内容不符；D Conspiracy theories are harmless.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Why do citizens follow public health guidance, according to the passage?", "options": ["Because they fear punishment only.", "Partly because they believe institutions act in good faith and competently.", "Because guidance is always perfect.", "Because they never question authority."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Because they fear punishment only.，与文章内容不符；B Partly because they believe institutions act in good faith and competently.，文章说公民接受选举结果、遵循公共卫生指引，部分原因是相信机构善意且有能力；C Because guidance is always perfect.，与文章内容不符；D Because they never question authority.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What role does social media play in declining trust?", "options": ["It always reports accurately.", "It increases trust in all institutions.", "It amplifies errors and scandals while ignoring routine competence.", "It has no effect on trust."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A It always reports accurately.，与文章内容不符；B It increases trust in all institutions.，与文章内容不符；C It amplifies errors and scandals while ignoring routine competence.，文章指出社交媒体放大错误与丑闻，却很少关注日常的胜任表现；D It has no effect on trust.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What does rebuilding trust require?", "options": ["Better slogans only.", "Ignoring citizens' concerns.", "More secrecy.", "Demonstrable accountability, transparency, and consequences for wrongdoing."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Better slogans only.，与文章内容不符；B Ignoring citizens' concerns.，与文章内容不符；C More secrecy.，与文章内容不符；D Demonstrable accountability, transparency, and consequences for wrongdoing.，文章强调重建信任需要可证明的问责、透明决策与对不当行为的后果。"}, {"prompt": "Why do local institutions retain higher trust?", "options": ["Because proximity and personal contact matter.", "Because they are larger.", "Because they never make errors.", "Because they avoid all contact."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Because proximity and personal contact matter.，文章指出社区诊所、邻里学校等本地机构信任度更高，说明 proximity 与个人接触重要；B Because they are larger.，与文章内容不符；C Because they never make errors.，与文章内容不符；D Because they avoid all contact.，与文章内容不符。"}]}, {"title": "The Aging of Societies", "text": "Across much of the world, populations are aging at an unprecedented rate as birth rates fall and life expectancy rises, a demographic shift with profound economic, social, and political implications. In countries such as Japan, Italy, and South Korea, the proportion of people over sixty-five already exceeds one fifth of the population, and the ratio of workers to retirees is shrinking steadily. This transformation challenges pension systems designed when lives were shorter and families larger, raising difficult questions about retirement ages, benefit levels, and intergenerational fairness. Healthcare systems face similar pressures, as older populations require more treatment for chronic conditions, long-term care, and support for dementia. Yet aging is not only a burden to be managed; it is also a triumph of public health and a source of potential. Older adults contribute through paid work, volunteering, childcare for grandchildren, and accumulated knowledge and experience. The concept of healthy aging emphasizes that many age-related declines can be delayed through exercise, social engagement, and preventive care, allowing people to remain active well into their seventies and beyond. Policies that encourage flexible retirement, lifelong learning, and age-friendly cities, with accessible transport, housing, and public spaces, can help societies adapt. The alternative, framing aging solely as a crisis, risks reinforcing ageism and overlooking the contributions of older citizens. The central challenge, demographers argue, is to redesign institutions built for a younger world, from workplaces to healthcare, so that longer lives become an opportunity for fulfillment and continued contribution rather than a source of fiscal panic and social division. Intergenerational programs, in which older adults mentor young people and young people help elders with technology, can strengthen bonds across age groups. Such exchanges remind societies that aging is not a separation from community life but a different way of participating in it. Planning for longer lives now will spare future generations far more difficult choices later.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What demographic shift is described?", "options": ["Birth rates are rising rapidly.", "Life expectancy is falling.", "Populations aging as birth rates fall and life expectancy rises.", "Populations are getting younger."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Birth rates are rising rapidly.，与文章内容不符；B Life expectancy is falling.，与文章内容不符；C Populations aging as birth rates fall and life expectancy rises.，文章描述出生率下降、预期寿命上升导致人口老龄化；D Populations are getting younger.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What challenge does aging pose to pensions?", "options": ["Pensions need no reform.", "Retirees are disappearing.", "Workers are increasing rapidly.", "Systems designed for shorter lives and larger families face shrinking worker-to-retiree ratios."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Pensions need no reform.，与文章内容不符；B Retirees are disappearing.，与文章内容不符；C Workers are increasing rapidly.，与文章内容不符；D Systems designed for shorter lives and larger families face shrinking worker-to-retiree ratios.，文章指出养老金制度是为寿命较短、家庭较大的时代设计的，劳动力与退休者比例在缩小。"}, {"prompt": "How do older adults contribute?", "options": ["Through work, volunteering, childcare, and experience.", "They contribute nothing.", "They only consume resources.", "They avoid all social roles."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Through work, volunteering, childcare, and experience.，文章列举老年人通过有偿工作、志愿服务、照看孙辈与积累的经验做贡献；B They contribute nothing.，与文章内容不符；C They only consume resources.，与文章内容不符；D They avoid all social roles.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What does “healthy aging” emphasize?", "options": ["Aging cannot be influenced.", "Many declines can be delayed through exercise, social engagement, and preventive care.", "Only medicine matters.", "Exercise is harmful for older people."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Aging cannot be influenced.，与文章内容不符；B Many declines can be delayed through exercise, social engagement, and preventive care.，文章说健康老龄化强调锻炼、社交参与与预防保健可延缓许多衰退；C Only medicine matters.，与文章内容不符；D Exercise is harmful for older people.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward framing aging only as a crisis?", "options": ["It is the only correct view.", "It encourages respect.", "It risks reinforcing ageism and overlooking older citizens' contributions.", "It has no consequences."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A It is the only correct view.，与文章内容不符；B It encourages respect.，与文章内容不符；C It risks reinforcing ageism and overlooking older citizens' contributions.，文章警告仅把老龄化视为危机会强化年龄歧视、忽视老年人的贡献；D It has no consequences.，与文章内容不符。"}]}]}, {"no": 19, "passages": [{"title": "Can Machines Be Creative?", "text": "The emergence of artificial intelligence systems that can write poems, compose music, and generate images has sparked a fierce debate about the nature of creativity itself. For centuries, creativity was regarded as a uniquely human trait, rooted in consciousness, emotion, and lived experience. When a machine produces a painting that wins a competition or a song that moves listeners, that assumption is shaken. Supporters of AI creativity argue that human artists also build on existing works, combining influences, patterns, and techniques in novel ways, and that machines do something similar, albeit at vastly greater scale and speed. They point out that audiences often cannot distinguish AI-generated works from human ones, suggesting that the value of art lies in the experience it creates rather than in the identity of its creator. Skeptics counter that AI systems merely remix patterns from their training data without understanding, intention, or feeling, and that calling their output creative stretches the word beyond recognition. They argue that true creativity involves breaking rules deliberately, expressing a personal vision, and taking risks, qualities that require a self that machines do not possess. Legal and economic questions follow: who owns AI-generated art, how should human artists be compensated when their work trains models, and will the flood of cheap generated content devalue human craftsmanship? The debate may ultimately reveal less about machines than about humans, forcing us to articulate what we value in art: originality, emotional resonance, technical skill, or the knowledge that another conscious being made it. Whatever the answer, the boundary between human and machine creativity is unlikely to remain as clear as it once seemed. Schools might respond by teaching students to use AI tools critically, as collaborators that can suggest ideas while humans retain judgment about what is meaningful. In this partnership, creativity becomes less about producing something from nothing and more about choosing wisely among many possibilities.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What debate has AI art sparked?", "options": ["Whether machines can be creative and what creativity itself is.", "Whether machines can fly.", "Whether art should be banned.", "Whether humans can paint."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Whether machines can be creative and what creativity itself is.，文章指出 AI 能写诗作曲作画，引发关于创造力本质的激烈争论；B Whether machines can fly.，与文章内容不符；C Whether art should be banned.，与文章内容不符；D Whether humans can paint.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What do supporters argue about human artists?", "options": ["Human artists create from nothing.", "They also build on existing works, combining influences in novel ways.", "Human artists never learn from others.", "Machines are identical to humans."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Human artists create from nothing.，与文章内容不符；B They also build on existing works, combining influences in novel ways.，文章说支持者认为人类艺术家同样建立在已有作品之上、组合影响与模式；C Human artists never learn from others.，与文章内容不符；D Machines are identical to humans.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What do skeptics claim AI lacks?", "options": ["Speed and scale.", "Training data.", "Understanding, intention, and feeling.", "The ability to copy patterns."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Speed and scale.，与文章内容不符；B Training data.，与文章内容不符；C Understanding, intention, and feeling.，文章指出怀疑者认为 AI 只是重组训练数据模式，缺乏理解、意图与情感；D The ability to copy patterns.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What economic question is raised?", "options": ["Whether art should be free for all.", "Whether machines need salaries.", "Whether competitions should end.", "How human artists should be compensated when their work trains models."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Whether art should be free for all.，与文章内容不符；B Whether machines need salaries.，与文章内容不符；C Whether competitions should end.，与文章内容不符；D How human artists should be compensated when their work trains models.，文章提出当人类艺术家的作品用于训练模型时应如何补偿他们。"}, {"prompt": "What does the debate reveal, according to the author?", "options": ["What humans value in art, such as originality and emotional resonance.", "That machines have consciousness.", "That art has no value.", "That humans cannot create."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A What humans value in art, such as originality and emotional resonance.，文章结尾说争论迫使我们阐明在艺术中看重什么：原创性、情感共鸣等；B That machines have consciousness.，与文章内容不符；C That art has no value.，与文章内容不符；D That humans cannot create.，与文章内容不符。"}]}, {"title": "Water Scarcity in a Thirsty World", "text": "Water, the most essential resource for life, is becoming scarcer for billions of people as climate change, population growth, and unsustainable consumption converge. Agriculture consumes roughly seventy percent of freshwater withdrawals, much of it inefficiently, while cities expand and industries demand ever more water. Aquifers that took thousands of years to fill are being depleted in decades, and rivers that once reached the sea now run dry before they arrive. Climate change intensifies the problem by altering rainfall patterns, making wet regions wetter and dry regions drier, and by melting glaciers that supply water to millions during dry seasons. The consequences are already severe: crop failures, conflicts over shared rivers, and the displacement of communities whose wells have run dry. Yet water scarcity is not merely a matter of physical shortage; it is also a problem of governance. In many places, water is priced far below its true value, encouraging waste, while powerful users, such as large farms and industries, secure privileged access at the expense of smallholders and the poor. Solutions require both technology and politics: drip irrigation and wastewater recycling can stretch supplies, but they must be accompanied by fair allocation, metering, and protection of ecosystems that store and purify water. Some experts advocate treating water as a human right rather than a commodity, while others argue for market mechanisms that reflect scarcity. What is clear is that business as usual is untenable. Societies that learn to value, measure, and share water wisely will be far better prepared for a drier future than those that continue to treat it as an inexhaustible gift of nature. Farmers who adopt water-saving techniques often discover that they can grow more with less, protecting both their incomes and the aquifers beneath their land. Sharing such success stories can persuade neighbors to change faster than regulations alone ever could.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What share of freshwater does agriculture consume?", "options": ["About ten percent.", "Almost none.", "Roughly seventy percent of freshwater withdrawals.", "Nearly all of it."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A About ten percent.，与文章内容不符；B Almost none.，与文章内容不符；C Roughly seventy percent of freshwater withdrawals.，文章指出农业消耗约70%的淡水取水量；D Nearly all of it.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "How does climate change worsen scarcity?", "options": ["By making all regions wetter.", "By increasing rainfall everywhere.", "By refilling aquifers quickly.", "By altering rainfall and melting glaciers that supply dry-season water."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A By making all regions wetter.，与文章内容不符；B By increasing rainfall everywhere.，与文章内容不符；C By refilling aquifers quickly.，与文章内容不符；D By altering rainfall and melting glaciers that supply dry-season water.，文章说气候变化改变降雨模式并融化为旱季供水的冰川。"}, {"prompt": "Why is scarcity also a governance problem?", "options": ["Water is often priced below its value and powerful users get privileged access.", "There is no water at all.", "Governments never manage water.", "Pricing is always correct."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Water is often priced below its value and powerful users get privileged access.，文章指出水价常远低于真实价值，强势用户获得特权接入；B There is no water at all.，与文章内容不符；C Governments never manage water.，与文章内容不符；D Pricing is always correct.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What technological solutions are mentioned?", "options": ["Building more dams only.", "Drip irrigation and wastewater recycling.", "Desalination is banned.", "Using more water."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Building more dams only.，与文章内容不符；B Drip irrigation and wastewater recycling.，文章提到滴灌与废水回收可延长供水；C Desalination is banned.，与文章内容不符；D Using more water.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main conclusion?", "options": ["Water is inexhaustible.", "Scarcity will solve itself.", "Business as usual is untenable; societies must value, measure, and share water wisely.", "Only technology matters."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Water is inexhaustible.，与文章内容不符；B Scarcity will solve itself.，与文章内容不符；C Business as usual is untenable; societies must value, measure, and share water wisely.，文章结论是照旧行事不可持续，社会必须明智地珍视、计量与分享水资源；D Only technology matters.，与文章内容不符。"}]}]}, {"no": 20, "passages": [{"title": "The Tyranny of Standardized Testing", "text": "Standardized tests have long been defended as objective, efficient tools for measuring student achievement and holding schools accountable. A single score, supporters argue, allows fair comparisons across schools and regions and identifies students who need extra help. Yet a growing chorus of educators, psychologists, and students argues that high-stakes testing distorts education in profound ways. When funding, teacher evaluations, and university admissions hinge on test scores, schools inevitably narrow their curricula, devoting disproportionate time to tested subjects and to test-taking strategies, while art, music, physical education, and open-ended inquiry are squeezed out. Students, meanwhile, learn to equate learning with memorizing the right answers, and anxiety about a single exam can overwhelm years of steady work. Research also raises questions about fairness: test scores correlate strongly with family income, reflecting differences in tutoring, test preparation, and home resources rather than innate ability alone. Critics do not deny that assessment matters; rather, they advocate for richer measures, such as portfolios, projects, and teacher evaluations, that capture creativity, collaboration, and persistence. Some countries and universities have moved in this direction, reducing the weight of single exams or making them optional. Defenders of testing counter that alternative assessments are more subjective and harder to compare, and that abandoning standardized measures could hide inequalities rather than reveal them. The debate, therefore, is not between assessment and no assessment, but about what kind of assessment best serves learning. A system that measures only what is easy to test, critics warn, will teach students that only what is tested matters, a lesson that impoverishes education far beyond the examination hall. Teachers who are trusted as professionals, rather than judged by a single score, are more likely to experiment, inspire curiosity, and adapt lessons to their students. Assessment should therefore support teachers' judgment instead of replacing it with a number.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What do supporters claim about standardized tests?", "options": ["They allow fair comparisons and identify students needing help.", "They measure creativity perfectly.", "They eliminate all anxiety.", "They replace teachers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A They allow fair comparisons and identify students needing help.，文章说支持者认为统一分数可公平比较学校并识别需要帮助的学生；B They measure creativity perfectly.，与文章内容不符；C They eliminate all anxiety.，与文章内容不符；D They replace teachers.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "How does high-stakes testing distort curricula?", "options": ["Schools expand art and music.", "Schools narrow curricula to tested subjects and test strategies.", "Schools ignore test scores.", "Curricula become broader."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Schools expand art and music.，与文章内容不符；B Schools narrow curricula to tested subjects and test strategies.，文章指出学校压缩课程，集中于应试科目与应试策略，挤占艺术音乐等；C Schools ignore test scores.，与文章内容不符；D Curricula become broader.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What fairness concern is raised?", "options": ["Scores are unrelated to income.", "All students have equal tutoring.", "Scores correlate strongly with family income, reflecting tutoring and resources.", "Tests favor the poor."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Scores are unrelated to income.，与文章内容不符；B All students have equal tutoring.，与文章内容不符；C Scores correlate strongly with family income, reflecting tutoring and resources.，文章说分数与家庭收入高度相关，反映补习与家庭资源差异；D Tests favor the poor.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What alternatives do critics advocate?", "options": ["No assessment at all.", "Only multiple-choice tests.", "More high-stakes exams.", "Portfolios, projects, and teacher evaluations."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A No assessment at all.，与文章内容不符；B Only multiple-choice tests.，与文章内容不符；C More high-stakes exams.，与文章内容不符；D Portfolios, projects, and teacher evaluations.，文章说批评者主张作品集、项目与教师评价等更丰富的评估。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's warning?", "options": ["A system measuring only what is easy to test teaches students only tested things matter.", "Testing is perfect.", "Assessment should be abolished.", "Students love exams."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A A system measuring only what is easy to test teaches students only tested things matter.，文章警告只测易测内容会让学生以为只有被考的才重要；B Testing is perfect.，与文章内容不符；C Assessment should be abolished.，与文章内容不符；D Students love exams.，与文章内容不符。"}]}, {"title": "Overtourism: When Visitors Become a Burden", "text": "Tourism is often celebrated as a driver of economic growth, creating jobs and funding the preservation of historic sites. In recent years, however, a growing number of destinations have begun to describe visitors not as guests but as a burden, a phenomenon known as overtourism. Venice, Barcelona, and Kyoto have all experienced the symptoms: streets crowded beyond capacity, housing converted to short-term rentals that price out residents, and fragile heritage sites damaged by sheer numbers. The causes are familiar: cheap flights, social media that turns quiet villages into overnight sensations, and cruise ships that disgorge thousands of passengers for a few hours. The benefits of tourism, moreover, are unevenly distributed. Large hotel chains and platform companies capture much of the revenue, while local residents bear the costs of congestion, noise, and rising prices. Some cities have responded with entry fees, caps on visitor numbers, and restrictions on short-term rentals, measures that have provoked debate about who has the right to travel and who decides. Others promote dispersal, encouraging visitors to explore lesser-known areas and to travel in off-peak seasons. Critics of restrictions argue that tourism provides livelihoods for millions and that limiting it punishes ordinary travelers for problems created by poor planning. Supporters counter that unmanaged growth destroys the very qualities that attracted visitors in the first place, and that a destination that becomes unlivable for residents will eventually lose its appeal. The challenge, therefore, is to find a balance: tourism that supports local economies without overwhelming the communities and environments that make a place worth visiting, a balance that requires planning, regulation, and a willingness to say that more visitors is not always better. Residents who benefit directly from tourism, through local guiding, crafts, and family-run guesthouses, are more likely to welcome visitors warmly. Spreading income more fairly may therefore be as important as limiting numbers in keeping tourism sustainable.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is overtourism?", "options": ["When no tourists visit.", "When tourism creates no jobs.", "When visitor numbers overwhelm destinations and burden residents.", "When visitors stay too briefly to spend."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A When no tourists visit.，与文章内容不符；B When tourism creates no jobs.，与文章内容不符；C When visitor numbers overwhelm destinations and burden residents.，文章定义过度旅游为游客数量压垮目的地、成为居民负担；D When visitors stay too briefly to spend.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What causes overtourism?", "options": ["Expensive flights only.", "Lack of social media.", "No hotels.", "Cheap flights, social media fame, and cruise ships."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Expensive flights only.，与文章内容不符；B Lack of social media.，与文章内容不符；C No hotels.，与文章内容不符；D Cheap flights, social media fame, and cruise ships.，文章列举廉价航班、社交媒体使宁静村庄一夜成名、邮轮大量送客。"}, {"prompt": "Who captures much tourism revenue?", "options": ["Large hotel chains and platform companies.", "Local residents equally.", "Small farmers only.", "No one profits."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Large hotel chains and platform companies.，文章说大型连锁酒店与平台公司获取大部分收入，而居民承担成本；B Local residents equally.，与文章内容不符；C Small farmers only.，与文章内容不符；D No one profits.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What measures have some cities taken?", "options": ["Banning all travel.", "Entry fees, visitor caps, and rental restrictions.", "Building more cruise ports.", "Ignoring the problem."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Banning all travel.，与文章内容不符；B Entry fees, visitor caps, and rental restrictions.，文章提到一些城市采取入场费、游客上限与短租限制；C Building more cruise ports.，与文章内容不符；D Ignoring the problem.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What balance does the author call for?", "options": ["Unlimited growth.", "Ending all tourism.", "Tourism supporting economies without overwhelming communities and environments.", "More visitors always better."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Unlimited growth.，与文章内容不符；B Ending all tourism.，与文章内容不符；C Tourism supporting economies without overwhelming communities and environments.，文章呼吁旅游支持经济又不压垮社区与环境，更多游客并非总更好；D More visitors always better.，与文章内容不符。"}]}]}, {"no": 21, "passages": [{"title": "Burnout in the Modern Workplace", "text": "Burnout, once dismissed as a personal failure to cope, is now recognized by the World Health Organization as an occupational phenomenon resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. Its symptoms are familiar to millions: emotional exhaustion, cynicism toward one's job, and a declining sense of professional efficacy. Unlike ordinary tiredness, burnout does not disappear after a weekend off; it accumulates over months of excessive demands, insufficient control, and a lack of recognition or support. The causes are primarily organizational rather than individual. Workplaces that reward constant availability, set unrealistic targets, and offer little autonomy create the conditions for burnout, regardless of employees' resilience. The COVID-19 pandemic intensified the problem by blurring boundaries between home and work and by increasing workloads in healthcare, education, and logistics. The consequences are costly for both workers and employers: higher rates of depression and cardiovascular disease, increased absenteeism, and the loss of experienced staff who quit or quietly disengage. Individual coping strategies, such as mindfulness and exercise, can help but cannot fix a broken work design. Effective responses require structural change: realistic workloads, clear expectations, opportunities for recovery, and managers trained to recognize warning signs. Some countries have begun to legislate the right to disconnect, while some companies experiment with four-day weeks and meeting-free days. The lesson is that burnout is not a sign of weakness but a signal that the way work is organized has become unsustainable, and that addressing it benefits not only employees' health but also the long-term productivity of organizations themselves. Regular recovery, including genuine holidays and time away from screens, should be treated as part of work, not as a reward for surviving it. Organizations that protect rest often find that their people return with more energy, focus, and willingness to tackle difficult problems. A rested workforce is not a luxury; it is the foundation of sustainable performance.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How does the WHO define burnout?", "options": ["An occupational phenomenon from chronic workplace stress not successfully managed.", "A personal failure to cope.", "Ordinary tiredness.", "A rare medical disease."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A An occupational phenomenon from chronic workplace stress not successfully managed.，文章指出世卫组织将职业倦怠定义为长期职场压力未得到成功管理导致的职业现象；B A personal failure to cope.，与文章内容不符；C Ordinary tiredness.，与文章内容不符；D A rare medical disease.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What distinguishes burnout from ordinary tiredness?", "options": ["It disappears quickly.", "It affects only managers.", "It accumulates over months and does not disappear after a weekend off.", "It is caused by laziness."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A It disappears quickly.，与文章内容不符；B It affects only managers.，与文章内容不符；C It accumulates over months and does not disappear after a weekend off.，文章说倦怠与普通疲劳不同，数月积累、周末休息无法消除；D It is caused by laziness.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main cause of burnout?", "options": ["Employees' lack of resilience.", "Too much vacation.", "Low salaries only.", "Organizational factors such as excessive demands and little autonomy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Employees' lack of resilience.，与文章内容不符；B Too much vacation.，与文章内容不符；C Low salaries only.，与文章内容不符；D Organizational factors such as excessive demands and little autonomy.，文章强调倦怠主因是组织因素：过度要求、缺乏控制与认可。"}, {"prompt": "What structural response is mentioned?", "options": ["More mindfulness only.", "Longer working hours.", "Ignoring warning signs.", "Realistic workloads, clear expectations, and opportunities for recovery."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A More mindfulness only.，与文章内容不符；B Longer working hours.，与文章内容不符；C Ignoring warning signs.，与文章内容不符；D Realistic workloads, clear expectations, and opportunities for recovery.，文章说有效应对需要结构性改变：合理工作量、明确期望与恢复机会。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's view of burnout?", "options": ["It is a signal that work organization has become unsustainable.", "It shows personal weakness.", "It is unimportant.", "It only affects the lazy."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A It is a signal that work organization has become unsustainable.，文章结论是倦怠不是软弱的标志，而是工作组织方式不可持续的信号；B It shows personal weakness.，与文章内容不符；C It is unimportant.，与文章内容不符；D It only affects the lazy.，与文章内容不符。"}]}, {"title": "Democracy and the Power of Voting", "text": "Voting is often described as the cornerstone of democracy, the mechanism by which citizens choose their leaders and hold them accountable. Yet in many democracies, turnout is declining, particularly among young people, raising questions about the health of representative government. Some citizens abstain because they believe their vote does not matter, others because they feel alienated from parties that seem indistinguishable, and still others because practical barriers, such as registration requirements, long queues, and voting on a workday, make participation difficult. Research suggests that these explanations interact: when people feel that politics does not address their daily concerns, they are less motivated to overcome even modest obstacles. The consequences of low turnout are not neutral. Older, wealthier, and better-educated citizens vote at higher rates, so their preferences weigh more heavily in policy, while the interests of younger and poorer citizens receive less attention, creating a cycle of neglect and disengagement. Proposals to increase participation range from automatic registration and weekend voting to compulsory voting, as practiced in Australia, and to lowering the voting age. Critics of compulsory voting argue that forcing citizens to vote violates freedom, while supporters contend that it treats voting as a civic duty akin to jury service. Technology offers additional possibilities, such as online voting, but raises concerns about security and trust. Ultimately, the health of democracy depends not only on the right to vote but on citizens' belief that voting can make a difference, a belief that is strengthened when governments respond visibly to public concerns and when political debate offers genuine choices rather than empty slogans. Civic education in schools can help by showing young people how local decisions affect their daily lives, from bus routes to parks. When voting feels connected to tangible outcomes, the habit of participation is far more likely to take root and last.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why do some citizens abstain from voting?", "options": ["They are all satisfied.", "Voting is too easy.", "They believe their vote does not matter or feel alienated from parties.", "They prefer dictatorship."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A They are all satisfied.，与文章内容不符；B Voting is too easy.，与文章内容不符；C They believe their vote does not matter or feel alienated from parties.，文章说一些公民弃权是因为认为投票无关紧要或对政党感到疏离；D They prefer dictatorship.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What practical barriers are mentioned?", "options": ["Too many polling stations.", "Free transport to polls.", "Online voting everywhere.", "Registration requirements, long queues, and voting on a workday."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Too many polling stations.，与文章内容不符；B Free transport to polls.，与文章内容不符；C Online voting everywhere.，与文章内容不符；D Registration requirements, long queues, and voting on a workday.，文章提到登记要求、长队与工作日投票等实际障碍。"}, {"prompt": "What is a consequence of low turnout?", "options": ["Older, wealthier citizens' preferences weigh more heavily in policy.", "All groups are equally represented.", "Young people's interests dominate.", "Policy becomes fairer."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Older, wealthier citizens' preferences weigh more heavily in policy.，文章指出年长、富裕、受教育程度高的公民投票率更高，其偏好在政策中权重更大；B All groups are equally represented.，与文章内容不符；C Young people's interests dominate.，与文章内容不符；D Policy becomes fairer.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is compulsory voting compared to?", "options": ["A tax on the poor.", "A military draft.", "A civic duty akin to jury service.", "A school exam."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A A tax on the poor.，与文章内容不符；B A military draft.，与文章内容不符；C A civic duty akin to jury service.，文章说支持者认为强制投票如同陪审义务，是一种公民责任；D A school exam.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What strengthens belief that voting matters?", "options": ["Empty slogans.", "Ignoring public concerns.", "Fewer elections.", "Governments responding visibly to public concerns and genuine political choices."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Empty slogans.，与文章内容不符；B Ignoring public concerns.，与文章内容不符；C Fewer elections.，与文章内容不符；D Governments responding visibly to public concerns and genuine political choices.，文章结尾说政府 visibly 回应公众关切、政治辩论提供真正选择时，信念会增强。"}]}]}, {"no": 22, "passages": [{"title": "Processed Food and Public Health", "text": "The modern diet, dominated by ultra-processed foods, has become one of the leading drivers of obesity, diabetes, and cardiovascular disease worldwide. Ultra-processed products, from sugary drinks and instant noodles to packaged snacks and ready meals, are engineered to be cheap, convenient, and irresistibly tasty, combining high levels of sugar, salt, and fat with additives that extend shelf life. Their rise reflects deep changes in how societies eat: longer working hours, the decline of home cooking, and aggressive marketing, especially to children. The health effects are well documented. Diets high in ultra-processed foods are associated with weight gain, even when total calories are controlled, partly because these foods are less satiating and easier to overeat. They also displace healthier options, such as vegetables, whole grains, and legumes, from the diet. The burden falls unevenly: low-income neighborhoods often have limited access to fresh food but abundant fast-food outlets, a pattern known as a food desert. Policy responses are contested. Some countries have introduced taxes on sugary drinks, front-of-pack warning labels, and restrictions on advertising to children, measures that evidence suggests can shift consumption. The food industry argues that consumers should be free to choose and that education, not regulation, is the answer. Public health experts counter that the playing field is tilted by marketing budgets and by the addictive qualities of engineered foods, and that individual willpower alone cannot counter an environment designed to encourage overconsumption. The most effective strategies, they argue, combine regulation with improved access to healthy food, cooking education, and urban planning that makes fresh markets as convenient as fast-food outlets, recognizing that diet is shaped not only by personal choices but by the food environment in which those choices are made. Cooking skills, once passed down at home, may need to be taught in schools and community kitchens so that healthy meals feel achievable rather than intimidating. A population that knows how to cook simple, affordable food is less dependent on the products that harm its health.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What characterizes ultra-processed foods?", "options": ["Cheap, convenient, tasty, high in sugar, salt, and fat.", "Expensive and hard to find.", "Rich in vegetables.", "Unprocessed and natural."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Cheap, convenient, tasty, high in sugar, salt, and fat.，文章指出超加工食品廉价、方便、美味，含高糖高盐高脂与添加剂；B Expensive and hard to find.，与文章内容不符；C Rich in vegetables.，与文章内容不符；D Unprocessed and natural.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Why are ultra-processed foods linked to weight gain?", "options": ["They contain no calories.", "They are hard to digest.", "They are less satiating and easier to overeat.", "They replace all drinks."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A They contain no calories.，与文章内容不符；B They are hard to digest.，与文章内容不符；C They are less satiating and easier to overeat.，文章说这类食物饱腹感较差、容易过量食用，即使控制总热量也与增重相关；D They replace all drinks.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is a “food desert”?", "options": ["A desert with no people.", "A place with only vegetables.", "A farm in a desert.", "An area with limited fresh food but abundant fast food."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A A desert with no people.，与文章内容不符；B A place with only vegetables.，与文章内容不符；C A farm in a desert.，与文章内容不符；D An area with limited fresh food but abundant fast food.，文章说低收入社区常缺乏新鲜食物、快餐店众多，即食物沙漠。"}, {"prompt": "What policy measures are mentioned?", "options": ["Banning all food.", "Free sugary drinks.", "No labels at all.", "Taxes on sugary drinks, warning labels, and advertising restrictions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Banning all food.，与文章内容不符；B Free sugary drinks.，与文章内容不符；C No labels at all.，与文章内容不符；D Taxes on sugary drinks, warning labels, and advertising restrictions.，文章提到含糖饮料税、包装正面警示标签与限制向儿童做广告。"}, {"prompt": "What do public health experts argue?", "options": ["Individual willpower alone cannot counter an environment designed to encourage overconsumption.", "Education alone is enough.", "Marketing has no effect.", "Regulation never works."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Individual willpower alone cannot counter an environment designed to encourage overconsumption.，文章说公共卫生专家认为仅靠个人意志无法对抗被设计来鼓励过量消费的环境；B Education alone is enough.，与文章内容不符；C Marketing has no effect.，与文章内容不符；D Regulation never works.，与文章内容不符。"}]}, {"title": "Cybersecurity in an Interconnected World", "text": "As daily life migrates online, from banking and healthcare to education and government services, cybersecurity has become a matter of national security and personal safety. Cyberattacks, once the domain of hobbyist hackers, are now carried out by organized criminal groups and state-sponsored actors seeking financial gain, espionage, or disruption. Ransomware attacks can paralyze hospitals, schools, and pipelines, while data breaches expose the personal information of millions. The scale of the threat is amplified by the Internet of Things: billions of connected devices, from smart thermostats to industrial sensors, expand the surface that attackers can exploit, often with weak default passwords and infrequent updates. Defending against these threats is complicated by the asymmetry between attackers and defenders. An attacker needs to find a single vulnerability, while defenders must secure every entry point, a task made harder by the complexity of modern software and the shortage of trained cybersecurity professionals. Individuals, too, play a role: weak passwords, reused credentials, and falling for phishing emails remain among the most common causes of breaches. Experts argue that cybersecurity cannot be solved by technology alone; it requires a combination of secure design, regular updates, employee training, and international cooperation, since attackers operate across borders. Governments are increasingly treating critical infrastructure as a strategic asset and imposing security standards on operators. Yet the tension between security and convenience persists: measures that make systems safer, such as multi-factor authentication, can also frustrate users. The challenge is to build a digital world that is both open and resilient, where the benefits of connectivity are not undermined by the constant risk that the systems we depend on can be compromised. Schools should teach basic digital hygiene alongside reading and mathematics, so that the next generation treats strong passwords and careful clicking as ordinary habits. Security learned early becomes second nature, reducing the risks that no firewall alone can eliminate.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Who carries out modern cyberattacks?", "options": ["Organized criminal groups and state-sponsored actors.", "Only hobbyist hackers.", "Only children.", "No one."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Organized criminal groups and state-sponsored actors.，文章指出网络攻击现由有组织犯罪集团与国家支持的行为者实施；B Only hobbyist hackers.，与文章内容不符；C Only children.，与文章内容不符；D No one.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What do ransomware attacks do?", "options": ["They improve security.", "They protect data.", "They can paralyze hospitals, schools, and pipelines.", "They speed up systems."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A They improve security.，与文章内容不符；B They protect data.，与文章内容不符；C They can paralyze hospitals, schools, and pipelines.，文章说勒索软件攻击可使医院、学校与管道瘫痪；D They speed up systems.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Why does the Internet of Things increase risk?", "options": ["Devices are never connected.", "All devices are secure.", "It reduces connectivity.", "Billions of devices expand the attack surface, often with weak passwords."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Devices are never connected.，与文章内容不符；B All devices are secure.，与文章内容不符；C It reduces connectivity.，与文章内容不符；D Billions of devices expand the attack surface, often with weak passwords.，文章指出数十亿联网设备扩大攻击面，常使用弱默认密码且很少更新。"}, {"prompt": "What asymmetry favors attackers?", "options": ["Defenders need one flaw.", "Attackers must secure everything.", "Both have equal tasks.", "Attackers need one vulnerability; defenders must secure every entry point."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Defenders need one flaw.，与文章内容不符；B Attackers must secure everything.，与文章内容不符；C Both have equal tasks.，与文章内容不符；D Attackers need one vulnerability; defenders must secure every entry point.，文章说攻击者只需找到一个漏洞，防御者必须守住每个入口。"}, {"prompt": "What is needed besides technology?", "options": ["Secure design, updates, training, and international cooperation.", "Technology alone.", "No training.", "Ignoring users."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Secure design, updates, training, and international cooperation.，文章强调网络安全不能仅靠技术，还需安全设计、更新、培训与国际合作；B Technology alone.，与文章内容不符；C No training.，与文章内容不符；D Ignoring users.，与文章内容不符。"}]}]}, {"no": 23, "passages": [{"title": "Cities of Many Cultures", "text": "Migration has made many of the world's great cities profoundly multicultural, places where dozens of languages are spoken, cuisines from every continent are served, and festivals from different traditions fill the calendar. This diversity is often celebrated as a source of creativity, economic dynamism, and cultural richness. Immigrants bring skills, entrepreneurial energy, and new perspectives, while their children, growing up between cultures, often become bridges between communities. Yet multiculturalism also presents challenges that cities cannot ignore. Newcomers may face discrimination in housing and employment, language barriers that limit access to services, and the painful process of adapting to unfamiliar norms while preserving their own identities. Host communities, meanwhile, may feel that rapid change threatens social cohesion, particularly when integration is left to chance rather than supported by policy. Research suggests that diversity itself is neither inherently beneficial nor harmful; what matters is how it is managed. Cities that invest in language classes, anti-discrimination enforcement, mixed neighborhoods, and shared public spaces tend to build trust across groups, while cities that allow segregation and neglect tend to experience tension. Schools play a crucial role, as classrooms where children of different backgrounds learn together foster friendships that can last a lifetime. The most successful multicultural cities, scholars argue, do not demand assimilation into a single identity, nor do they accept parallel lives with no interaction. Instead, they cultivate a shared civic identity, a sense of belonging to the same city and its future, while respecting the distinct traditions that make each community unique, a balance that is difficult to achieve but essential for urban life in an age of movement. Festivals that invite everyone to share food, music, and stories can turn diversity from an abstract idea into a lived experience. When neighbors celebrate together, differences become sources of curiosity rather than fear, and a city feels like home to more of its people.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What benefits does migration bring to cities?", "options": ["Skills, entrepreneurial energy, and new perspectives.", "Only problems.", "No economic benefit.", "Less creativity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A Skills, entrepreneurial energy, and new perspectives.，文章指出移民带来技能、创业活力与新视角；B Only problems.，与文章内容不符；C No economic benefit.，与文章内容不符；D Less creativity.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What challenges do newcomers face?", "options": ["No challenges at all.", "Only language benefits.", "Discrimination, language barriers, and adapting while preserving identity.", "Instant integration."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A No challenges at all.，与文章内容不符；B Only language benefits.，与文章内容不符；C Discrimination, language barriers, and adapting while preserving identity.，文章说新移民面临住房就业歧视、语言障碍与适应新规范同时保留自身认同的痛苦过程；D Instant integration.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What does research say about diversity?", "options": ["Diversity is always harmful.", "Diversity is always beneficial.", "Management is irrelevant.", "What matters is how diversity is managed, not diversity itself."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Diversity is always harmful.，与文章内容不符；B Diversity is always beneficial.，与文章内容不符；C Management is irrelevant.，与文章内容不符；D What matters is how diversity is managed, not diversity itself.，文章指出多样性本身既非天生有益也非有害，关键在于如何管理。"}, {"prompt": "What helps build trust across groups?", "options": ["Segregation and neglect.", "Banning festivals.", "Closing schools.", "Language classes, anti-discrimination enforcement, and shared public spaces."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Segregation and neglect.，与文章内容不符；B Banning festivals.，与文章内容不符；C Closing schools.，与文章内容不符；D Language classes, anti-discrimination enforcement, and shared public spaces.，文章说投资语言课程、反歧视执法、混合社区与共享公共空间有助于建立跨群体信任。"}, {"prompt": "What do successful multicultural cities cultivate?", "options": ["A shared civic identity while respecting distinct traditions.", "Forced assimilation only.", "Parallel lives with no interaction.", "A single identity for all."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A A shared civic identity while respecting distinct traditions.，文章说最成功的多元文化城市培养共同的公民认同，同时尊重各自传统；B Forced assimilation only.，与文章内容不符；C Parallel lives with no interaction.，与文章内容不符；D A single identity for all.，与文章内容不符。"}]}, {"title": "Learning from Failure", "text": "Failure is universally feared and almost universally experienced, yet societies differ profoundly in how they treat it. In some cultures, a failed business or a rejected application is seen as a stigma, a mark of incompetence that follows a person for years. In others, notably in parts of the technology industry, failure is reframed as a necessary step toward success, summarized in slogans such as fail fast and learn fast. Research in psychology supports the second view, with important qualifications. When people reflect on their failures, identify what went wrong, and adjust their strategies, they often perform better in subsequent attempts than those who succeed on the first try. This learning effect, however, depends on the environment. In organizations where mistakes are punished, employees hide errors and repeat them; in organizations with psychological safety, where admitting a mistake is treated as information rather than a crime, teams learn faster and innovate more. The distinction matters in education as well. Students who are taught that intelligence is fixed tend to avoid challenging tasks for fear of failure, while those who believe abilities can grow through effort are more willing to take risks and persist after setbacks. Yet celebrating failure uncritically can also be misleading. Not all failures are instructive, and repeated failure without reflection or support can damage confidence and waste resources. The most productive approach, experts suggest, is neither to fear failure nor to romanticize it, but to treat it as data: to ask what the failure reveals, what could be changed, and whether the goal itself remains worth pursuing, turning an experience that feels like an ending into the beginning of a better attempt. Parents can model this attitude by talking openly about their own mistakes and what they learned, showing children that setbacks are normal. A household where failure is discussed honestly raises children who are braver about trying difficult things.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How is failure viewed in some cultures?", "options": ["As a stigma marking incompetence for years.", "As a celebration always.", "As irrelevant.", "As a guarantee of success."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A As a stigma marking incompetence for years.，文章说在一些文化中失败被视为耻辱、多年跟随个人的无能标志；B As a celebration always.，与文章内容不符；C As irrelevant.，与文章内容不符；D As a guarantee of success.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What does psychology research show?", "options": ["Failure never helps.", "Success on first try is always best.", "Reflecting on failure and adjusting strategies often improves later performance.", "Reflection is useless."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Failure never helps.，与文章内容不符；B Success on first try is always best.，与文章内容不符；C Reflecting on failure and adjusting strategies often improves later performance.，文章指出反思失败、找出问题并调整策略的人常在后续尝试中表现更好；D Reflection is useless.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is “psychological safety”?", "options": ["Punishing all mistakes.", "Hiding all errors.", "Avoiding all risks.", "An environment where admitting mistakes is treated as information, not a crime."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Punishing all mistakes.，与文章内容不符；B Hiding all errors.，与文章内容不符；C Avoiding all risks.，与文章内容不符；D An environment where admitting mistakes is treated as information, not a crime.，文章说心理安全指承认错误被视为信息而非罪行，团队学习更快。"}, {"prompt": "How do students with a growth mindset respond to failure?", "options": ["They are more willing to take risks and persist after setbacks.", "They avoid all challenges.", "They believe intelligence is fixed.", "They give up immediately."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A They are more willing to take risks and persist after setbacks.，文章说相信能力可通过努力增长的学生更愿意冒险、受挫后坚持；B They avoid all challenges.，与文章内容不符；C They believe intelligence is fixed.，与文章内容不符；D They give up immediately.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What approach do experts recommend?", "options": ["Fear failure always.", "Romanticize all failure.", "Treat failure as data: ask what it reveals and what could change.", "Ignore failure completely."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Fear failure always.，与文章内容不符；B Romanticize all failure.，与文章内容不符；C Treat failure as data: ask what it reveals and what could change.，文章建议既不惧怕也不浪漫化失败，而是把它当作数据来分析；D Ignore failure completely.，与文章内容不符。"}]}]}, {"no": 24, "passages": [{"title": "The Ocean's Plastic Burden", "text": "Every year, an estimated eight million tonnes of plastic enter the world's oceans, a flow that, if unchecked, will soon mean more plastic than fish by weight. The sources are diverse: rivers carry waste from inland cities, fishing nets are lost or discarded, and microplastics shed from clothing and tires wash into waterways. Once in the sea, plastic does not biodegrade; it fragments into ever smaller pieces that are ingested by seabirds, turtles, and fish, causing injury, starvation, and death. Microplastics have now been found in the deepest ocean trenches, in Arctic ice, and in the seafood and drinking water consumed by humans, although the health effects of this exposure remain uncertain. The economic costs are also significant: plastic pollution damages tourism, fisheries, and shipping, and coastal communities spend heavily on cleanup. Solutions are often framed as a matter of individual behavior, such as using reusable bags and bottles, but experts argue that the scale of the problem requires systemic change. Much plastic is designed to be used once and discarded, and recycling rates remain low because virgin plastic is cheap and sorting is expensive. Policies such as bans on single-use items, extended producer responsibility, and deposit return schemes have shown promise in several countries. International cooperation is essential, since ocean currents carry waste across borders and no single nation can solve the problem alone. Negotiations for a global plastics treaty reflect growing recognition that the ocean's plastic burden is not an inevitable byproduct of modern life but a design failure, one that can be corrected by rethinking how plastic is produced, used, and disposed of. Designers can help by creating products that are durable, repairable, and easy to recycle, so that plastic serves a useful life instead of a single moment. When reuse becomes the default, the flow of waste into the sea begins to slow at its source.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How much plastic enters oceans each year?", "options": ["About eight million tonnes.", "Almost none.", "One thousand tonnes.", "One billion tonnes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A About eight million tonnes.，文章估计每年约八百万吨塑料进入海洋；B Almost none.，与文章内容不符；C One thousand tonnes.，与文章内容不符；D One billion tonnes.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What happens to plastic in the sea?", "options": ["It biodegrades quickly.", "It fragments into smaller pieces ingested by marine life.", "It disappears completely.", "It becomes harmless."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A It biodegrades quickly.，与文章内容不符；B It fragments into smaller pieces ingested by marine life.，文章说塑料在海中不降解，而是碎成越来越小的碎片，被海鸟、海龟与鱼类摄入；C It disappears completely.，与文章内容不符；D It becomes harmless.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Where have microplastics been found?", "options": ["Only in factories.", "Only on beaches.", "In deep trenches, Arctic ice, seafood, and drinking water.", "Nowhere."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Only in factories.，与文章内容不符；B Only on beaches.，与文章内容不符；C In deep trenches, Arctic ice, seafood, and drinking water.，文章指出微塑料已在最深海沟、北极冰、人类食用的海鲜与饮用水中发现；D Nowhere.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Why are recycling rates low?", "options": ["Recycling is banned.", "Plastic is never collected.", "Sorting is free.", "Virgin plastic is cheap and sorting is expensive."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Recycling is banned.，与文章内容不符；B Plastic is never collected.，与文章内容不符；C Sorting is free.，与文章内容不符；D Virgin plastic is cheap and sorting is expensive.，文章说回收率低是因为原生塑料便宜、分拣昂贵。"}, {"prompt": "What systemic policies are mentioned?", "options": ["Using more plastic.", "Ignoring producers.", "Banning all recycling.", "Bans on single-use items, producer responsibility, and deposit schemes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Using more plastic.，与文章内容不符；B Ignoring producers.，与文章内容不符；C Banning all recycling.，与文章内容不符；D Bans on single-use items, producer responsibility, and deposit schemes.，文章提到禁用一次性用品、生产者责任延伸与押金返还制度等政策。"}]}, {"title": "What Makes a Good Leader?", "text": "Leadership is often associated with charisma, decisiveness, and vision, qualities that dominate popular biographies of successful executives and politicians. Research on effective leadership, however, paints a more nuanced picture. The most successful leaders, studies suggest, are not necessarily the loudest or most confident, but those who combine humility with determination, listen carefully, and create conditions in which others can do their best work. This style, sometimes called servant leadership, emphasizes empowering team members, sharing credit, and taking responsibility for failures. It contrasts with authoritarian leadership, which may produce short-term compliance but often stifles creativity and drives talented people away. Emotional intelligence also matters: leaders who recognize their own emotions, manage stress, and empathize with others build trust and loyalty. Context matters too. A crisis may call for rapid, directive decisions, while a creative project may require patience and open-ended exploration. The best leaders adapt their style to the situation and to the needs of their team, rather than applying a single formula. Leadership can also be learned: training in communication, feedback, and conflict resolution improves performance, challenging the myth that leaders are simply born. Organizations that invest in developing leaders at all levels, not only at the top, tend to be more resilient, because decisions are better and employees feel valued. Ultimately, good leadership is less about personal glory than about enabling a group to achieve something none of its members could accomplish alone, a definition that applies as much to a classroom or a community project as to a corporation or a country. Mentoring programs that pair experienced leaders with newcomers can pass on this wisdom more effectively than any textbook. Leadership grows best through guided practice, honest feedback, and the chance to learn from real decisions. The best leaders are remembered not for their titles but for the people they helped grow.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What qualities do popular biographies emphasize?", "options": ["Humility only.", "Silence only.", "Charisma, decisiveness, and vision.", "Avoiding decisions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Humility only.，与文章内容不符；B Silence only.，与文章内容不符；C Charisma, decisiveness, and vision.，文章说流行传记常强调魅力、果断与远见；D Avoiding decisions.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What is “servant leadership”?", "options": ["Ordering others around.", "Taking all credit.", "Blaming the team.", "Empowering team members, sharing credit, and taking responsibility for failures."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Ordering others around.，与文章内容不符；B Taking all credit.，与文章内容不符；C Blaming the team.，与文章内容不符；D Empowering team members, sharing credit, and taking responsibility for failures.，文章说服务型领导强调赋能团队成员、分享功劳、承担失败责任。"}, {"prompt": "What is a drawback of authoritarian leadership?", "options": ["It always inspires innovation.", "It builds lasting trust.", "It stifles creativity and drives talented people away.", "It empowers everyone."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A It always inspires innovation.，与文章内容不符；B It builds lasting trust.，与文章内容不符；C It stifles creativity and drives talented people away.，文章指出威权领导虽可带来短期服从，但常扼杀创造力并赶走人才；D It empowers everyone.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Why does emotional intelligence matter?", "options": ["It makes leaders louder.", "It eliminates all stress.", "It replaces all skills.", "Leaders who empathize build trust and loyalty."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A It makes leaders louder.，与文章内容不符；B It eliminates all stress.，与文章内容不符；C It replaces all skills.，与文章内容不符；D Leaders who empathize build trust and loyalty.，文章说情商高的领导者能识别自身情绪、管理压力、共情他人，从而建立信任与忠诚。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's definition of good leadership?", "options": ["Seeking personal glory.", "Making all decisions alone.", "Avoiding all responsibility.", "Enabling a group to achieve what no member could do alone."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Seeking personal glory.，与文章内容不符；B Making all decisions alone.，与文章内容不符；C Avoiding all responsibility.，与文章内容不符；D Enabling a group to achieve what no member could do alone.，文章结尾定义好领导是使团队实现任何成员单独无法完成的事。"}]}]}, {"no": 25, "passages": [{"title": "The Future of Reading in a Digital World", "text": "For centuries, the printed book was the dominant medium for deep, sustained reading, and its decline has been predicted many times, from the arrival of radio and television to the rise of the internet. Yet books have proved remarkably resilient, and the real change is subtler: people now read more than ever, but in shorter bursts, across screens, and often while doing other things. Research on reading comprehension suggests that the medium matters. Readers of printed texts tend to remember details and follow complex arguments better than readers of the same texts on screens, partly because screens encourage skimming and invite distractions from notifications and hyperlinks. E-books and audiobooks, however, have expanded access for people with visual impairments, busy commuters, and those who cannot afford or store many physical books. The challenge for educators is not to defend one medium against another but to cultivate deep reading as a skill that can transfer across formats. This involves teaching students to slow down, annotate, question, and revisit difficult passages, habits that are harder to sustain when algorithms reward speed and novelty. Libraries are adapting, offering both physical collections and digital resources, as well as quiet spaces for concentration in an increasingly noisy world. Some worry that the habit of deep reading is being lost, with consequences for empathy, critical thinking, and democratic deliberation, all of which depend on the ability to engage with sustained arguments. Others are more optimistic, noting that new forms, such as long-form journalism, serialized fiction, and interactive narratives, continue to attract dedicated readers. The future of reading, in this view, will not be determined by technology alone but by the value societies place on attention, reflection, and the willingness to spend time with a single text. Parents who read for pleasure themselves, even for a few minutes each day, set a powerful example for their children. A home where books are visible and reading is enjoyed teaches young people that attention is worth protecting.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What has happened to reading habits?", "options": ["People read more, but in shorter bursts across screens.", "People stopped reading entirely.", "Books have disappeared.", "No one reads online."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A People read more, but in shorter bursts across screens.，文章说人们阅读量更大，但以更短的片段、在屏幕上、常同时做其他事；B People stopped reading entirely.，与文章内容不符；C Books have disappeared.，与文章内容不符；D No one reads online.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What do studies show about print vs. screens?", "options": ["Screen readers always remember more.", "Print readers remember details and follow arguments better.", "Medium makes no difference.", "Print readers forget everything."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A Screen readers always remember more.，与文章内容不符；B Print readers remember details and follow arguments better.，文章指出纸质阅读者更能记住细节、跟随复杂论证，屏幕鼓励略读与分心；C Medium makes no difference.，与文章内容不符；D Print readers forget everything.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Who benefits from e-books and audiobooks?", "options": ["Only publishers.", "No one.", "People with visual impairments, commuters, and those who cannot store many books.", "Only children."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Only publishers.，与文章内容不符；B No one.，与文章内容不符；C People with visual impairments, commuters, and those who cannot store many books.，文章说电子书与有声书扩大了视障人士、通勤者与无力购买或存放大量纸书者的可及性；D Only children.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What should educators cultivate?", "options": ["Speed reading only.", "Avoiding all books.", "Banning screens.", "Deep reading as a skill transferable across formats."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Speed reading only.，与文章内容不符；B Avoiding all books.，与文章内容不符；C Banning screens.，与文章内容不符；D Deep reading as a skill transferable across formats.，文章说教育者的挑战是培养深度阅读这一可跨格式迁移的技能。"}, {"prompt": "What will determine the future of reading?", "options": ["Technology alone.", "The price of paper.", "The number of libraries.", "The value societies place on attention and reflection, not technology alone."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Technology alone.，与文章内容不符；B The price of paper.，与文章内容不符；C The number of libraries.，与文章内容不符；D The value societies place on attention and reflection, not technology alone.，文章结尾说阅读的未来不只由技术决定，而取决于社会对注意力与反思的重视。"}]}, {"title": "Cooperation Against the Next Pandemic", "text": "The COVID-19 pandemic revealed both the strengths and the weaknesses of global cooperation in the face of a shared threat. On one hand, scientists shared genomic data within days, vaccine development proceeded at unprecedented speed, and health workers across borders exchanged knowledge about treatment. On the other hand, competition for masks, ventilators, and vaccines exposed deep inequalities, with wealthy countries securing supplies while poorer nations waited months for doses. The lesson, many experts argue, is that pandemics are not merely medical emergencies but tests of global governance. The World Health Organization, the principal coordinating body, was criticized both for acting too slowly and for lacking the authority and funding to enforce its recommendations. Proposals for reform include a pandemic treaty that would require countries to share data, stockpile supplies, and distribute medical countermeasures equitably, as well as a global fund to strengthen health systems in low-income countries. Critics caution that treaties are only as strong as the political will behind them, and that national governments will always prioritize their own citizens in a crisis. Supporters respond that enlightened self-interest points the other way: in an interconnected world, a virus anywhere is a threat everywhere, and leaving reservoirs of infection unaddressed allows new variants to emerge. Building trust before a crisis, through transparent communication, joint exercises, and fair allocation rules agreed in advance, is far easier than negotiating under pressure. The next pandemic, scientists warn, is a matter of when, not if, and whether humanity responds with cooperation or with competition may determine how many lives are lost and how quickly societies recover. Investing in primary healthcare, clean water, and disease surveillance in every country is therefore not charity but shared protection. The world is only as safe as its least prepared health system, and strengthening those systems protects everyone. Preparedness is far cheaper, in lives and money, than responding after a crisis has begun.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What did scientists do quickly during COVID-19?", "options": ["They shared genomic data within days and developed vaccines fast.", "They hid all data.", "They stopped all research.", "They refused to cooperate."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A They shared genomic data within days and developed vaccines fast.，文章说科学家几天内分享基因组数据，疫苗研发速度前所未有；B They hid all data.，与文章内容不符；C They stopped all research.，与文章内容不符；D They refused to cooperate.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What inequality was exposed?", "options": ["All countries got equal supplies.", "Wealthy countries secured supplies while poorer nations waited months.", "Poor nations got vaccines first.", "No competition occurred."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A All countries got equal supplies.，与文章内容不符；B Wealthy countries secured supplies while poorer nations waited months.，文章指出富国获得口罩、呼吸机与疫苗，穷国等了数月；C Poor nations got vaccines first.，与文章内容不符；D No competition occurred.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "What reform is proposed?", "options": ["Abolishing the WHO.", "Banning all travel forever.", "A pandemic treaty requiring data sharing and equitable distribution.", "Ending all research."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A Abolishing the WHO.，与文章内容不符；B Banning all travel forever.，与文章内容不符；C A pandemic treaty requiring data sharing and equitable distribution.，文章提到大流行条约，要求各国分享数据、储备物资、公平分配医疗对策；D Ending all research.，与文章内容不符。"}, {"prompt": "Why do supporters favor cooperation?", "options": ["Cooperation is always easy.", "National interests never matter.", "Variants never emerge.", "A virus anywhere is a threat everywhere and unaddressed infection breeds variants."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Cooperation is always easy.，与文章内容不符；B National interests never matter.，与文章内容不符；C Variants never emerge.，与文章内容不符；D A virus anywhere is a threat everywhere and unaddressed infection breeds variants.，文章说支持者认为病毒无处不在即处处是威胁，未解决的感染会产生新变种。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's warning?", "options": ["Pandemics will never happen again.", "Cooperation is unnecessary.", "Trust is irrelevant.", "The next pandemic is a matter of when, not if."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A Pandemics will never happen again.，与文章内容不符；B Cooperation is unnecessary.，与文章内容不符；C Trust is irrelevant.，与文章内容不符；D The next pandemic is a matter of when, not if.，文章警告下一次大流行是何时而非是否会发生。"}]}]}, {"no": 26, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过度夸大，文章说AI是辅助而非替代；B 概括了利弊并存的主旨；C 缩小范围，与文意不符；D 与文中农村医院受益的例子相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["AI will soon replace doctors in most hospitals.", "AI in diagnosis offers great benefits but also raises serious ethical and practical concerns.", "Medical imaging is the only field where AI is useful.", "Rural hospitals should not use AI systems."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 文中未提医生拒绝使用；B 与训练数据反映不平等、导致少数群体表现较差一致；C 过于笼统且非原因；D 与文中效率优势相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Because doctors refuse to use the systems.", "Because training data may reflect existing inequalities.", "Because patients distrust all new technology.", "Because algorithms are too slow for clinical use."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why might AI diagnosis be less reliable for some groups?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 曲解了比喻含义；B 准确指出模型不解释推理过程；C 文中无秘密研究之意；D 与其高准确率矛盾。", "answer": "B", "options": ["They are physically difficult to install.", "Their reasoning process is not transparent.", "They are only used in secret research.", "They cannot process medical images."], "prompt": "What does the author imply by calling AI models black boxes?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 忽略了文中大量担忧；B 符合既肯定潜力又强调监督的立场；C 与支持在条件下使用相反；D 与全文深入讨论不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Entirely optimistic and uncritical.", "Cautiously supportive, stressing oversight and accountability.", "Completely opposed to any clinical use.", "Indifferent to its development."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward AI in medicine?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于极端，作者主张有条件使用；B 与多样数据和明确问责的要求一致；C 与强调监督相反；D 与把AI定位为第二意见相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Banning AI diagnosis until it is perfect.", "Requiring diverse training data and clear responsibility rules.", "Letting hospitals use any algorithm without review.", "Replacing radiologists with algorithms immediately."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author would most likely support which policy?"}], "text": "The application of artificial intelligence to medical diagnosis has moved rapidly from laboratory speculation to clinical practice, raising both enthusiasm and caution among physicians, policymakers, and patients. Machine learning systems trained on millions of medical images can now detect certain cancers, eye diseases, and lung conditions with accuracy that rivals, and in some narrow tasks exceeds, that of experienced specialists. Supporters argue that such tools could reduce diagnostic errors, shorten waiting times, and extend expert-level care to regions where specialists are scarce. In rural hospitals, for example, an algorithm might flag a suspicious scan for urgent review, allowing a single radiologist to serve a much larger population. Yet the technology also introduces profound uncertainties. Algorithms learn patterns from historical data, and if that data reflects existing inequalities in access to care, the system may perform less reliably for women, older patients, or minority groups. A model trained mainly on images from one population may misinterpret conditions in another, turning a promising innovation into a source of systematic disadvantage. Transparency presents another difficulty. Many advanced models operate as black boxes, producing a recommendation without explaining the reasoning behind it. When a diagnosis proves wrong, it is unclear whether responsibility lies with the software developer, the hospital, or the doctor who accepted the machine's judgment. Legal frameworks have not kept pace with this ambiguity, leaving clinicians in an uncertain position. Moreover, the introduction of AI may alter the doctor-patient relationship itself. If patients come to trust an algorithm more than a human physician, or if doctors become reluctant to challenge a machine's verdict, clinical judgment could gradually erode. The most thoughtful advocates therefore describe AI not as a replacement for doctors but as a second opinion, powerful yet fallible, whose value depends on careful oversight, diverse training data, and clear lines of accountability. Only under those conditions, they argue, can the technology fulfill its promise without undermining the trust on which medicine depends.", "title": "AI and the Future of Medical Diagnosis"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中隐藏成本的讨论相反；B 概括了利弊并存与需要有意管理的主旨；C 过于绝对，作者主张混合模式；D 与视频会议劣于面对面创意交流相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Remote work has no real disadvantages.", "Remote work brings clear benefits but also hidden costs that need deliberate management.", "All companies should return to the office full-time.", "Video meetings are superior to face-to-face discussion."], "prompt": "What is the author's main argument?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 文中未提培训缺失；B 与旁听、观察和随口提问消失一致；C 文中无此暗示；D 过于绝对且非原因。", "answer": "B", "options": ["They lack formal training programs.", "They miss informal, spontaneous learning in the office.", "They are less intelligent than senior staff.", "They refuse to ask questions online."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why do junior employees learn more slowly remotely?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与远程语境相反；B 与深夜回邮件、因不在线而内疚一致；C 是监控手段而非该现象；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Being physically present in the office.", "Feeling pressure to appear online even outside work hours.", "Using digital tools to track attendance.", "Pretending to work while on holiday."], "prompt": "What does digital presenteeism refer to in the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与结尾提倡有意设计的混合模式一致；B 与作者指出的弊端相悖；C 与抵制侵入式监控相反；D 与全文关注隐藏成本相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["A hybrid model with redesigned workflows and mentorship.", "Complete remote work with no office contact.", "Strict monitoring software for all remote staff.", "Ignoring the social costs of remote work."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合全面权衡利弊的分析语气；B 文中无愤怒情绪；C 无幽默讽刺；D 全文客观论述而非个人抒情。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Angry and dismissive.", "Humorous and ironic.", "Highly emotional and personal."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone in the passage?"}], "text": "Remote work was once celebrated as a liberation from commuting, rigid schedules, and crowded offices, yet several years of large-scale experience have revealed a more complicated picture. On the surface, the benefits are obvious: employees save hours each week, companies reduce spending on office space, and talented workers can be hired regardless of location. Surveys consistently show that many people report higher job satisfaction when they can control their working environment. Beneath these advantages, however, lie costs that are harder to measure but no less real. One of the most frequently cited is the erosion of informal learning. In a physical office, junior employees absorb knowledge by overhearing conversations, watching experienced colleagues solve problems, and asking quick questions that would seem too trivial for a scheduled video call. When work moves online, those spontaneous exchanges largely disappear, and newcomers may take much longer to understand unwritten norms. Collaboration also changes in subtle ways. While video meetings can handle formal decisions efficiently, they are poor substitutes for the creative friction that arises when people with different perspectives gather around a whiteboard. Ideas that might have emerged from an unplanned discussion are less likely to surface when every interaction must be arranged in advance. Furthermore, the boundary between work and home becomes blurred. Without a clear physical separation, many remote workers find themselves answering emails late at night or feeling guilty when they are not visibly online, a phenomenon researchers call digital presenteeism. Paradoxically, the flexibility intended to reduce stress can increase it. Employers, meanwhile, face their own dilemma. They must balance trust with accountability, resisting the temptation to monitor employees through invasive software while still ensuring that teams remain coordinated. The most successful organizations, studies suggest, do not treat remote work as simply working from home, but redesign workflows, invest in mentorship, and protect time for unstructured interaction. The future of work, therefore, is unlikely to be fully remote or fully office-based, but a deliberate hybrid that acknowledges both the freedom remote work offers and the human connections it can quietly erode.", "title": "The Hidden Costs of Remote Work"}]}, {"no": 27, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了法律空白这一核心问题；B 与事实相反，战争难民已有法律保护；C 与文中多数为境内流离相悖；D 与文中穷国受害最重相反。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Climate migrants lack clear legal protection under international law.", "Refugees from war are not protected by any treaty.", "Most people prefer to migrate across borders.", "Climate change has stopped affecting poor countries."], "prompt": "What is the central problem discussed in the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 文中未提人口规模；B 与领土可能消失、国家存续成问题一致；C 文中无拒绝帮助之意；D 与面临海平面上升相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Because their populations are very large.", "Because their entire territory may disappear.", "Because they refuse all international help.", "Because they have no experience of flooding."], "prompt": "Why is the case of Pacific island nations described as extraordinary?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于极端且非批评者观点；B 与优先就地适应一致；C 与批评者主张帮助适应相反；D 是支持者而非批评者的提议。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Opening all borders immediately.", "Helping people adapt where they live.", "Ignoring climate displacement entirely.", "Expanding refugee law without limits."], "prompt": "According to critics, what should be the priority?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与富国排放多、穷国受害重的论述一致；B 与共同责任相反；C 否认了文中的因果联系；D 与作者主张相反。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Wealthy countries bear special responsibility because their emissions caused much of the harm.", "Poor countries should solve the problem alone.", "Climate migration is unrelated to historical emissions.", "Justice requires closing all borders."], "prompt": "What can be inferred about the author's view on justice?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中政治意愿不足的暗示不符；B 与取决于政治意愿而非科学确定性一致；C 与全文关切不符；D 与作者强调政治因素相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Confident it will happen soon.", "Skeptical, because it depends on political willingness rather than science.", "Indifferent to whether it happens.", "Certain that science alone will solve it."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward a future international response?"}], "text": "As sea levels rise, deserts expand, and extreme weather becomes more frequent, millions of people are being forced to leave homes their families have occupied for generations. Unlike refugees fleeing war, who are protected by established international law, those displaced by climate change occupy a legal gray area. No binding treaty defines their status, guarantees their right to cross borders, or obliges other countries to receive them. This gap is becoming increasingly difficult to ignore. In Bangladesh, for instance, flooding and saltwater intrusion are steadily destroying farmland in the delta, pushing farming families toward overcrowded cities where jobs and housing are already scarce. In parts of sub-Saharan Africa, prolonged drought has undermined pastoral livelihoods, intensifying competition for water and fertile land. In the Pacific, entire island nations face the prospect of becoming uninhabitable within decades, raising the extraordinary question of what happens to a state when its territory disappears. Some scholars argue that existing refugee law should be expanded, while others propose a new international framework specifically for climate migrants, including planned relocation, financial compensation, and legal pathways for movement. Critics of these proposals warn that creating a separate category could be politically unfeasible, since many governments are already reluctant to accept migrants of any kind. They also note that most climate displacement occurs within national borders, so the priority should be helping people adapt where they live rather than facilitating cross-border movement. Yet adaptation has limits. When land becomes permanently submerged or rainfall patterns collapse, no amount of local investment can restore a viable livelihood. The debate therefore reflects a deeper tension between national sovereignty and shared responsibility. Climate change is driven disproportionately by emissions from wealthy industrialized countries, while its worst consequences fall on poorer nations that contributed least to the problem. From this perspective, assisting climate migrants is not charity but a matter of justice. Whether the world develops a coherent response will depend less on scientific certainty, which is already strong, than on political willingness to recognize that borders cannot contain a crisis created by the atmosphere itself.", "title": "When Climate Change Forces People to Move"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与全文批判立场相反；B 概括了权力、隐私与所有权问题的主旨；C 与大多数人不读隐私政策相反；D 与匿名数据可被重新识别相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Data collection is harmless and should continue unchanged.", "The collection and control of personal data raises serious questions of power, privacy, and ownership.", "Privacy policies are always read carefully by users.", "Anonymization completely protects personal privacy."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 文中未提强制付费；B 与冗长政策、无法谈判、一点即同意一致；C 与数据收集事实相反；D 与权力失衡的论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Because users are forced to pay for services.", "Because privacy policies are long and non-negotiable.", "Because companies never collect any data.", "Because users enjoy sharing their information."], "prompt": "Why does the author say consent is often meaningless?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与研究者通过合并信息重新识别一致；B 与文中研究存在相反；C 文中未提及；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Researchers have re-identified supposedly anonymous datasets.", "No researcher has ever studied the issue.", "Companies voluntarily delete all anonymous data.", "Anonymization is illegal in most countries."], "prompt": "What evidence does the author give that anonymization is insufficient?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于绝对，作者只介绍该提议；B 符合将其列为改革者提议之一的写法；C 与介绍多项改革相反；D 与数据有价值的论述相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["The author fully endorses paying individuals for their data.", "The author presents it as one reform proposal among others.", "The author rejects all proposals for reform.", "The author believes data has no economic value."], "prompt": "What can be inferred about the author's view of data as property?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃关切的语气；B 与议题的严肃性不符；C 过于极端，作者肯定监管进展；D 忽略了作者的批判立场。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and concerned.", "Light-hearted and amused.", "Hostile toward all technology.", "Neutral and purely descriptive."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Every search query, location check-in, and online purchase leaves a digital trace, and together these traces form a detailed portrait of an individual's habits, preferences, and vulnerabilities. Technology companies argue that collecting such data is necessary to provide free services, personalize content, and improve products. Users, in this view, exchange information for convenience, and the bargain is voluntary. Yet this framing obscures a fundamental imbalance of power. Most people do not read lengthy privacy policies, nor could they meaningfully negotiate their terms. Consent is typically obtained through a single click on a button labeled Accept, after which data may be shared with advertisers, data brokers, and other third parties in ways the user never anticipated. The consequences extend beyond targeted advertising. Data profiles can influence the price a person pays for insurance, the advertisements for jobs they see, and even their eligibility for credit. In some cases, sensitive information about health or political views has been inferred from seemingly innocent behavior, such as browsing history or shopping patterns. Defenders of the current system contend that anonymization protects privacy, but researchers have repeatedly shown that supposedly anonymous datasets can be re-identified by combining them with other available information. Regulation has begun to respond. The European Union's General Data Protection Regulation grants individuals the right to access, correct, and delete their data, and imposes substantial fines for violations. Similar laws are emerging elsewhere, though enforcement remains uneven. Some reformers go further, proposing that individuals should own their data as property and be paid when companies profit from it. Others argue that data is better understood as a collective resource, since its value arises from patterns across millions of people rather than from any single person's contribution. What is clear is that the old assumption, that data collection is a harmless byproduct of digital life, can no longer be sustained. As societies become increasingly dependent on data-driven systems, the question of who controls personal information is becoming one of the defining political issues of the digital age, touching on autonomy, equality, and the very nature of freedom.", "title": "Who Owns Your Data?"}]}, {"no": 28, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了灵活与保障缺失的矛盾主旨；B 过于绝对，作者肯定灵活性；C 与文中大量批评相反；D 与零工规模扩大的论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["The gig economy offers flexibility but leaves workers without traditional protections.", "Gig work is entirely harmful and should be banned.", "All gig workers are satisfied with their conditions.", "The gig economy is shrinking rapidly."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 文中未提工会要求；B 与平台称工人是独立承包商以规避责任一致；C 与文中算法控制相反；D 与证据相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Because unions demand it.", "Because platforms classify workers as independent contractors.", "Because workers prefer having no benefits.", "Because governments forbid employee status."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why do gig workers often lack benefits?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与算法决定收入相反；C 与接单率和评分决定派单一致；D 与工人实际体验相反；B 过于夸大，未说完全不能拒绝。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Workers can freely choose all their hours.", "Platforms never monitor workers.", "Algorithmic ratings and acceptance rates strongly influence earnings.", "Gig workers set their own pay rates."], "prompt": "What does the passage imply about algorithmic management?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者的批判语气相反；D 与结尾提倡可携带福利和最低收入保障一致；B 与文中建议相反；C 与主张改革相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Gig companies should face no regulation.", "Workers should be denied any protection.", "Flexibility is worthless to workers.", "Portable benefits and minimum earnings guarantees are desirable reforms."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author would support which reform?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合分析利弊的语气；B 全文客观论述；C 与讨论改革的态度不符；D 未表现出愤怒。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely descriptive without judgment.", "Completely indifferent.", "Angry and hostile."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "The rise of app-based gig work has transformed how millions of people earn a living, promising freedom from the nine-to-five routine while quietly dismantling many of the protections associated with traditional employment. Drivers, couriers, and freelance task workers can choose when to log on, accept assignments that suit their schedules, and, in theory, work for several platforms simultaneously. For students, parents, and others who need flexible hours, this autonomy is genuinely valuable. Yet the same flexibility often comes at a steep price. Because platforms classify workers as independent contractors rather than employees, they are generally not obliged to provide minimum wages, paid sick leave, health insurance, or protection against unfair dismissal. Income can fluctuate wildly from week to week, depending on demand, weather, and opaque algorithmic decisions about which workers receive the most lucrative tasks. Workers who decline too many assignments or receive low ratings from customers may find their access to work sharply reduced, creating a form of control that is less visible than a manager's orders but no less powerful. Supporters of the model argue that gig work was never intended to be a full-time career and that workers value independence over security. This argument, however, overlooks the reality that for many people gig work is not a side income but their main source of livelihood. When a driver works sixty hours a week yet still cannot afford medical care, the distinction between flexibility and precarity becomes difficult to sustain. Some governments have begun to respond, introducing minimum earnings guarantees, portable benefits that follow workers between platforms, and requirements for greater algorithmic transparency. These reforms attempt to preserve what workers value about gig work while restoring a basic safety net. Whether they succeed will depend on enforcement and on whether platforms can adapt their business models. The broader lesson extends beyond any single app: when technology reorganizes labor markets, societies must decide which risks should be borne by individuals and which should be shared, rather than assuming that innovation alone will produce a fair outcome.", "title": "Gig Work: Freedom or Insecurity?"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中比较机制相反；B 与算法放大比较和点赞焦虑一致；C 过于绝对，作者承认积极作用；D 与青少年脆弱性相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Social media affects all users in exactly the same way.", "Algorithms amplify comparison and make validation anxiety worse.", "Social media has only positive effects on teenagers.", "Adults are more vulnerable than adolescents."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what role do algorithms play?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中大脑发育和同伴认同相反；C 与青春期对同伴评价敏感一致；B 与证据相反；D 与使用时间的影响相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["They have fully mature brains.", "They use social media less than adults.", "They are especially sensitive to peer approval during adolescence.", "They are immune to online pressure."], "prompt": "Why are adolescents particularly vulnerable, according to the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张个人和平台共同负责相反；D 与设计更健康、教育和家庭沟通的建议一致；B 过于简单化；C 与承认平台设计责任相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Banning all social media for teenagers.", "Blaming only teenagers for their problems.", "Ignoring platform design entirely.", "Combining healthier design, education, and family discussion."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃关切的语气；B 与全文分析不符；C 与承认积极面相反；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and concerned.", "Dismissive and sarcastic.", "Entirely optimistic.", "Indifferent and detached."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}, {"prompt": "What does the passage suggest about the relationship between platform design and user behavior?", "options": ["Platform design has no influence on how teenagers behave.", "Design features such as notifications and infinite scrolling are engineered to maximize engagement.", "Teenagers can easily resist all design features.", "Platforms are designed mainly to protect wellbeing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中设计起决定作用相反；B 与通知、无限滚动为最大化参与而设计一致；C 与即使自律用户也难脱身相反；D 与商业模式依赖注意力相悖。"}], "text": "For many teenagers, social media is not merely a pastime but the principal arena in which identity, friendship, and status are negotiated. Platforms offer genuine benefits: they allow young people to maintain friendships across distance, find communities that share their interests, and express creativity. Yet a growing body of research suggests that heavy use is associated with higher levels of anxiety, depression, and poor sleep, particularly among adolescent girls. The mechanisms are increasingly well understood. Social media encourages constant comparison with carefully curated images of peers, influencers, and celebrities, fostering the impression that everyone else is more attractive, successful, and popular. Because the brain during adolescence is especially sensitive to peer approval and social rejection, this comparison can feel far more consequential than it would for an adult. Algorithms intensify the effect by prioritizing content that provokes strong emotional reactions, often showcasing extreme lifestyles or appearance ideals that are unattainable for most viewers. The pursuit of validation through likes and comments can then become compulsive, with self-worth rising and falling according to numbers on a screen. It would be misleading, however, to attribute these outcomes solely to individual weakness or poor choices. Platform design plays a decisive role: notifications, infinite scrolling, and streaks are deliberately engineered to maximize engagement, making it difficult for even disciplined users to disengage. Critics therefore argue that responsibility should not rest only with teenagers or their parents, but also with companies whose business models depend on capturing attention. Some propose design changes, such as hiding like counts, limiting algorithmic recommendations for minors, and introducing prompts that encourage breaks. Others emphasize digital literacy education, helping young people recognize manipulation and develop healthier habits. The most promising approach likely combines both strategies, acknowledging that adolescents need support in navigating an environment that was not designed with their wellbeing in mind.", "title": "Social Media and Adolescent Mental Health"}]}, {"no": 29, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与电网和储能挑战相反；C 概括了技术可行、系统转型困难的主旨；B 过于悲观，作者肯定进展；D 与文中就业讨论不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Renewable energy is too expensive to ever succeed.", "Solar power has failed in all countries.", "Renewable technology is advancing, but transforming the whole energy system remains difficult.", "Fossil fuels will remain dominant forever."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 文中未提土地不足；D 与太阳能夜间不发电、风能不稳定一致；B 与成本下降相反；C 与文中技术成熟相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["There is not enough land for solar panels.", "Renewable energy is becoming more expensive.", "The technology does not exist yet.", "Solar and wind output varies with weather and time of day."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what is the main challenge of intermittency?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与需要新电网、储能和市场规则一致；B 与文中系统性变革相反；C 与作者主张全面改革相悖；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Building new grids, storage, and market rules.", "Simply installing more solar panels.", "Ignoring the existing power system.", "Reducing all energy consumption to zero."], "prompt": "What does the author suggest is necessary for a full transition?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既肯定进展又强调制度障碍的立场一致；C 与承认困难相反；D 与全文深入讨论不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about renewables.", "Cautiously optimistic, recognizing progress and systemic barriers.", "Unaware of any challenges.", "Opposed to all renewable energy."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward renewable energy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与储能和电网投资的建议相反；C 与结尾强调政策与投资决定成败一致；B 与技术已成熟相悖；D 与文中多国案例相反。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Abandoning solar and wind projects.", "Waiting for technology to solve everything alone.", "Investing in storage, grids, and supportive policy.", "Relying only on fossil fuels."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author would support which policy?"}], "text": "The cost of solar panels has fallen by more than eighty percent in a decade, and wind turbines now generate electricity more cheaply than coal in many regions, yet the transition to renewable energy remains far from complete. This paradox reveals an important truth: technological progress alone does not transform an energy system. Solar and wind power are intermittent, producing electricity only when the sun shines or the wind blows, while demand for electricity follows its own rhythms, peaking in the early evening when solar output declines. Without large-scale storage, flexible grids, and new market rules, excess renewable power may be wasted at midday and shortages may occur after sunset. Batteries are becoming cheaper, but storing enough energy for cloudy weeks or windless periods is still expensive and technically demanding. Grids designed for a small number of large coal and gas plants must be redesigned to handle thousands of dispersed solar rooftops, offshore wind farms, and electric vehicles that can both consume and supply power. These changes require enormous investment, careful planning, and coordination across regions and countries. Politics adds another layer of complexity. Fossil fuel industries employ millions of people and wield considerable influence, while communities dependent on coal mining or oil production fear job losses and economic decline. A just transition therefore requires retraining programs, regional investment, and social protections for affected workers, without which political opposition can stall even the most promising projects. Some countries have made remarkable progress: Denmark generates more than half its electricity from wind, and China installs more solar capacity each year than most nations possess in total. Others have moved more slowly, constrained by outdated regulations or limited finance. The lesson is that the energy transition is as much an institutional and social challenge as a technological one. The tools largely exist; what remains uncertain is whether societies can rebuild the systems around them quickly enough to meet climate goals while ensuring that the benefits and burdens are fairly shared.", "title": "The Shift to Renewable Energy"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与债务风险相反；D 概括了文凭价值与债务负担并存的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中多国情况相反。", "answer": "D", "options": ["University degrees have no value at all.", "All graduates earn high salaries immediately.", "Tuition fees are falling everywhere.", "Rising tuition creates both opportunity and heavy debt burdens."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与公共拨款削减转嫁给学生一致；B 与成本上升相反；C 与文中无此说法相悖；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Governments reduced public funding for universities.", "Universities became much cheaper to run.", "Students demanded higher fees.", "Employers paid tuition for all students."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why have tuition fees risen?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与收入挂钩还款是替代方案而非现行贷款；B 与低收入毕业生负担更重一致；C 与文中毕业生推迟购房结婚相反；D 与债务影响相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Student debt affects all graduates equally.", "Low-income graduates bear the heaviest burden.", "Student loans have no effect on life choices.", "Only wealthy students take out loans."], "prompt": "What does the passage imply about the impact of student debt?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张改革相反；C 与结尾提倡收入挂钩还款和增加拨款一致；B 与文中担忧相悖；D 与作者的批判立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Abolishing all universities.", "Keeping the current system unchanged.", "Income-linked repayment and greater public funding.", "Making all students pay upfront in full."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which reform?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于绝对，作者承认文凭价值；D 与既肯定收益又强调不平等风险一致；B 与讨论不平等相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Tuition should be ignored as a policy issue.", "Higher education always reduces inequality.", "Degrees guarantee success for everyone.", "Higher education can widen inequality if access depends on wealth."], "prompt": "What is the author's main concern?"}], "text": "In many countries, the price of a university education has risen far faster than wages, forcing students and their families to make difficult calculations about whether a degree is worth its cost. Proponents of higher fees argue that graduates earn substantially more over their lifetimes than those without degrees, and that students should therefore contribute to the cost of their education. Universities, they contend, need additional revenue to maintain facilities, hire qualified staff, and compete internationally. Yet this logic conceals a deeper shift in who bears the burden of funding higher education. As governments have reduced public subsidies, the cost has increasingly been transferred to students through tuition fees and loans. In the United States, total student debt now exceeds one and a half trillion dollars, with many borrowers struggling for decades. In England, graduates repay a proportion of their income above a threshold, but large balances may never be fully repaid, in effect becoming a graduate tax. The consequences extend beyond individual finances. Research shows that fear of debt deters some talented students from low-income backgrounds from applying to prestigious universities, while others choose shorter, cheaper courses rather than those that best match their abilities. Graduates burdened by debt are also more likely to delay buying a home, starting a family, or launching a business, with ripple effects for the wider economy. Defenders of the current system point out that degrees still offer substantial returns on average, and that no system can provide unlimited free education without trade-offs. Critics respond that averages conceal wide variation: some degrees lead to high-paying careers, while others offer modest returns that do not justify large debts. The debate is ultimately about the purpose of higher education. If universities are seen primarily as engines of social mobility, then access should not depend on family wealth. If they are treated as private investments, then students must weigh costs and benefits like consumers. Most societies attempt to balance these visions through grants, income-linked repayment, and subsidized loans, but the balance has shifted steadily toward the individual. Whether that shift is sustainable, both economically and socially, remains one of the most pressing questions in education policy.", "title": "The Rising Cost of University Education"}]}, {"no": 30, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了技术潜力与伦理争议并存的主旨；B 过于绝对，作者强调风险；C 与文中广泛讨论相反；D 与治疗疾病的例子相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Gene editing offers medical promise but raises profound ethical questions.", "Gene editing should be used without any limits.", "Gene editing is irrelevant to modern medicine.", "Gene editing can only treat minor illnesses."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中体细胞与生殖系区分相反；B 与生殖系改变可遗传给后代一致；C 与技术已用于治疗相反；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Somatic editing is more dangerous than germline editing.", "Germline changes can be inherited by future generations.", "Gene editing has never been tested in humans.", "All gene editing is currently illegal everywhere."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why is germline editing especially controversial?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与富人获取增强的担忧相反；C 与增强可能加剧不平等一致；B 与文中风险讨论相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Enhancement would reduce all inequality.", "Gene editing is too cheap to matter.", "Enhancement could widen social inequality.", "Only poor families would use enhancement."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about genetic enhancement?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张谨慎相反；D 与结尾提倡公开辩论和明确界限一致；B 与强调监督相悖；C 与区分治疗与增强相反。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Scientists should decide alone without public input.", "All research should be banned immediately.", "Treatment and enhancement are identical.", "Society needs public debate and clear boundaries."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃分析的语气；B 与伦理讨论不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and analytical.", "Light-hearted and amused.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "The ability to edit the human genome with tools such as CRISPR has transformed medicine from a discipline that treats symptoms into one that can, in principle, correct the underlying genetic causes of disease. Already, gene editing is being tested as a treatment for sickle cell disease, certain forms of blindness, and inherited cholesterol disorders, offering hope to patients for whom conventional therapies provide only partial relief. These somatic therapies, which alter cells in a single patient without affecting their children, are widely regarded as ethically acceptable when conducted with rigorous safety testing and informed consent. The controversy intensifies, however, when editing moves from somatic cells to the germline, the eggs, sperm, or early embryos whose genetic changes would be inherited by all future descendants. In 2018, the announcement that gene-edited babies had been born provoked worldwide condemnation, not only because the experiment was conducted secretly, but because it crossed a line that many scientists and ethicists believed should not be crossed without broad societal agreement. The technical risks are substantial: unintended edits, known as off-target effects, could introduce new diseases, and the long-term consequences of altering a developing embryo cannot be fully predicted. Beyond safety, germline editing raises questions about fairness and human identity. If editing were used not merely to prevent serious disease but to enhance intelligence, height, or athletic ability, access would likely be limited to wealthy families, potentially creating a genetic divide between enhanced and unenhanced children. Some philosophers argue that such enhancement would undermine the idea that human worth is independent of genetic traits, while others contend that parents already seek advantages for their children through education and nutrition, and that genetic enhancement is merely a more powerful extension of that impulse. Regulation varies sharply across countries, with some banning germline editing outright and others permitting research under strict oversight. The central challenge, therefore, is not merely scientific but democratic: who should decide which genetic changes are permissible, and by what process? Until societies develop transparent rules and inclusive public debate, the technology will continue to advance faster than the ethical frameworks needed to govern it.", "title": "Gene Editing: Promise and Peril"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与基础设施不足的论述相反；B 与城市化既带来机会也带来压力的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中贫民窟问题相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Urbanization has only benefits.", "Urbanization brings opportunity but also strains housing, services, and the environment.", "Cities are shrinking worldwide.", "Urbanization affects only wealthy countries."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中服务跟不上的论述相反；B 与住房短缺和贫民窟扩张一致；C 与大量人口涌入相悖；D 与文中就业机会的吸引力相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["All migrants immediately find good housing.", "Rapid growth often outpaces housing and infrastructure.", "Cities have no informal settlements.", "Migration to cities has stopped."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what happens when urban growth is too rapid?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与城市资源集中的论述相反；C 与规划和投资可改善结果一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中城市创新作用相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Urbanization is always a disaster.", "Cities cannot be improved.", "Good planning and investment can make urbanization beneficial.", "Rural areas should be abandoned."], "prompt": "What can be inferred from the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者承认机会相反；D 与既肯定活力又强调规划的立场一致；B 与文中经济机遇相悖；C 与全文讨论不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Urbanization should be stopped completely.", "Cities offer no economic benefits.", "Governments should ignore urban growth.", "Urbanization needs deliberate planning to manage its costs."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward urbanization?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与文中具体分析不符；C 未表现出愤怒；D 与承认挑战相反。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely emotional.", "Angry and hostile.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "By the middle of this century, nearly seven in ten people on Earth are expected to live in cities, a transformation that is reshaping economies, cultures, and the environment on an unprecedented scale. Urbanization is driven by powerful forces: cities offer jobs, education, healthcare, and social mobility that rural areas often cannot match. A young person moving from a village to a metropolis may gain access to opportunities that would have been unimaginable a generation ago. Yet when urban growth outpaces planning, the same forces that attract migrants can produce overcrowding, pollution, and inequality. In many rapidly growing cities in Africa and Asia, informal settlements expand faster than governments can provide clean water, sanitation, or transport, leaving millions to live without basic services. Traffic congestion wastes time and fuel, while air pollution contributes to respiratory disease and premature death. Housing markets in successful cities often become unaffordable, forcing low-income workers to live far from their jobs or in precarious conditions. At the same time, cities are engines of innovation and efficiency. Dense populations make public transport viable, reduce per capita energy use, and foster the exchange of ideas that drives creativity. Well-planned cities can provide a higher quality of life with a smaller environmental footprint than dispersed settlements. The difference between these outcomes depends largely on governance. Cities that invest in affordable housing, reliable public transport, green spaces, and participatory planning tend to manage growth more successfully than those that leave development to market forces alone. International experience also shows that urbanization need not mean megacities alone; secondary cities and well-connected towns can absorb population growth while offering a more manageable scale. The challenge for policymakers is therefore not to halt urbanization, which is driven by deep economic and social aspirations, but to shape it deliberately, ensuring that the benefits of city life are shared rather than concentrated among the already privileged.", "title": "The Urban Century"}]}, {"no": 31, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于绝对，作者承认新岗位会出现；C 与工作性质改变、转型痛苦的主旨一致；B 与文中失业风险相悖；D 与历史比较相反。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Automation will eliminate all jobs forever.", "Automation has no effect on employment.", "Automation will transform work, creating new jobs while displacing many workers.", "Only factory workers are affected by automation."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中技能需求变化相反；D 与常规任务易被自动化一致；B 与创造性工作较难替代相悖；C 与文中分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Creative work is the easiest to automate.", "Only low-skilled jobs are at risk.", "Automation affects all jobs equally.", "Routine, predictable tasks are most vulnerable to automation."], "prompt": "According to the passage, which jobs are most at risk?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与历史转型最终创造新岗位但过程痛苦一致；B 过于乐观，忽略过渡成本；C 与文中新岗位出现相反；D 与历史证据相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Past transitions created new jobs but caused painful disruption.", "Past transitions caused no hardship at all.", "Technology has never created new occupations.", "History is irrelevant to current automation."], "prompt": "What does the author imply by referring to earlier industrial revolutions?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张支持转型相反；B 与教育、再培训和社会保障的建议一致；C 与文中政策讨论相悖；D 与作者的建设性立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Banning all automation technology.", "Investing in education, retraining, and social protection.", "Leaving displaced workers without any help.", "Relying only on market forces."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于极端；C 与既承认风险又强调应对的立场一致；B 与讨论失业风险相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Automation is purely beneficial.", "Automation will cause no problems.", "Automation poses real risks that require active preparation.", "Automation should be ignored."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward automation?"}], "text": "For more than two centuries, each wave of automation has provoked fears that machines would permanently eliminate work, and each time those fears have proved exaggerated in the long run, yet painful for those directly affected. The mechanization of agriculture displaced millions of farm workers, but eventually created jobs in manufacturing; the computer revolution disrupted clerical work while generating entirely new occupations in software and services. Artificial intelligence and robotics represent the latest wave, but they differ in one important respect: they threaten not only manual labor but also cognitive tasks once considered uniquely human, from analyzing legal documents to diagnosing medical images and even writing news reports. Economists distinguish between jobs that are routine and predictable, which are most vulnerable, and those requiring creativity, empathy, or complex physical dexterity in unpredictable environments, which are harder to automate. A truck driver following a fixed highway route faces greater risk than a plumber navigating varied homes, just as a data entry clerk faces greater risk than a therapist. The aggregate effect on employment is uncertain. Optimists argue that automation will increase productivity, lower prices, and create demand for new goods and services, ultimately generating more jobs than it destroys. Pessimists warn that this time the pace of change may outrun workers' ability to adapt, particularly for older workers or those without access to retraining. The distribution of gains also matters: if productivity rises but wages stagnate, automation could widen inequality even while the economy grows. History suggests that the outcome depends less on the technology itself than on institutions and policies. Countries that invest in education, lifelong learning, and social protection tend to navigate transitions more successfully than those that leave displaced workers to fend for themselves. Proposals such as shorter working weeks, universal basic income, and stronger labor protections reflect different visions of how to share the benefits of automation. What is clear is that passive optimism is insufficient; societies must actively prepare for a labor market in which the skills valued today may be obsolete tomorrow, while ensuring that technological progress serves broad prosperity rather than a narrow elite.", "title": "Automation and the Future of Work"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中虚假信息泛滥相反；D 概括了技术与心理因素共同作用的主旨；B 过于简单化；C 与文中社交媒体放大作用相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Misinformation is a new phenomenon.", "Only uneducated people believe false information.", "Fact-checking alone will solve the problem.", "Misinformation spreads because technology and human psychology reinforce each other."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中情绪化内容更易传播相反；D 与算法优先情绪化内容一致；B 与文中证据相悖；C 与平台设计的作用相反。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Algorithms prioritize calm, factual content.", "People share only verified information.", "Social media has no role in spreading falsehoods.", "Algorithms favor emotionally engaging content, including falsehoods."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why does misinformation spread quickly online?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与更正难以消除原有信念一致；B 与文中研究相反；C 与心理机制相悖；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Corrections often fail to fully erase false beliefs.", "Fact-checks always change minds immediately.", "People never remember false information.", "Repetition has no effect on belief."], "prompt": "What does the passage imply about fact-checking?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于绝对，作者主张综合措施；B 与媒体素养、透明算法和平台责任的建议一致；C 与承认平台责任相悖；D 与文中教育作用相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Censoring all online discussion.", "Combining media literacy, transparent algorithms, and platform accountability.", "Blaming only individual users.", "Ignoring the problem entirely."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author supports which response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者的分析语气不符；C 与严肃关切的语气一致；B 与承认复杂性相反；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Humorous and light-hearted.", "Indifferent and neutral.", "Serious and concerned.", "Angry and accusatory."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "False information is not new, but the digital age has transformed both its speed and its reach. Rumors that once traveled slowly through communities can now reach millions of people within hours, amplified by algorithms that reward engagement rather than accuracy. Research shows that false stories often spread faster than true ones, partly because they tend to be more novel and emotionally provocative, triggering surprise, fear, or outrage. When people encounter information that confirms their existing beliefs, they are more likely to accept it without scrutiny, a tendency psychologists call confirmation bias. Repetition compounds the problem: the more often a claim is seen, the more familiar and therefore credible it feels, even when it has been repeatedly debunked. Social media platforms intensify these psychological vulnerabilities through design choices. Content that provokes strong reactions receives greater visibility, while nuanced or uncertain information is less likely to be shared. During elections, health crises, and natural disasters, misinformation can have serious consequences, influencing voting decisions, discouraging vaccination, or spreading panic. Fact-checking organizations have expanded rapidly, yet corrections often reach far fewer people than the original falsehood, and they may even reinforce mistaken beliefs among those most committed to them. Some experts argue that the solution lies in media literacy education, teaching citizens to evaluate sources, recognize manipulation, and pause before sharing. Others call for changes to platform design, such as reducing the algorithmic promotion of sensational content, labeling disputed claims, and increasing transparency about how information is ranked. Regulation raises difficult questions about free speech, since governments that control information flows may suppress legitimate dissent. The most realistic approach likely combines individual skills, responsible platform design, and independent journalism, recognizing that misinformation is not merely a technical problem but a reflection of deeper social divisions and distrust. Addressing it therefore requires rebuilding trust in institutions as much as correcting individual falsehoods.", "title": "Why Misinformation Spreads"}]}, {"no": 32, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了快时尚的环境与伦理代价及可持续出路的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中大量批评相反；D 与可持续运动的论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Fast fashion creates serious environmental and ethical costs, and sustainable alternatives are emerging.", "Fashion has no impact on the environment.", "All clothing companies are already sustainable.", "Consumers should buy more clothes every week."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中水和农药消耗相反；B 与棉花耗水、合成纤维微塑料、染色污染一致；C 与工人处境相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Fashion production uses no natural resources.", "Cotton farming, synthetic fabrics, and dyeing all harm the environment.", "Garment workers are always well paid.", "Fashion waste is fully recycled."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what are some environmental costs of fashion?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与漂绿定义相反；C 与小规模环保系列掩盖整体不可持续做法一致；B 与文中批评相悖；D 与标准缺失的论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Companies always prove their sustainability claims.", "Greenwashing never happens in fashion.", "A company may promote a small green line while most production remains unsustainable.", "Consumers can easily verify all claims."], "prompt": "What does the author imply by greenwashing?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张系统改革相反；D 与监管、劳工标准和商业模式改革的建议一致；B 与文中个人选择重要相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Individual choices alone will solve the problem.", "Sustainable fashion should be abandoned.", "Only wealthy people should buy clothes.", "Systemic regulation and new business models are needed."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与伦理讨论不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Humorous and light-hearted.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "The fashion industry produces more than one hundred billion garments each year, yet a large proportion are worn only a few times before being discarded, creating an environmental burden that is increasingly difficult to ignore. Fast fashion companies thrive by offering cheap, rapidly changing styles, encouraging consumers to buy frequently and treat clothing as disposable. Behind low prices lie hidden costs: cotton cultivation consumes enormous quantities of water and pesticides, synthetic fabrics shed microplastics when washed, and dyeing processes pollute rivers in manufacturing regions. Workers in garment factories often face low wages, long hours, and unsafe conditions, raising ethical questions that go beyond environmental damage. In response, a sustainable fashion movement has emerged, advocating slower consumption, durable design, and transparent supply chains. Some brands now use organic cotton, recycled polyester, and innovative materials made from mushrooms or pineapple leaves. Others offer repair services, resale platforms, and rental schemes that extend the life of each garment. Consumers, too, are changing their habits, buying second-hand clothing, choosing quality over quantity, and learning to repair rather than replace. Critics caution, however, that sustainability can become a marketing slogan rather than a genuine commitment. Greenwashing occurs when companies highlight a small eco-friendly collection while continuing unsustainable practices across most of their business. Without clear standards and independent verification, shoppers cannot easily distinguish real progress from public relations. Moreover, sustainable clothing often costs more, raising concerns that ethical fashion remains accessible mainly to affluent consumers. The challenge, therefore, is systemic: individual choices matter, but they must be supported by regulation, fair labor standards, and business models that reward durability over volume. Fashion will always reflect identity and creativity, but its future depends on reconciling that expressive function with the finite resources of the planet and the dignity of the people who make what we wear.", "title": "Sustainable Fashion: Beyond Fast Fashion"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中多国趋势相反；B 与老龄化既带来挑战也带来机遇的主旨一致；C 过于悲观；D 与文中改革讨论相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Population aging affects only Japan.", "Aging populations create economic and healthcare challenges but also offer opportunities.", "Older people cannot contribute to society.", "Pension systems need no reform."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与出生率下降相反；C 与低生育率加长寿一致；B 与寿命延长相悖；D 与文中趋势不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Birth rates are rising rapidly.", "Life expectancy is falling.", "Falling birth rates and longer lives are increasing the share of older people.", "Young people are leaving cities."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why are populations aging?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中养老金压力相反；D 与退休年龄、缴费、福利或收入来源的选择一致；B 与改革必要性相悖；C 与文中讨论不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Pension systems face no financial pressure.", "Governments have unlimited funds.", "Aging has no effect on healthcare.", "Governments must choose among raising retirement ages, contributions, or cutting benefits."], "prompt": "What does the passage imply about pension reform?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与灵活工作和终身学习可变挑战为机遇一致；B 与作者肯定老年人贡献相反；C 与技术解决方案相悖；D 与主动规划的主张不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Flexible work and lifelong learning can help societies adapt to aging.", "Older workers should be forced to retire early.", "Technology cannot help older people.", "Societies should wait until systems collapse."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author supports which view?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既承认挑战又看到机遇的立场一致；C 与讨论改革相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about aging.", "Cautiously optimistic, seeing both challenges and opportunities.", "Indifferent to demographic change.", "Hostile toward older people."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward population aging?"}], "text": "Across much of the developed world, and increasingly in developing countries, populations are growing older at a pace that is transforming economies, healthcare systems, and family life. Falling birth rates combined with longer life expectancy mean that the proportion of people over sixty-five is rising steadily, while the number of working-age adults available to support them is shrinking. In Japan, Italy, and South Korea, this demographic shift is already advanced, but China and many European nations are following a similar trajectory. The economic implications are profound. Pension systems designed when most people retired at sixty and lived only a few years beyond that age now face decades of payments to each retiree. Governments must either raise retirement ages, increase contributions, reduce benefits, or find new sources of revenue, each option carrying political costs. Healthcare presents an even greater challenge, as older populations require more treatment for chronic conditions such as heart disease, diabetes, and dementia. Without reform, healthcare spending could consume an ever-larger share of national budgets. Yet an aging society is not merely a burden. Older adults possess experience, skills, and wealth that can contribute to society if institutions adapt. Many people in their sixties and seventies remain healthy, active, and eager to work part-time, volunteer, or care for grandchildren. Economies that enable flexible working, lifelong learning, and age-friendly workplaces can turn demographic change into an opportunity rather than a crisis. Technology also offers solutions, from telemedicine and assistive robots to smart homes that allow older people to live independently for longer. The key question is whether societies will plan proactively or react only when systems are already strained. Countries that invest early in preventive healthcare, pension reform, and inclusive labor markets are better positioned to manage the transition. Aging is a sign of success, reflecting better nutrition, medicine, and living conditions, but its benefits will only be realized if policies recognize older citizens as contributors rather than merely dependents.", "title": "The Aging Population Challenge"}]}, {"no": 33, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中航天预算占比小的论述相反；C 与成本小、回报广的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中实际效益相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Space spending is the largest part of government budgets.", "Space exploration has no practical benefits.", "Space exploration is relatively cheap and produces wide practical and inspirational benefits.", "All space missions are failures."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中卫星作用相反；D 与天气预报、导航、通信和医学成像一致；B 与事实不符；C 与文中论述相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Space research has produced nothing useful.", "Only astronomers benefit from space programs.", "Space technology is too expensive to use.", "Satellites and space research have improved forecasting, navigation, and medicine."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what practical benefits has space exploration produced?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与地球照片重塑环保意识一致；B 与文中启发作用相悖；C 与国际合作的论述相反；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Images of Earth from space changed environmental awareness.", "Space exploration discourages science education.", "Space missions prevent international cooperation.", "Space has no cultural impact."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about the cultural impact of space exploration?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张按项目评估相反；B 与按科学价值和成本效益评估的建议一致；C 与文中批评管理不善相悖；D 与作者的平衡立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["All space spending should be stopped.", "Each mission should be judged on merit, cost, and social value.", "Space budgets should be unlimited.", "Poverty can be solved by cutting space budgets."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于极端；C 与既承认批评有力又肯定价值的立场一致；B 与讨论批评相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Space exploration is a waste of money.", "Space exploration should never be criticized.", "Space exploration is valuable but should be carefully evaluated.", "Space exploration alone will solve all problems."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward space exploration?"}], "text": "When governments spend billions of dollars sending probes to Mars or telescopes into deep space, critics often ask whether such money would be better spent on poverty, education, or healthcare at home. The question is understandable, yet it misunderstands both the scale of space spending and the returns it generates. In most countries, space budgets represent a tiny fraction of total public expenditure, far smaller than spending on defense or social programs, and the argument that space exploration diverts resources from urgent needs often exaggerates the trade-off. Moreover, investment in space has produced practical benefits that extend far beyond astronomy. Satellite technology underpins weather forecasting, disaster warning, navigation, and global communications, while research for space missions has led to advances in materials, computing, and medical imaging. The effort to keep astronauts alive in extreme environments has improved water purification, food preservation, and telemedicine, technologies that now serve people on Earth. Supporters also emphasize less tangible benefits. Space exploration inspires young people to study science and engineering, fosters international cooperation, and provides a long-term perspective on humanity's place in the universe. The famous image of Earth as a fragile blue sphere, photographed from space, reshaped environmental consciousness in ways that no policy report could achieve. Critics respond that these benefits could be obtained more cheaply through direct investment in science on Earth, and that grand missions often serve national prestige rather than genuine scientific need. They point to failed launches, cost overruns, and the risks faced by astronauts as evidence that enthusiasm sometimes outruns judgment. There is force in this criticism, particularly when projects are poorly managed. Yet abandoning space exploration would not solve poverty or inequality, whose causes lie in political and economic structures rather than in science budgets. A more reasonable approach is to evaluate each mission on its scientific merit, cost-effectiveness, and broader social value, rather than treating space as either a luxury or a panacea.", "title": "Should We Fund Space Exploration?"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中低完成率相反；D 与在线教育既扩大机会也有局限的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与混合模式的论述相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Online education works perfectly for everyone.", "Online courses always have high completion rates.", "Traditional classrooms are obsolete.", "Online education expands access but has serious limits."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与缺乏结构和自律要求一致；B 与文中社交缺失相悖；C 与课程质量无关；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Many learners lack structure and self-discipline.", "Online courses are too expensive.", "Online teachers are unqualified.", "Internet access is universal."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why are completion rates low?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中不平等加剧相反；B 与有资源者受益更多一致；C 与文中差距论述相悖；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Online learning eliminates all inequality.", "Students with better resources benefit more from online learning.", "All students benefit equally.", "Poor students always outperform rich students online."], "prompt": "What does the passage imply about inequality in online education?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张混合模式相反；C 与技术增强而非替代教学的建议一致；B 与文中评估难题相悖；D 与作者的建设性立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Replacing all teachers with videos.", "Abandoning online education entirely.", "Using technology to support human teaching in blended models.", "Ignoring student isolation."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which model?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既肯定潜力又强调局限的立场一致；B 与讨论局限相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Online education is worthless.", "Online education has no problems.", "Traditional education is perfect.", "Online education is promising but needs human support."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward online education?"}], "text": "The rapid expansion of online education, accelerated by the pandemic, has been hailed as a democratizing force, bringing university lectures, professional training, and language courses to anyone with an internet connection. Students in remote villages can now access courses from prestigious universities, working adults can study at their own pace, and institutions can reach far larger audiences at lower marginal cost. For many learners, this flexibility is transformative, allowing education to fit around work and family responsibilities rather than requiring relocation or full-time attendance. Yet experience has revealed significant limitations that temper early enthusiasm. Completion rates for massive open online courses are notoriously low, often below ten percent, suggesting that freedom without structure suits only highly motivated and self-disciplined learners. Many students miss the social dimension of learning: discussion with peers, immediate feedback from teachers, and the informal encouragement that comes from belonging to a learning community. Without these supports, isolation and procrastination can undermine even well-designed courses. Inequality also shapes outcomes. Students with reliable internet, quiet study space, and supportive families benefit far more from online learning than those without these advantages, potentially widening educational gaps rather than closing them. Assessment presents another challenge, as it is difficult to verify identity and prevent cheating in remote examinations, while automated grading often fails to capture creativity or critical thinking. None of this means online education is a failure. When combined with face-to-face teaching in blended models, or when designed with interactive elements, mentoring, and clear deadlines, online learning can be highly effective. The most successful programs treat technology as a tool to enhance human teaching rather than replace it, using online materials for content delivery and in-person time for discussion and problem-solving. The future of education is therefore likely to be hybrid, drawing on the accessibility of online platforms and the human connection of classrooms, rather than a wholesale shift to either extreme.", "title": "Online Education: Promise and Limits"}]}, {"no": 34, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了滥用导致耐药性这一全球威胁的主旨；B 过于乐观；C 与文中医院感染相反；D 与新药研发困难相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Antibiotic resistance is a growing global threat caused largely by misuse.", "Antibiotics will always work perfectly.", "Resistant bacteria only exist in laboratories.", "New antibiotics are easy to develop."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中农业使用相反；B 与医生过度处方和患者未完成疗程一致；C 与耐药机制相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Antibiotics are never prescribed.", "Overprescription, incomplete courses, and agricultural use accelerate resistance.", "Bacteria cannot evolve.", "Resistance is caused only by hospitals."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what accelerates antibiotic resistance?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中手术风险相反；C 与耐药感染使常规手术和化疗更危险一致；B 与威胁严重性相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Resistance affects only minor illnesses.", "Resistance has no effect on modern medicine.", "Resistant infections make surgery and chemotherapy more dangerous.", "Doctors no longer need antibiotics."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about the impact on modern medicine?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张综合行动相反；D 与合理用药、感染控制和新药研发的建议一致；B 与文中农业改革相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Using antibiotics for every cold.", "Banning all antibiotics immediately.", "Ignoring agricultural use.", "Combining stewardship, hygiene, and research investment."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃关切的语气；B 与全球威胁的论述不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and concerned.", "Light-hearted and amused.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Antibiotics rank among the greatest medical advances in history, transforming once-fatal infections into treatable conditions and making modern surgery, childbirth, and cancer therapy possible. Yet this triumph is increasingly threatened by the emergence of bacteria that no longer respond to standard drugs. The World Health Organization has warned that antibiotic resistance could become one of the leading causes of death worldwide, potentially reversing a century of medical progress. The causes are well documented. Doctors sometimes prescribe antibiotics for viral infections, where they are useless, while patients frequently stop taking their medication once symptoms improve, allowing the strongest bacteria to survive and multiply. In agriculture, large quantities of antibiotics are given to livestock to promote growth and prevent disease in crowded conditions, creating reservoirs of resistant organisms that can spread to humans through food, water, and the environment. The consequences are already visible in hospitals, where infections such as methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus require more toxic, expensive, and less effective treatments. Routine operations, including hip replacements and caesarean sections, become riskier when prophylactic antibiotics fail, and chemotherapy patients, whose immune systems are weakened, face greater danger from infections that once were easily controlled. Developing new antibiotics is slow and commercially unattractive, because drugs that should be used sparingly generate limited profits compared with medicines taken daily for chronic conditions. Many pharmaceutical companies have therefore abandoned antibiotic research, leaving the pipeline dangerously thin. Addressing the crisis requires coordinated action across medicine, agriculture, and policy. Stewardship programs can reduce unnecessary prescriptions, better diagnostics can distinguish bacterial from viral infections, and improved sanitation can prevent infections in the first place. Governments must also create incentives for research, such as prizes or guaranteed purchases, to revive development. The lesson is sobering: antibiotics are a shared resource, and their effectiveness depends on restraint today to preserve their power for future generations.", "title": "Antibiotic Resistance: A Silent Crisis"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中消费与幸福关系复杂相反；B 与消费主义既带来便利也有心理环境代价的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中身份认同论述相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Consumption always increases happiness.", "Consumerism offers convenience but also carries psychological and environmental costs.", "Advertising has no influence on people.", "Shopping is the only source of identity."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中研究相反；C 与物质主义者幸福感较低、适应性导致满足短暂一致；B 与证据相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Buying more always makes people happier.", "Wealth has no effect on wellbeing.", "Materialistic values are linked to lower wellbeing and short-lived satisfaction.", "Poor people are always unhappy."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what does research suggest about materialism?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中广告塑造欲望相反；D 与广告将产品与身份、地位关联一致；B 与营销作用相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Advertising only provides factual information.", "People never compare themselves to others.", "Advertising has disappeared.", "Advertising links products to identity and status."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about advertising?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与体验、关系和社区参与带来更持久满足一致；B 与作者主张反思消费相反；C 与文中可持续选择相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Experiences, relationships, and community engagement bring more lasting satisfaction.", "Buying luxury goods is the best path to happiness.", "Consumerism should be expanded without limits.", "People should never buy anything new."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which view?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者的分析语气不符；B 与平衡反思的语气一致；C 过于极端；D 未表现出愤怒。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Angry and hostile.", "Reflective and analytical.", "Completely indifferent.", "Uncritically celebratory."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Modern economies depend on consumption, and for many people shopping provides pleasure, comfort, and a sense of identity. Yet the relentless encouragement to buy more raises profound questions about wellbeing, equality, and environmental sustainability. Consumerism, the cultural belief that acquiring goods is central to happiness and success, has been amplified by advertising, social media, and easy credit. Advertisements do not merely inform consumers about products; they associate goods with youth, beauty, status, and belonging, creating desires that did not previously exist. Research in psychology offers a cautionary picture. While higher income improves wellbeing up to the point where basic needs are met, additional wealth beyond that threshold brings diminishing returns. People who prioritize materialistic values tend to report lower life satisfaction, weaker relationships, and greater anxiety than those who value experiences, community, and personal growth. The excitement of a new purchase typically fades quickly, a phenomenon known as hedonic adaptation, prompting further purchases in pursuit of the same feeling. The environmental costs are equally significant. Producing, transporting, and disposing of consumer goods consumes resources, generates waste, and contributes to climate change. Fast-changing trends encourage disposal rather than repair, turning durable products into short-lived commodities. Inequality also shapes consumption: for affluent households, shopping is a leisure activity, while for low-income families, rising expectations can create pressure to spend beyond their means. None of this implies that consumption itself is wrong. Goods and services can genuinely improve lives, from medicines to education to comfortable housing. The problem arises when consumption becomes the primary measure of a successful life, crowding out other sources of meaning. Some people respond through voluntary simplicity, minimalism, or the sharing economy, choosing experiences over possessions. Others advocate policies such as advertising regulation, product durability standards, and support for repair and reuse. The deeper challenge is cultural: learning to distinguish between needs that enhance wellbeing and desires manufactured by markets, and recognizing that a fulfilling life may depend less on what we own than on how we spend our time and whom we spend it with.", "title": "Consumerism and the Pursuit of Happiness"}]}, {"no": 35, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中波动和监管风险相反；C 与区块链既有创新也有局限的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与金融包容讨论相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Blockchain will replace all banks immediately.", "Blockchain has no useful applications.", "Blockchain offers innovation in finance but faces volatility, energy, and regulatory challenges.", "Cryptocurrencies are completely stable."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中跨境支付例子相反；D 与去中心化账本减少中介和欺诈风险一致；B 与技术原理相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Blockchain requires a central bank to operate.", "Blockchain makes all transactions anonymous.", "Blockchain is only used for speculation.", "A shared ledger can reduce intermediaries and fraud risk."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what is a potential benefit of blockchain?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与价格剧烈波动和投机一致；B 与文中风险相悖；C 与能源消耗的论述相反；D 与监管不确定性不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Cryptocurrency prices are highly volatile and speculative.", "All cryptocurrencies are backed by gold.", "Blockchain uses no energy.", "Regulation is fully settled worldwide."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about cryptocurrencies?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张谨慎评估相反；B 与按用例、风险和监管评估的建议一致；C 与文中金融包容潜力相悖；D 与作者的平衡立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Banning blockchain research entirely.", "Evaluating each use case on evidence, risk, and regulation.", "Investing life savings in crypto without research.", "Ignoring energy consumption."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于乐观；C 与既承认潜力又强调风险的立场一致；B 与讨论创新相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Blockchain is a proven success.", "Blockchain is a complete failure.", "Blockchain is promising but unproven and risky.", "Blockchain should never be studied."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward blockchain?"}], "text": "Blockchain technology, first introduced as the foundation of Bitcoin, has been described as both a revolutionary breakthrough and an overhyped bubble. At its core, a blockchain is a decentralized digital ledger in which transactions are recorded across many computers, making records difficult to alter or forge without detection. Supporters argue that this design could transform finance by reducing reliance on intermediaries, lowering transaction costs, and increasing transparency. Cross-border payments, which currently pass through multiple banks and take days to settle, could in principle be completed in minutes. Smart contracts, self-executing agreements coded onto a blockchain, could automate insurance claims, property transfers, and supply-chain tracking. For people without access to traditional banking, particularly in developing countries, blockchain-based services might offer a pathway to financial inclusion. Yet the technology faces substantial obstacles. Cryptocurrencies built on blockchains are notoriously volatile, with prices swinging dramatically in response to speculation, regulation, and market sentiment, making them unreliable as stores of value or everyday currencies. Some blockchain systems consume enormous amounts of energy, raising environmental concerns that sit uneasily with climate goals. Fraud, hacking, and the use of cryptocurrencies for illicit transactions have also damaged public trust, while regulators struggle to classify digital assets and protect consumers without stifling innovation. Moreover, many proposed blockchain applications could be achieved more simply with conventional databases, raising questions about whether decentralization justifies the added complexity and cost. The history of technology suggests that transformative innovations often follow a cycle of excitement, disappointment, and gradual maturity. The internet itself was once dismissed as a niche curiosity before becoming essential infrastructure. Blockchain may follow a similar path, finding durable value in specific niches rather than replacing entire financial systems. The wise response is therefore neither uncritical enthusiasm nor outright dismissal, but careful evaluation of each use case, honest assessment of costs and risks, and regulation that protects users while allowing experimentation to continue.", "title": "Blockchain: Revolution or Hype?"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中认知优势相反；D 与双语教育既有优势也有执行挑战的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中研究相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Bilingual education harms children's brains.", "All children become fluent easily.", "Bilingual programs have no challenges.", "Bilingual education offers cognitive and cultural benefits but requires careful implementation."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与注意力转换和问题解决优势一致；B 与研究证据相反；C 与文中双语优势相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Bilingual children often show stronger attention control and problem-solving.", "Bilingualism causes confusion permanently.", "Bilingual children learn more slowly in all subjects.", "Only adults benefit from bilingualism."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what cognitive benefit is associated with bilingualism?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中移民儿童语言流失相反；B 与家庭语言被贬低导致认同受损一致；C 与双语教育目标相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Bilingual programs always succeed.", "When home languages are devalued, children may lose cultural identity.", "Children never lose their first language.", "Schools should ban home languages."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about language and identity?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张双语教育相反；C 与优质师资、课程和家庭支持的建议一致；B 与文中挑战相悖；D 与作者的建设性立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Abandoning bilingual programs.", "Teaching only in English everywhere.", "Investing in trained teachers, curriculum, and family support.", "Ignoring the home language entirely."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既肯定优势又承认挑战的立场一致；B 与讨论执行难题相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Bilingual education is harmful.", "Bilingual education is easy to implement.", "Bilingual education has no value.", "Bilingual education is valuable but demanding."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward bilingual education?"}], "text": "In an increasingly interconnected world, the ability to speak more than one language is often described as an essential skill, yet bilingual education remains a subject of debate among parents, teachers, and policymakers. Research in cognitive science suggests that bilingual children develop certain advantages, including stronger attention control, greater flexibility in switching between tasks, and enhanced problem-solving skills. Managing two languages appears to exercise the brain's executive functions, much as physical exercise strengthens muscles. Bilingualism also opens cultural doors, allowing children to communicate with grandparents, participate in diverse communities, and access literature and media in multiple languages. For immigrant families, maintaining the home language can preserve identity and family bonds that might otherwise weaken across generations. Despite these benefits, bilingual programs face practical challenges. Effective teaching requires well-trained teachers who are fluent in both languages, carefully designed curricula, and sufficient instructional time. When programs are poorly implemented, with inadequate resources or unclear goals, students may struggle in both languages rather than mastering either. Social attitudes also matter: if a minority language is viewed as inferior, children may feel ashamed to use it, undermining both learning and self-esteem. Some critics argue that time spent on a second language detracts from mastery of the dominant language, particularly for children who need to succeed in national examinations. Evidence, however, generally shows that strong bilingual programs do not harm academic achievement in the long term, and may even enhance it, provided that both languages are taught systematically. The most successful models, such as dual-language immersion, aim for proficiency in both languages rather than treating one as a temporary bridge to the other. They also involve families and communities, recognizing that language learning extends beyond the classroom. Ultimately, bilingual education is not merely a pedagogical choice but a statement about what kind of society we wish to build: one that values linguistic diversity as an asset, or one that treats it as an obstacle to be overcome.", "title": "Bilingual Education: Opportunity and Challenge"}]}, {"no": 36, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了海洋塑料的规模、危害与系统治理需求的主旨；B 与文中清理局限相反；C 与微塑料证据相悖；D 与全球性论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Ocean plastic pollution is a large-scale problem requiring systemic action.", "Ocean cleanup alone will solve plastic pollution.", "Microplastics are harmless to marine life.", "Plastic pollution affects only one ocean."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中河流输送相反；B 与河流、渔具和陆地垃圾一致；C 与塑料来源相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Plastic in the ocean comes only from ships.", "Rivers, fishing gear, and land waste carry plastic to the sea.", "Plastic is created in the ocean.", "No plastic comes from land."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how does plastic reach the ocean?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与微塑料进入食物链相反；C 与微塑料被摄入并进入食物链一致；B 与研究证据相悖；D 与文中健康担忧不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Marine animals never eat plastic.", "Microplastics disappear quickly.", "Microplastics are ingested by marine life and enter food chains.", "Plastic pollution has no effect on humans."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about microplastics?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张减少生产相反；D 与减产、回收、替代材料和国际协议的建议一致；B 与文中清理局限相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Producing more single-use plastic.", "Relying only on ocean cleanup.", "Ignoring plastic waste entirely.", "Reducing production, improving recycling, and agreeing on global rules."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃关切的语气；B 与环境议题不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and concerned.", "Humorous and light-hearted.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Each year, an estimated eight million tonnes of plastic enter the oceans, joining vast accumulations that already circulate in gyres, wash onto remote beaches, and sink to the seabed. The scale of the problem is difficult to grasp: by some estimates, the weight of plastic in the ocean could exceed the weight of fish by 2050 if current trends continue. Plastic reaches the sea through many pathways. Rivers carry waste from inland cities, fishing nets and gear are lost or discarded at sea, and inadequate waste management in coastal regions allows litter to escape into waterways. Once in the ocean, plastic does not biodegrade but breaks into ever smaller fragments, known as microplastics, which are ingested by plankton, fish, seabirds, and turtles. These particles can block digestive systems, reduce feeding, and carry toxic chemicals into food chains, with uncertain consequences for human health as seafood is consumed. Large debris also poses direct threats, entangling seals, dolphins, and seabirds, while floating plastic transports invasive species across oceans. Cleanup efforts, including ocean-cleaning devices and beach cleanups, attract considerable attention, yet they address only a fraction of the waste already present and cannot keep pace with the flow of new plastic. The most effective solutions therefore lie upstream: reducing production of single-use plastics, designing products for reuse and recycling, improving waste collection, and developing biodegradable alternatives where appropriate. International negotiations for a global plastics treaty reflect growing recognition that no single country can solve a problem that crosses borders and involves global supply chains. Consumers also have a role, through reducing consumption, choosing reusable products, and supporting companies that take responsibility for their packaging. Ultimately, ocean plastic pollution is not merely a litter problem but a symptom of a linear economy that treats durable materials as disposable. Addressing it requires rethinking how plastics are produced, used, and valued, before the oceans become irreversibly altered.", "title": "Plastic in the Ocean"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中AI工具用途相反；B 与AI既辅助创作也有原创性争议的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中艺术家担忧相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["AI has no role in art.", "AI tools assist creation but raise questions about originality and authorship.", "AI art is identical to human art.", "Artists universally welcome AI."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中训练数据来源相反；C 与使用大量人类作品训练一致；B 与版权争议相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["AI creates art without any data.", "AI art is always original.", "AI models are trained on large collections of human-created works.", "AI never copies existing styles."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how are AI art models trained?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中意图讨论相反；D 与人类创作依赖意图、经验和文化语境一致；B 与作者观点相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["AI and humans create in exactly the same way.", "Creativity requires no intention.", "AI understands cultural meaning fully.", "Human creativity depends on intention, experience, and cultural context."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about human creativity?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与将AI视为工具而非替代者的建议一致；B 与作者主张合作相反；C 与文中版权讨论相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Treating AI as a tool that extends human creativity.", "Banning all AI art immediately.", "Ignoring questions of authorship.", "Claiming AI art has no value."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which view?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者的分析语气不符；B 与平衡反思的语气一致；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Angry and hostile.", "Thoughtful and analytical.", "Completely indifferent.", "Uncritically celebratory."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "When an artificial intelligence system produces a painting, poem, or piece of music that moves audiences, it challenges long-held assumptions about creativity as a uniquely human capacity. Modern generative models are trained on vast collections of human-created works, learning patterns of style, structure, and color, and then producing new combinations that can be strikingly original in appearance. For many artists, these tools offer exciting possibilities: a writer can use AI to overcome a block, a designer can rapidly generate variations, and a musician can explore harmonies that might never have occurred to them. Yet the rise of AI art also provokes anxiety about authorship, originality, and livelihood. If a model trained on thousands of living artists' works can imitate their styles without permission or payment, questions of copyright and fairness become urgent. Some artists have discovered their distinctive styles reproduced by AI systems, with no compensation or acknowledgment, prompting lawsuits and calls for regulation. Philosophers debate whether AI can truly be creative, or whether it merely recombines existing patterns without intention, experience, or understanding. Human creativity, they argue, is shaped by lived experience, emotion, and cultural context, qualities that a statistical model, however sophisticated, does not possess. Others respond that human artists also learn by imitating predecessors, and that creativity has always involved recombination. The distinction may lie less in the output than in the process and the meaning attributed to it. An AI-generated image may be beautiful, but it does not know why it chose particular forms, nor does it bear responsibility for its message. This does not make AI art worthless, but it suggests that its value depends on how humans use it. As a collaborator, AI can extend human imagination; as a replacement, it risks devaluing the labor and meaning behind artistic work. The challenge for society is to develop norms that reward human creators, ensure transparency about AI involvement, and preserve space for art that expresses authentic human experience.", "title": "Can Machines Really Be Creative?"}]}, {"no": 37, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中分配问题相反；C 与粮食安全既是产量也是分配、收入和气候问题的主旨一致；B 过于简单化；D 与文中浪费论述相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Food security depends only on total production.", "Hunger has been eliminated worldwide.", "Food security involves production, distribution, income, and climate resilience.", "Climate change has no effect on farming."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中分配不均相反；D 与食物充足但贫困和分配导致饥饿一致；B 与事实不符；C 与文中论述相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["The world produces too little food in total.", "Hunger is caused only by drought.", "All countries have equal access to food.", "Enough food is produced, but poverty and poor distribution leave many hungry."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why does hunger persist despite sufficient production?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与气候变化威胁产量和营养一致；B 与文中证据相反；C 与气候影响相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Climate change threatens yields, nutrition, and farming livelihoods.", "Climate change will improve all farming.", "Farmers are unaffected by weather.", "Irrigation solves all climate risks."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about climate change and food?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张综合措施相反；B 与提高产量、减少浪费和社会保障的建议一致；C 与文中气候适应相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Relying only on more fertilizer.", "Combining sustainable farming, waste reduction, and social protection.", "Ignoring smallholder farmers.", "Banning all food trade."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于悲观；C 与既承认挑战又强调可行方案的立场一致；B 与讨论饥饿相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Hunger is inevitable.", "Food security is already achieved.", "Food security is a solvable challenge requiring coordinated action.", "Technology alone will solve hunger."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward food security?"}], "text": "Despite producing enough food to feed the entire global population, the world continues to face widespread hunger and malnutrition, a paradox that reveals food security as far more than a question of agricultural output. Nearly one in ten people suffers from chronic undernourishment, while many more experience seasonal food shortages or lack access to nutritious diets. The reasons are complex and interconnected. Poverty is the most immediate cause: even where food is available in markets, millions cannot afford it, particularly when prices rise due to conflict, inflation, or supply disruptions. Inequality in land ownership, limited access to credit and technology, and inadequate infrastructure further disadvantage smallholder farmers, who produce a large share of food in developing countries yet often remain food insecure themselves. Climate change adds another layer of vulnerability, as droughts, floods, and heatwaves reduce yields, damage crops, and undermine the livelihoods of farming communities. At the same time, approximately one third of all food produced is lost or wasted, through poor storage and transport in developing countries and through consumer waste in wealthy nations. This waste represents not only lost calories but also squandered water, land, and energy. Addressing food security therefore requires action on multiple fronts. Sustainable farming practices, including drought-resistant crops, efficient irrigation, and soil conservation, can increase resilience while protecting ecosystems. Reducing waste, improving storage and transport, and strengthening social protection programs can ensure that food reaches those who need it most. Trade policies also matter, as open and fair trade can stabilize supplies, while export bans during crises can worsen shortages elsewhere. Ultimately, food security is inseparable from broader questions of justice, governance, and environmental sustainability. A world that can produce enough food yet allows millions to go hungry is not facing a technical failure, but a failure of distribution, priorities, and political will.", "title": "Food Security in a Hungry World"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中差距扩大相反；D 与远程学习既扩大机会也加剧不平等的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中设备和网络差距相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Remote learning benefits all students equally.", "Remote learning has no disadvantages.", "Only wealthy students use remote learning.", "Remote learning expands access but can deepen inequality."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与设备、网络、空间和家庭支持差距一致；B 与文中不平等相悖；C 与事实不符；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Students differ in devices, internet, study space, and family support.", "All students have identical home conditions.", "Internet access is universal.", "Teachers are the only factor."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why do remote learning outcomes differ?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中教师培训重要相反；B 与教师培训、互动设计和支持服务一致；C 与文中论述相悖；D 与作者主张相反。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Technology alone guarantees success.", "Teacher training, interactive design, and student support matter greatly.", "Schools should ignore student wellbeing.", "Remote learning needs no planning."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about successful remote learning?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张缩小差距相反；C 与提供设备、补贴网络和线下支持的建议一致；B 与文中不平等相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Leaving disadvantaged students without help.", "Banning remote learning entirely.", "Providing devices, connectivity, and offline support.", "Assuming all families can afford technology."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既肯定潜力又强调公平的立场一致；B 与讨论不平等相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Remote learning is worthless.", "Remote learning has no problems.", "Inequality does not matter.", "Remote learning must be designed for equity."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward remote learning?"}], "text": "The shift to remote learning during the pandemic revealed both the potential and the pitfalls of education delivered through screens. On one hand, digital platforms allowed lessons to continue when schools were closed, reaching students in their homes and offering flexibility that traditional classrooms could not match. Recorded lectures could be replayed, resources could be shared instantly, and students could learn at their own pace. On the other hand, the experience exposed deep inequalities that had previously been less visible. Students from affluent families often had personal laptops, high-speed internet, quiet rooms, and parents able to help with homework, while students from poorer households shared a single phone, struggled with unstable connections, or lacked a suitable place to study. These differences translated directly into learning outcomes, with disadvantaged students falling further behind during extended periods of remote instruction. Teachers also faced challenges, needing to redesign lessons for online delivery, maintain engagement through a screen, and identify students who were struggling without the cues available in person. Many lacked training or technical support, and the burden fell unevenly across schools. The social and emotional dimensions of schooling were also diminished: friendships, teamwork, and informal support from teachers are difficult to replicate online, and prolonged isolation affected motivation and mental health. None of these problems means remote learning should be abandoned. Rather, they highlight the conditions under which it can succeed: reliable devices and connectivity for all students, training and support for teachers, interactive and well-structured materials, and deliberate efforts to maintain community and wellbeing. Policymakers can address equity through device lending programs, subsidized internet, and community learning hubs where students can access technology and support. The pandemic offered a natural experiment in remote education, and its most important lesson is not that technology can replace schools, but that education systems must be designed for equity from the outset, ensuring that innovation benefits all students rather than widening existing gaps.", "title": "Remote Learning and Educational Equity"}]}, {"no": 38, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了电动车既减排也有电池与电网挑战的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中减排潜力相反；D 与成本下降论述相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Electric vehicles can reduce emissions but face battery, cost, and grid challenges.", "Electric vehicles produce no emissions at all.", "Electric vehicles will never become affordable.", "Electric vehicles are worse than petrol cars in every way."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中制造排放相反；B 与电池制造、采矿和电力来源影响总排放一致；C 与生命周期分析相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Electric vehicles have no environmental impact.", "Battery production, mining, and electricity sources affect total emissions.", "Electric vehicles use no resources.", "Charging always uses clean energy."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why is the environmental benefit of EVs not automatic?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中充电基础设施不足相反；C 与充电桩、续航焦虑和电网升级一致；B 与成本下降相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Electric vehicles never need charging.", "Electric vehicles are already cheap for everyone.", "Charging infrastructure, range anxiety, and grid capacity limit adoption.", "Governments oppose electric vehicles."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about barriers to EV adoption?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张综合政策相反；D 与补贴、充电网络和清洁电网的建议一致；B 与文中回收讨论相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Banning all petrol cars overnight.", "Ignoring battery recycling.", "Relying only on market forces.", "Combining subsidies, charging networks, and cleaner electricity."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与讨论挑战不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely descriptive.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Electric vehicles have moved from niche products to mainstream alternatives, driven by falling battery costs, government incentives, and growing concern about climate change. Unlike petrol cars, which emit carbon dioxide directly from their exhausts, electric vehicles produce no tailpipe emissions, improving air quality in cities and reducing greenhouse gas emissions when charged with clean electricity. In countries where renewable energy is expanding, the climate benefit is substantial, and even where electricity still comes partly from fossil fuels, electric vehicles are often more efficient overall. Yet their environmental advantage is not automatic. Manufacturing batteries requires lithium, cobalt, and nickel, whose mining can damage ecosystems and raise ethical concerns about labor conditions. Battery production is energy-intensive, meaning that an electric vehicle starts its life with a larger carbon footprint than a conventional car, which it must offset through years of cleaner driving. The source of electricity matters greatly: charging with coal-fired power reduces the benefit, while charging with solar or wind maximizes it. Adoption also faces practical barriers. Electric vehicles remain more expensive upfront than comparable petrol cars, although lower running costs narrow the gap over time. Charging infrastructure is uneven, with rural areas and apartment dwellers facing particular difficulties, and concerns about driving range persist despite improvements. Grids must be upgraded to handle increased demand, especially if millions of vehicles charge simultaneously in the evening. Recycling batteries presents both a challenge and an opportunity, as valuable materials can be recovered but processes are still developing. Policymakers therefore face a complex task: subsidies and charging networks can accelerate adoption, but they must be paired with cleaner electricity, responsible mining, and recycling systems. Electric vehicles are a crucial part of decarbonizing transport, but they are not a complete solution on their own; public transport, cycling, and urban planning must also play a role in reducing overall demand for car travel.", "title": "Electric Vehicles: A Cleaner Future?"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中多元化不只是数字相反；B 与多元化既带来创新也有冲突与执行挑战的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中包容文化论述相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Diversity is only about meeting quotas.", "Workplace diversity can improve innovation but requires inclusive culture and management.", "Diverse teams always outperform others.", "Diversity has no effect on business."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中研究证据相反；C 与不同背景带来不同视角和创意一致；B 与创新论述相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Diverse teams think in exactly the same way.", "Diversity makes decision-making slower in all cases.", "Different backgrounds bring varied perspectives that can improve creativity.", "Homogeneous teams are always more innovative."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why might diverse teams be more creative?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中无包容则多元化失效相反；D 与包容文化、公平晋升和偏见培训一致；B 与执行挑战相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Hiring diverse staff is enough on its own.", "Diversity programs never work.", "Inclusion is irrelevant to diversity.", "Inclusive culture, fair promotion, and bias training are needed."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about making diversity work?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与领导层承诺和问责的建议一致；B 与作者主张结构改革相反；C 与文中多元化价值相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Leadership commitment, clear goals, and accountability help diversity succeed.", "Companies should abandon diversity efforts.", "Only recruitment matters.", "Diversity should be a slogan without action."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既肯定价值又强调执行困难的立场一致；C 与讨论挑战相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about diversity.", "Cautiously optimistic, recognizing benefits and implementation challenges.", "Indifferent to workplace diversity.", "Hostile toward diverse teams."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward workplace diversity?"}], "text": "Workplace diversity has become a central goal for many organizations, motivated by both moral arguments about fairness and business arguments about performance. A workforce that includes people of different genders, ethnicities, ages, and backgrounds is more likely to reflect the customers it serves, understand varied markets, and avoid the blind spots that arise when everyone thinks alike. Research suggests that diverse teams can be more creative, as different perspectives challenge assumptions and generate a wider range of ideas. Yet diversity alone does not guarantee these benefits. Without an inclusive culture, diverse employees may feel marginalized, their ideas ignored, and their careers limited by invisible barriers. Tokenism, hiring a few individuals from underrepresented groups without changing power structures, can create resentment and reinforce stereotypes rather than dismantling them. Communication challenges can also arise, as different cultural norms around directness, hierarchy, and decision-making may lead to misunderstandings if not managed thoughtfully. Successful diversity therefore requires more than recruitment. It demands fair promotion practices, mentoring, training to recognize unconscious bias, and leadership that models inclusive behavior. Organizations must also measure progress, setting clear goals and holding managers accountable, rather than treating diversity as a public relations exercise. Critics argue that diversity programs can be divisive, prioritizing identity over merit, while supporters respond that merit itself is shaped by unequal opportunities, and that ignoring background perpetuates existing advantages. The evidence suggests that the most effective programs focus on inclusion and belonging, creating environments where all employees can contribute fully, rather than simply counting demographic categories. When employees feel genuinely included, they are more likely to share ideas, collaborate across differences, and remain committed to their organization over the long term. Ultimately, workplace diversity is not a quick fix but a long-term commitment to building organizations that are both fairer and more capable of adapting to a diverse and rapidly changing world.", "title": "Workplace Diversity and Inclusion"}]}, {"no": 39, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中监控扩张相反；C 与监控既提升安全也有隐私与权力风险的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中面部识别论述相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Surveillance technology has no benefits.", "Surveillance is always used fairly.", "Surveillance can improve safety but threatens privacy and concentrates power.", "Only criminals are affected by surveillance."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中监控误判相反；D 与面部识别对少数群体错误率更高一致；B 与研究证据相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Facial recognition works equally well for everyone.", "Surveillance systems never make mistakes.", "Governments never misuse surveillance.", "Facial recognition is less accurate for some groups, raising fairness concerns."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what is a problem with facial recognition?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与人们在被监视时自我审查一致；B 与寒蝉效应相反；C 与文中论述相悖；D 与民主参与的担忧不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["People may self-censor when they know they are watched.", "Surveillance encourages free expression.", "Privacy has no value in public spaces.", "Surveillance never affects behavior."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about the chilling effect?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张限制相反；B 与透明度、独立监督和法律限制的建议一致；C 与文中权力制衡相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Allowing unlimited surveillance without oversight.", "Requiring transparency, independent oversight, and legal limits.", "Banning all cameras everywhere.", "Trusting companies without regulation."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于极端；C 与既承认安全价值又强调隐私风险的立场一致；B 与讨论滥用相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Surveillance should never be criticized.", "Surveillance is purely beneficial.", "Surveillance is a trade-off requiring careful limits.", "Privacy is unimportant in modern society."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward surveillance?"}], "text": "Cameras now watch city streets, workplaces, and schools, while smartphones, social media, and online services track location, browsing, and communication on an unprecedented scale. Governments and companies argue that such surveillance is necessary for public safety, crime prevention, and efficient services. CCTV can deter theft, help find missing persons, and provide evidence in criminal investigations. During health emergencies, location data has been used to trace contacts and limit the spread of disease. Yet the expansion of surveillance raises fundamental questions about privacy, freedom, and power. When people know they are being watched, they may alter their behavior, avoiding protests, unpopular opinions, or unconventional lifestyles, a phenomenon known as the chilling effect. Facial recognition technology adds particular concerns: studies have shown that some systems are less accurate for women and people with darker skin, increasing the risk of wrongful identification and reinforcing existing inequalities. Data collected for one purpose may be repurposed for another without consent, as information gathered for security is later used for marketing, employment screening, or political monitoring. The concentration of surveillance capabilities in the hands of a few governments or corporations creates an imbalance of power that is difficult to reverse. Once infrastructure is built and norms shift, surveillance tends to expand rather than contract, a pattern sometimes called function creep. Regulation has struggled to keep pace. Some jurisdictions require warrants, transparency reports, and independent oversight, while others impose few limits. The debate is not simply between safety and privacy, as if they were opposing values, but about what kind of society surveillance creates. A society that accepts constant monitoring may become more orderly in the short term, but it may also become less free, less creative, and less trusting. The challenge is to design systems that provide genuine security while preserving autonomy, through clear legal limits, democratic oversight, and technology that minimizes data collection by default.", "title": "Surveillance in the Digital Age"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中绿色建筑不只是外观相反；D 与绿色建筑既节能减排也有成本与执行挑战的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中健康效益相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Green buildings are only about appearance.", "Green buildings always cost the same as ordinary buildings.", "Green buildings have no health benefits.", "Green buildings reduce energy and emissions but face cost and implementation challenges."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与隔热、自然采光、通风和可再生能源一致；B 与绿色设计原理相悖；C 与文中节能措施相反；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Insulation, natural light, ventilation, and renewable energy reduce energy use.", "Green buildings use more energy deliberately.", "Green buildings ignore occupant comfort.", "Green buildings never use local materials."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how do green buildings save energy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中室内空气质量相反；B 与自然光、新鲜空气和无毒材料改善健康一致；C 与研究证据相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Green buildings make occupants sicker.", "Natural light, fresh air, and non-toxic materials can improve wellbeing.", "Building design has no effect on health.", "Occupants prefer dark, sealed buildings."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about green buildings and health?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张推广绿色建筑相反；C 与标准、激励和全生命周期评估的建议一致；B 与文中成本讨论相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Abandoning green building standards.", "Building only luxury green homes.", "Using standards, incentives, and lifecycle assessment to spread green design.", "Ignoring existing buildings entirely."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既肯定效益又承认成本障碍的立场一致；B 与讨论挑战相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Green buildings are a waste of money.", "Green buildings have no challenges.", "Green buildings are perfect already.", "Green buildings are valuable but need support to become mainstream."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward green architecture?"}], "text": "Buildings account for nearly forty percent of global energy consumption and a similar share of carbon emissions, making architecture a crucial frontier in the fight against climate change. Green buildings aim to reduce this impact through design, materials, and technology. Orientation, insulation, and shading can minimize the need for heating and cooling, while natural light and ventilation reduce reliance on artificial lighting and air conditioning. Solar panels, heat pumps, and rainwater harvesting further lower resource use, and the choice of materials, such as recycled steel, sustainably sourced timber, and low-carbon concrete, can significantly reduce embodied emissions. Beyond environmental benefits, green buildings often improve occupant health and productivity. Better air quality, natural light, and thermal comfort have been linked to fewer sick days and higher satisfaction among workers and students. Yet the transition to green architecture faces obstacles. Upfront costs are often higher, even if savings over a building's lifetime outweigh the initial investment, and developers focused on short-term returns may be reluctant to pay more. Certification systems such as LEED and BREEAM provide standards and recognition, but they can also become box-ticking exercises if design prioritizes points over genuine performance. Retrofitting existing buildings, which constitute the vast majority of the building stock, is particularly challenging, as older structures were not designed for energy efficiency. In rapidly urbanizing countries, the challenge is even greater, as millions of new buildings are constructed each year, locking in energy patterns for decades. Policy can accelerate change through building codes, tax incentives, and public procurement that favors sustainable design. Education also matters: architects, engineers, and construction workers need training in new techniques and materials. Ultimately, green architecture is not merely a technical specialty but a rethinking of how buildings relate to their environment and inhabitants, balancing comfort, beauty, and ecological responsibility in structures that will shape daily life for generations.", "title": "Green Architecture: Building for the Future"}]}, {"no": 40, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了疫情暴露准备不足与需系统投资的主旨；B 与文中全球合作相反；C 与文中不平等论述相悖；D 与事实不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Pandemics revealed gaps in preparedness and the need for systemic investment.", "Pandemics are no longer a threat.", "All countries responded equally well.", "Vaccines alone solve all pandemic problems."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中供应链中断相反；B 与医疗物资短缺和供应链脆弱一致；C 与疫情影响相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Hospitals had unlimited supplies.", "Shortages of protective equipment and fragile supply chains worsened the crisis.", "Supply chains were unaffected.", "Only wealthy countries faced shortages."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what worsened the pandemic response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中疫苗分配不均相反；C 与富国先获得疫苗、穷国等待更久一致；B 与不平等证据相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Vaccines were distributed equally worldwide.", "Poor countries received vaccines first.", "Wealthy countries secured vaccines earlier while poorer nations waited longer.", "Vaccine distribution was irrelevant."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about vaccine equity?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张长期投资相反；D 与监测、储备、研发和国际合作的建议一致；B 与文中准备重要性相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Waiting until the next pandemic begins.", "Cutting public health budgets.", "Ignoring international cooperation.", "Investing in surveillance, stockpiles, research, and cooperation."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃关切的语气；B 与全球威胁论述不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and concerned.", "Humorous and light-hearted.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "The COVID-19 pandemic was not an unforeseeable catastrophe but a warning that experts had issued for decades, yet most countries were caught unprepared when it arrived. Hospitals faced shortages of protective equipment, ventilators, and intensive care beds, while supply chains for essential goods proved fragile under sudden demand. Testing systems were slow to scale, contact tracing was overwhelmed, and public health messages were undermined by misinformation and political division. The economic consequences were equally severe, as lockdowns disrupted businesses, schools, and livelihoods, with the poorest and most vulnerable bearing the heaviest burden. Yet the pandemic also demonstrated remarkable scientific capacity: vaccines were developed in less than a year, a feat previously thought impossible, and digital technologies enabled remote work and education on a massive scale. The distribution of those vaccines, however, exposed deep global inequalities, as wealthy countries secured doses early while poorer nations waited months or years, prolonging the pandemic and allowing new variants to emerge. Preparedness for future pandemics therefore requires more than stockpiling masks. It demands sustained investment in surveillance systems that can detect outbreaks early, in healthcare infrastructure that can surge when needed, and in research that can rapidly develop diagnostics, treatments, and vaccines. International cooperation is essential, since pathogens do not respect borders, and a weak response in one country endangers all others. Equally important is building public trust, through transparent communication, community engagement, and policies that protect livelihoods during health emergencies. The cost of preparedness is substantial, but the cost of unpreparedness, measured in lives lost, economies damaged, and education disrupted, is far greater. Regular exercises, clear communication plans, and funding that survives political cycles are essential. Societies must decide whether to treat pandemic preparedness as an ongoing commitment or to forget the lessons of COVID-19 until the next crisis forces them to remember.", "title": "Preparing for the Next Pandemic"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中差距持续相反；B 与数字鸿沟既是接入也是技能与使用差异的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中教育和就业影响相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["The digital divide has disappeared.", "The digital divide involves access, skills, and meaningful use.", "Only rural areas lack internet.", "Digital skills do not matter."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中技能差距相反；C 与设备、网络和数字素养差异一致；B 与不平等论述相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Everyone uses the internet in the same way.", "Internet access is identical everywhere.", "Differences in devices, connectivity, and digital literacy create unequal outcomes.", "Age has no effect on digital use."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what creates unequal digital outcomes?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中弱势群体受影响更大相反；D 与低收入和农村社区更可能掉队一致；B 与证据相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Digital exclusion affects only wealthy people.", "The digital divide has no economic consequences.", "Technology automatically benefits everyone.", "Low-income and rural communities are most at risk of exclusion."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about who is most affected?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与基础设施、补贴、培训和包容设计相结合的建议一致；B 与作者主张综合措施相反；C 与文中技能培训相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Combining infrastructure, affordable access, training, and inclusive design.", "Providing internet alone without any support.", "Ignoring digital literacy.", "Assuming the market will solve everything."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既承认差距又强调可缩小差距的立场一致；C 与讨论不平等相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about technology.", "Cautiously optimistic, seeing a solvable challenge.", "Indifferent to digital inequality.", "Hostile toward the internet."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward the digital divide?"}], "text": "Access to the internet is increasingly essential for education, employment, healthcare, and civic participation, yet billions of people remain offline or underconnected, creating a digital divide that mirrors and reinforces existing inequalities. The divide is not simply a matter of having a connection or not. It encompasses the quality of devices, the speed and reliability of networks, the skills needed to use digital tools effectively, and the ability to benefit from online opportunities. A student with a shared smartphone and intermittent mobile data faces very different prospects from one with a personal laptop and broadband, even if both are technically online. In rural areas, infrastructure gaps leave communities isolated, while in cities, affordability can exclude low-income households from essential services that have moved online. Older adults, people with disabilities, and those with limited literacy may also struggle to navigate digital systems designed without their needs in mind. The consequences extend beyond convenience. During the pandemic, students without adequate access fell behind in their studies, workers without digital skills found it harder to find jobs, and patients without telemedicine access faced greater health risks. As governments digitize public services, those who cannot access them risk being left behind in fundamental aspects of citizenship. Closing the divide requires more than laying cables. Affordable devices, subsidized connectivity, and community access points can help, but digital literacy training is equally important, enabling people to evaluate information, protect their privacy, and use technology confidently. Design also matters: services should be accessible, multilingual, and usable on low-cost devices. The digital divide is often framed as a technical problem, but it is fundamentally a social one, reflecting who has resources, education, and power. Addressing it is therefore not only about technology, but about ensuring that the benefits of the digital age are shared by all rather than concentrated among the already connected.", "title": "The Digital Divide"}]}, {"no": 41, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中线性经济浪费相反；C 与循环经济既减少浪费也有设计与商业挑战的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中回收局限相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["The linear economy is sustainable forever.", "Recycling alone solves all waste problems.", "The circular economy aims to keep materials in use but faces design and business challenges.", "Products should never be reused."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中提取制造丢弃模式相反；D 与取用制造丢弃一致；B 与线性模式相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Products are designed to last forever.", "Materials are always recycled.", "Waste is never produced.", "Resources are extracted, products are made, and then discarded."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what characterizes the linear economy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与耐用设计、维修和共享模式一致；B 与循环原则相反；C 与文中商业模式相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Designing durable, repairable products and sharing models supports circularity.", "Making products impossible to repair.", "Encouraging single-use consumption.", "Ignoring product design entirely."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about circular design?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张政策支持相反；B 与生产者责任、标准和激励的建议一致；C 与文中系统变革相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Leaving circularity entirely to consumers.", "Using producer responsibility, standards, and incentives.", "Banning all manufacturing.", "Relying only on recycling bins."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于乐观；C 与既肯定潜力又承认转型困难的立场一致；B 与讨论创新相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["The circular economy is already fully achieved.", "The circular economy is impossible.", "The circular economy is promising but requires systemic change.", "Waste is not a problem."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward the circular economy?"}], "text": "For most of industrial history, economies have followed a linear pattern: extract raw materials, manufacture products, and discard them after use. This take-make-waste model has delivered unprecedented material abundance, but it has also depleted resources, generated vast quantities of waste, and contributed to pollution and climate change. The circular economy offers an alternative vision, in which products, materials, and resources are kept in use for as long as possible, through design for durability, repair, reuse, remanufacturing, and recycling. A circular smartphone, for example, might be designed so its battery can be replaced, its components upgraded, and its materials recovered at the end of its life, rather than being discarded after two years. Companies are experimenting with new business models, such as leasing products instead of selling them, offering repair services, and selling refurbished goods. These models can create value while reducing material consumption, as manufacturers retain ownership and have incentives to make products last. Consumers also play a role, through buying second-hand goods, repairing rather than replacing, and sharing items that are used infrequently. Yet the transition faces significant obstacles. Many products are designed for obsolescence, with glued batteries, proprietary parts, and limited spare parts making repair difficult or expensive. Recycling, while important, is often inefficient, with mixed materials, contamination, and low market prices for recovered materials limiting its effectiveness. Economic incentives also favor virgin materials, whose environmental costs are not reflected in their prices. Policy can help by setting durability standards, requiring producers to take responsibility for end-of-life products, and supporting markets for recycled materials. The circular economy is not merely a waste management strategy but a fundamental rethinking of production and consumption. Its success will depend on whether businesses, governments, and consumers can align incentives to value longevity over disposability, and whether innovation can deliver products that serve human needs without exhausting the planet's finite resources.", "title": "The Circular Economy"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中习惯自动化相反；D 与习惯既节省认知资源也有改变困难的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中环境线索论述相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Habits require constant conscious effort.", "Habits have no effect on behavior.", "Willpower alone always changes habits.", "Habits automate behavior, saving mental effort but making change difficult."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与重复行为与稳定线索关联一致；B 与习惯形成机制相反；C 与文中研究相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Repeated behavior linked to stable cues creates habits.", "Habits form instantly without repetition.", "Habits depend only on motivation.", "Environment has no role in habits."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how do habits form?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中意志力有限相反；B 与改变环境线索比依赖意志力更有效一致；C 与研究证据相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Relying only on willpower is the best strategy.", "Changing environmental cues is often more effective than willpower.", "Habits cannot be changed at all.", "Motivation never matters."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about changing habits?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张小步骤和环境设计相反；C 与小而具体、可重复的行动和环境支持的建议一致；B 与文中习惯机制相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Making dramatic changes overnight.", "Ignoring the environment entirely.", "Starting with small, specific, repeatable actions supported by environment design.", "Waiting for motivation to appear."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author recommends which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既承认习惯强大又强调可塑性的立场一致；B 与讨论改变困难相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Habits are unchangeable.", "Habits are unimportant.", "Willpower is unlimited.", "Habits are powerful but changeable with the right approach."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward habit change?"}], "text": "Much of daily life is governed not by deliberate decisions but by habits, automatic behaviors triggered by familiar cues in the environment. From the moment we wake up, routines guide us through brushing teeth, commuting, checking phones, and eating meals, often without conscious thought. Psychologists estimate that more than forty percent of everyday actions are habitual rather than intentional, a fact that reveals both the power and the danger of habits. Habits form through repetition: when a behavior is consistently performed in response to a stable cue, such as exercising after breakfast or scrolling through social media when bored, the brain gradually automates the sequence, freeing mental resources for other tasks. This efficiency is invaluable, allowing us to navigate complex days without exhausting our limited capacity for attention and willpower. Yet the same mechanism makes unhealthy habits difficult to break. A person who automatically reaches for a cigarette after meals or snacks while watching television is not simply lacking willpower; they are responding to deeply ingrained associations between cues and rewards. Research shows that willpower alone is often insufficient, because habits operate largely outside conscious control. More effective strategies focus on changing the environment: removing temptations, altering routines, and designing cues that support desired behaviors. For example, placing running shoes by the door, preparing healthy meals in advance, or using apps that block distracting websites can make good habits easier and bad habits harder. Identity also plays a role: people who see themselves as healthy, organized, or disciplined are more likely to sustain behaviors consistent with that self-image. The popular idea that habits form in twenty-one days is a myth; studies suggest that forming a new habit typically takes between two and ten months, depending on complexity and consistency. Understanding habits therefore offers a more realistic approach to self-improvement than relying on motivation alone, emphasizing small, repeatable actions, supportive environments, and patience with setbacks rather than dramatic resolutions that quickly fade.", "title": "The Psychology of Habits"}]}, {"no": 42, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了自动驾驶既有安全潜力也有技术伦理挑战的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中安全论述相反；D 与文中责任讨论相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Self-driving cars offer safety potential but face technical, ethical, and legal challenges.", "Self-driving cars are already perfect.", "Self-driving cars will never be used.", "Self-driving cars have no benefits."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中人类错误是主因相反；B 与消除疲劳、分心和酒驾一致；C 与安全潜力相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Human drivers never make mistakes.", "Removing fatigue, distraction, and drunk driving could reduce crashes.", "Self-driving cars cause more accidents in all cases.", "Sensors work perfectly in all weather."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why might self-driving cars be safer?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中传感器局限相反；C 与恶劣天气、复杂路况和伦理困境一致；B 与技术挑战相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Self-driving cars handle all conditions perfectly.", "Ethical dilemmas do not exist.", "Bad weather, complex roads, and unavoidable crash choices remain challenges.", "Liability is already fully settled."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about remaining challenges?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张逐步推进相反；D 与分阶段推广、明确责任和基础设施的建议一致；B 与文中安全测试相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Deploying fully driverless cars everywhere immediately.", "Banning all research.", "Ignoring liability questions.", "Gradual deployment with clear liability rules and infrastructure."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与讨论风险不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely descriptive.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Self-driving cars, once confined to science fiction, are now being tested on public roads in several countries, promising to transform transport by removing human error from driving. Supporters argue that the benefits could be enormous. More than ninety percent of road accidents are caused by human mistakes, including fatigue, distraction, speeding, and drunk driving. Autonomous vehicles, equipped with sensors, cameras, and artificial intelligence, do not tire, lose concentration, or drive under the influence. They could also improve mobility for elderly and disabled people, reduce traffic congestion through coordinated driving, and free passengers to work or rest during journeys. Yet the technology faces formidable challenges. Sensors can struggle in heavy rain, snow, or fog, and unpredictable situations, such as a child chasing a ball into the road or a construction worker gesturing unexpectedly, remain difficult for algorithms to interpret. Ethical dilemmas also arise: in an unavoidable crash, how should a vehicle choose between protecting its passengers and minimizing harm to pedestrians? There is no consensus on how such decisions should be programmed, or who should bear responsibility for them. Legal questions are equally complex. If a self-driving car causes an accident, liability might fall on the owner, the manufacturer, the software developer, or the company operating the fleet, and existing insurance and traffic laws were not designed for such cases. Employment effects add another concern, as millions of professional drivers could face displacement. Some cities have begun to allow limited driverless taxi services, gathering real-world data while restricting operations to favorable conditions and mapped areas. This gradual approach reflects a growing recognition that autonomous driving will not arrive as a sudden revolution but as a slow integration, with human drivers and automated systems sharing roads for decades. The ultimate success of self-driving cars will depend not only on technological progress but on public trust, regulatory clarity, and the ability to demonstrate that the technology is genuinely safer than the human drivers it seeks to replace.", "title": "Self-Driving Cars: Promise and Peril"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中改革必要性相反；B 与高等教育既需改革也有多重目标的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中批判相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Higher education needs no reform.", "Higher education faces pressures to reform teaching, access, and relevance.", "Universities should only focus on research.", "All students learn in the same way."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中讲座局限相反；C 与主动学习、项目和反馈更有效一致；B 与研究证据相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Lectures are always the best method.", "Students learn nothing from discussion.", "Active learning, projects, and feedback often improve understanding.", "Examinations are the only valid assessment."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what improves student learning?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中技能与知识并重相反；D 与批判性思维、沟通和适应能力一致；B 与雇主需求相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Employers only care about grades.", "Universities should teach only theory.", "Practical skills are worthless.", "Employers value critical thinking, communication, and adaptability."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about employer expectations?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与教学创新、公平准入和产学合作的建议一致；B 与作者主张改革相反；C 与文中多目标相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Combining teaching innovation, fair access, and links to work and society.", "Keeping lectures unchanged forever.", "Closing universities to most students.", "Ignoring student wellbeing."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which reform?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既肯定大学价值又强调改革的立场一致；C 与讨论挑战相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about universities.", "Constructively critical, valuing universities while urging reform.", "Indifferent to higher education.", "Hostile toward all academics."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward higher education?"}], "text": "Universities are facing unprecedented pressure to change, from rising costs and shifting student expectations to rapid technological advances and demands from employers for different skills. For centuries, the lecture has been the dominant form of teaching, yet research consistently shows that passive listening is less effective than active learning, in which students discuss, solve problems, and receive feedback. Many institutions are therefore redesigning courses around projects, group work, and digital tools, while also reconsidering assessment, moving beyond memorization toward tasks that measure critical thinking and creativity. Access remains a central concern. Despite expansion, students from disadvantaged backgrounds are still underrepresented at prestigious universities, and financial barriers, inadequate school preparation, and cultural factors continue to limit opportunity. Reforms such as contextual admissions, foundation programs, and financial support aim to widen participation, but progress is slow and contested. At the same time, universities are asked to serve multiple, sometimes conflicting purposes: advancing research, preparing graduates for employment, fostering citizenship, and contributing to local communities. Employers increasingly emphasize transferable skills, including communication, teamwork, and adaptability, alongside subject knowledge, prompting universities to integrate internships, interdisciplinary courses, and real-world projects. Technology adds both opportunity and disruption. Online resources can supplement teaching, while artificial intelligence raises questions about assessment integrity and the future of academic work. Mental health has also become a priority, as students report rising levels of anxiety and stress, linked to financial pressure, academic competition, and social isolation. Effective reform therefore requires balancing competing demands rather than pursuing a single solution. Universities must preserve their role as places of deep learning and independent inquiry while becoming more inclusive, flexible, and responsive to a changing world. Those that succeed will be those that listen very carefully to students, support staff, and engage with society, rather than those that simply defend tradition or chase rankings.", "title": "Reforming Higher Education"}]}, {"no": 43, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中水资源压力相反；C 与水短缺既是自然也是管理与分配问题的主旨一致；B 过于简单化；D 与文中农业用水论述相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Water scarcity is caused only by drought.", "Water is unlimited everywhere.", "Water scarcity involves climate, agriculture, governance, and inequality.", "Desalination solves all water problems."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中农业用水占比相反；D 与农业消耗大量水、灌溉效率低一致；B 与事实不符；C 与文中论述相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Agriculture uses almost no water.", "Industry is the only water user.", "Households consume most water.", "Agriculture consumes most freshwater, often inefficiently."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what is a major source of water demand?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与水价过低导致浪费一致；B 与文中管理失败相反；C 与水资源经济价值相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Underpricing water encourages wasteful use.", "Water pricing has no effect on consumption.", "All water should be free without limits.", "Farmers never waste water."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about water pricing?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张综合管理相反；B 与节水技术、定价、保护和合作的建议一致；C 与文中跨界合作相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Building only more dams.", "Combining efficient irrigation, pricing, conservation, and cooperation.", "Ignoring groundwater depletion.", "Relying on a single technology."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于悲观；C 与既承认危机又强调可行方案的立场一致；B 与讨论短缺相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Water scarcity is unavoidable.", "Water scarcity does not exist.", "Water scarcity is a serious but manageable challenge.", "Technology alone will solve everything."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward water scarcity?"}], "text": "Water covers most of the Earth's surface, yet only a tiny fraction is fresh, accessible, and safe to drink, and that fraction is under growing pressure from population growth, agriculture, industry, and climate change. More than two billion people live in countries experiencing high water stress, and in many regions, rivers are running dry, groundwater levels are falling, and competition for water is intensifying. Agriculture accounts for about seventy percent of freshwater withdrawals, much of it used inefficiently through flood irrigation that loses water to evaporation and runoff. Growing thirsty crops in arid regions, such as cotton or rice in desert climates, further strains supplies. Industry and energy production also demand large volumes, while expanding cities require water for households, sanitation, and services. Climate change is altering rainfall patterns, making wet regions wetter and dry regions drier, and melting glaciers that once provided reliable summer flows to millions of people. The consequences of scarcity are severe: crop failures, food price increases, health risks from unsafe water, and conflicts over shared rivers and aquifers. Women and girls in many developing countries spend hours each day collecting water, time that could be spent on education or work. Solutions exist but require difficult choices. Efficient irrigation, such as drip systems, can dramatically reduce agricultural water use, while fixing leaks in urban networks can save enormous volumes. Pricing water to reflect its scarcity can encourage conservation, though it must be designed to protect poor households. Protecting wetlands and forests helps maintain natural water cycles, and treating wastewater for reuse can supplement supplies. Desalination offers an option for coastal cities, but it is energy-intensive and expensive. International cooperation is also essential, as many major rivers cross national borders. Ultimately, water scarcity is not only a hydrological problem but a governance challenge, requiring societies to decide how to allocate a finite, essential resource fairly and sustainably in a warming world.", "title": "Water Scarcity: A Growing Crisis"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中网红经济规模相反；D 与网红文化既创造机会也有真实性与心理风险的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中商业影响相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Influencer culture has no economic impact.", "All influencers are dishonest.", "Influencer culture is purely positive.", "Influencer culture creates opportunities but raises authenticity and wellbeing concerns."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与网红推荐看似个人建议而非广告一致；B 与营销效果相反；C 与文中信任机制相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Followers trust influencers because recommendations feel personal.", "Influencer marketing never works.", "Consumers always recognize sponsored content.", "Influencers have no effect on buying."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why is influencer marketing effective?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中比较压力相反；B 与精心策划的生活方式引发比较和焦虑一致；C 与心理研究相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Influencer content always improves self-esteem.", "Curated lifestyles can trigger comparison and anxiety.", "Followers never compare themselves to influencers.", "Social media has no psychological effects."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about the psychological impact?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张透明度相反；C 与明确标注赞助、保护未成年人和媒体素养的建议一致；B 与文中监管讨论相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Banning all influencers.", "Allowing undisclosed advertising.", "Requiring disclosure, protecting minors, and promoting media literacy.", "Ignoring influencer marketing entirely."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既承认创造力又强调风险的立场一致；B 与讨论问题相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Influencer culture is worthless.", "Influencer culture has no problems.", "Influencers should never be trusted.", "Influencer culture is complex, with both value and risks."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward influencer culture?"}], "text": "In less than a decade, social media influencers have grown from hobbyists sharing personal interests into a multi-billion-dollar industry that shapes fashion, travel, food, and political opinions. Brands increasingly prefer influencers to traditional celebrities because their recommendations feel personal and authentic, as if coming from a trusted friend rather than a corporation. A beauty vlogger demonstrating a product in her bedroom can be more persuasive than a polished television advertisement, precisely because the setting appears informal and relatable. For creators, influencer work offers independence, creativity, and the possibility of earning a living from personal passions, without the gatekeepers of traditional media. Yet the culture raises significant concerns. Authenticity itself has become a performance, as influencers carefully curate images of effortless lifestyles while being paid to promote products, sometimes without clear disclosure. Followers may not realize that a seemingly spontaneous recommendation is a sponsored advertisement, blurring the line between genuine opinion and marketing. The pressure to maintain a perfect image can also harm influencers themselves, leading to burnout, anxiety, and a sense of losing privacy as their personal lives become content. For audiences, constant exposure to idealized lifestyles can fuel comparison, envy, and dissatisfaction, particularly among young people who may not recognize the editing, filtering, and staging behind the images. Regulation is beginning to catch up, with many countries requiring influencers to label sponsored content and restricting advertising to children. Media literacy education can help audiences distinguish between authentic sharing and commercial persuasion. None of this suggests that influencer culture should be dismissed. At its best, it democratizes creativity, gives voice to underrepresented communities, and allows niche interests to find audiences. The challenge is to preserve those benefits while ensuring transparency, protecting vulnerable audiences, and recognizing that behind every curated feed is a real person navigating the pressures of an attention economy.", "title": "Influencer Culture and Authenticity"}]}, {"no": 44, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了量子计算既有巨大潜力也有技术与安全挑战的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中应用论述相反；D 与文中不确定性相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Quantum computing offers great potential but faces technical and security challenges.", "Quantum computers will replace all computers tomorrow.", "Quantum computing has no practical uses.", "Quantum technology is already fully mature."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中量子比特原理相反；B 与叠加和纠缠使并行处理一致；C 与量子计算原理相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Quantum computers work exactly like ordinary computers.", "Qubits using superposition and entanglement enable parallel processing.", "Quantum computers are slower than laptops.", "Quantum computers use only binary bits."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how do quantum computers differ?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中密码风险相反；C 与量子计算可能破解现有加密一致；B 与安全担忧相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Quantum computing poses no security risk.", "All current encryption is quantum-proof.", "Quantum computers could break much current encryption.", "Passwords will become unnecessary."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about cybersecurity?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张持续研究相反；D 与投资研究、培养人才和开发抗量子加密的建议一致；B 与文中潜力相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Abandoning quantum research.", "Ignoring post-quantum cryptography.", "Waiting until quantum computers are perfect.", "Investing in research, talent, and quantum-resistant encryption."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与讨论风险不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely descriptive.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Quantum computing is often described as the next great technological revolution, promising to solve problems that would take conventional computers thousands of years. Unlike classical computers, which store information as bits representing either zero or one, quantum computers use qubits, which can exist in a superposition of states, allowing many calculations to be performed simultaneously. Entanglement, another quantum phenomenon, links qubits in ways that have no classical equivalent, further expanding computational power. If realized at scale, quantum computers could transform drug discovery by simulating molecules, optimize logistics and financial portfolios, and accelerate advances in materials science and artificial intelligence. Yet building a practical quantum computer remains extraordinarily difficult. Qubits are fragile, easily disturbed by heat, vibration, and electromagnetic interference, and maintaining their quantum states requires temperatures near absolute zero and sophisticated error correction. Current machines have only dozens or hundreds of qubits, far fewer than the millions likely needed for many promised applications, and error rates remain high. The timeline for useful quantum computing is therefore uncertain, with some experts predicting breakthroughs within a decade and others warning that fundamental obstacles may take much longer to overcome. Security adds urgency to the debate. Many encryption systems that protect banking, government communications, and personal data rely on mathematical problems that classical computers cannot solve efficiently, but a sufficiently powerful quantum computer could break them, prompting a race to develop quantum-resistant cryptography. Governments and companies are investing billions in quantum research, partly for economic advantage and partly for national security. The wise approach is to pursue the technology's promise while preparing for its risks, investing in research and talent, developing new security standards, and ensuring that the benefits of quantum computing are not concentrated in a few powerful actors. International collaboration, shared standards, and open research can help ensure that progress benefits many societies rather than a narrow group of firms or states.", "title": "Quantum Computing: The Next Revolution?"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中睡眠重要性相反；B 与睡眠既影响健康也有社会文化因素的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中研究相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Sleep is a waste of time.", "Sleep is essential for health, learning, and emotion, but modern life often undermines it.", "Sleep has no effect on memory.", "Everyone needs exactly eight hours."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中记忆巩固相反；C 与睡眠中记忆整理和情绪处理一致；B 与研究证据相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Sleep only rests muscles.", "Sleep has no brain function.", "During sleep, the brain consolidates memories and processes emotions.", "Dreams have no purpose."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what does the brain do during sleep?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中屏幕蓝光相反；D 与屏幕光、咖啡因和不规律作息干扰睡眠一致；B 与健康风险相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Screens improve sleep quality.", "Caffeine has no effect on sleep.", "Irregular schedules are harmless.", "Screens, caffeine, and irregular schedules disrupt sleep."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about modern habits?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与规律作息、减少屏幕和改善环境相结合的建议一致；B 与作者主张综合措施相反；C 与文中睡眠卫生相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Combining regular schedules, less screen time, and better sleep environments.", "Relying only on sleeping pills.", "Ignoring sleep problems.", "Working through the night regularly."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author recommends which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既承认睡眠不足普遍又强调可改善的立场一致；C 与讨论健康风险相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about sleep.", "Concerned but constructive, seeing sleep as improvable.", "Indifferent to sleep health.", "Hostile toward sleep research."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward sleep?"}], "text": "Sleep is often treated as a luxury that can be sacrificed for work, study, or entertainment, yet research increasingly shows that it is a biological necessity as fundamental as food and water. During sleep, the brain consolidates memories, processes emotions, and clears metabolic waste, while the body repairs tissues, regulates hormones, and strengthens the immune system. Chronic sleep deprivation is linked to impaired concentration, poor decision-making, weakened immunity, obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and depression. Students who sleep too little perform worse on tests, not because they studied less, but because their brains cannot effectively store what they learned. Despite this evidence, modern life systematically undermines sleep. Artificial light, especially the blue light from screens, suppresses melatonin, the hormone that signals bedtime, while caffeine, alcohol, and irregular schedules further disrupt natural rhythms. Shift work, long commutes, and a culture that celebrates busyness encourage people to view sleep as wasted time. The consequences are not merely individual. Sleep-deprived drivers cause accidents, medical errors increase when healthcare workers are exhausted, and productivity losses cost economies billions each year. Children and adolescents are particularly vulnerable, as their developing brains require more sleep, yet early school start times often conflict with their natural sleep patterns. Improving sleep requires both individual and societal changes. Sleep hygiene, maintaining regular bedtimes, reducing screen use before bed, creating dark and quiet bedrooms, and avoiding stimulants late in the day, can help many people. Schools and employers can also play a role, by scheduling later start times, limiting excessive overtime, and recognizing that rest is not laziness but a foundation for performance. Ultimately, valuing sleep means recognizing that human beings are not machines, and that sustainable productivity depends on respecting the rhythms that biology imposes. Employers who respect rest often find that well-rested teams make fewer errors, communicate more clearly, and sustain their performance over months rather than burning out quickly.", "title": "The Science of Sleep"}]}, {"no": 45, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中移民经济影响复杂相反；C 与移民既带来收益也有分配挑战的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中劳动力市场论述相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Immigration always harms the economy.", "Immigration has no economic effects.", "Immigration brings economic benefits but also distributional challenges.", "Immigrants never pay taxes."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中移民填补短缺相反；D 与移民补充劳动力、创业和缴纳税收一致；B 与经济贡献相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Immigrants never work.", "Immigration reduces all wages permanently.", "Immigrants only consume public services.", "Immigrants fill labor shortages, start businesses, and pay taxes."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how do immigrants contribute economically?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与低技能本土工人可能面临竞争一致；B 与文中总体收益相悖；C 与分配效应相反；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Low-skilled native workers may face short-term competition.", "All native workers benefit equally immediately.", "Immigration has no distributional effects.", "Wages always rise for everyone."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about distributional effects?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张政策支持相反；B 与语言培训、资格认证和融合支持的建议一致；C 与文中经济收益相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Closing all borders.", "Investing in language training, credential recognition, and integration.", "Ignoring immigrant integration.", "Banning immigrants from working."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于乐观；C 与既承认收益又强调需要政策应对的立场一致；B 与讨论经济贡献相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Immigration is purely harmful.", "Immigration is purely beneficial.", "Immigration is economically beneficial overall but requires good policy.", "Immigration should be ignored."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward immigration?"}], "text": "Immigration is one of the most debated topics in economics and politics, often framed in simplistic terms as either an unqualified benefit or an unmitigated burden. The reality, as research consistently shows, is more nuanced. Immigrants contribute to host economies in multiple ways: they fill labor shortages in agriculture, healthcare, construction, and technology, start businesses at higher rates than native-born citizens in many countries, and pay taxes that support public services. In aging societies with shrinking workforces, immigration can help sustain pension systems and maintain economic growth. Immigrants also bring diverse skills, ideas, and entrepreneurial energy, fostering innovation and cultural exchange. Yet these aggregate benefits do not mean that everyone gains equally. In the short term, an influx of low-skilled workers can increase competition for certain jobs, potentially depressing wages for native workers in similar occupations, particularly those without higher education. Housing markets in popular destinations may also face pressure, and public services such as schools and healthcare can become strained if investment does not keep pace with population growth. The fiscal impact depends on the characteristics of immigrants, including age, education, and employment status, as well as on integration policies. Young, skilled immigrants who work and pay taxes tend to be net contributors, while refugees who face barriers to employment may initially require support. Long-term outcomes improve substantially when immigrants gain language skills, have their qualifications recognized, and are allowed to work in jobs matching their abilities. Policies that restrict work rights or delay integration often worsen fiscal outcomes and social tensions. The economic evidence therefore suggests that immigration is generally beneficial overall, but that its benefits depend on context and policy. Societies that invest in integration, protect labor standards for all workers, and plan for population growth are more likely to realize the gains while minimizing the costs, turning a contentious issue into a shared opportunity.", "title": "The Economics of Immigration"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中回收率低相反；D 与塑料回收既重要也有技术与经济障碍的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中污染问题相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Plastic recycling works perfectly everywhere.", "All plastic can be recycled easily.", "Recycling is unnecessary.", "Plastic recycling is important but faces technical and economic barriers."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与混合材料、污染和分拣困难一致；B 与回收挑战相反；C 与文中技术限制相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Mixed materials, contamination, and sorting difficulties limit recycling.", "All plastics are identical.", "Recycling requires no sorting.", "Consumers always sort correctly."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why is plastic recycling difficult?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中回收塑料需求低相反；B 与原生塑料便宜导致回收料缺乏市场一致；C 与经济激励相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Recycled plastic always sells at high prices.", "Cheap virgin plastic makes recycled material uncompetitive.", "Manufacturers prefer recycled plastic in all cases.", "Oil prices have no effect on recycling."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about the economics of recycling?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张减量优先相反；C 与减量、再利用、设计改进和回收相结合的建议一致；B 与文中综合措施相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Relying only on recycling bins.", "Banning all plastic immediately.", "Combining reduction, reuse, better design, and recycling.", "Ignoring plastic waste entirely."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既承认回收局限又强调其作用的立场一致；B 与讨论障碍相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Recycling is worthless.", "Recycling alone solves plastic pollution.", "Recycling has no challenges.", "Recycling is useful but insufficient alone."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward recycling?"}], "text": "Recycling is often presented as the solution to plastic pollution, yet only about nine percent of all plastic ever produced has been recycled, a statistic that reveals deep problems in how the system works. The difficulty begins with the diversity of plastics. Different polymers, additives, and colors require different processes, and products often combine multiple materials that are difficult to separate. Food contamination, labels, and mixed waste further complicate sorting, while many items, such as thin films, multi-layer packaging, and small caps, are technically recyclable but economically impractical to process. Even when plastic is collected, much of it is downcycled into lower-quality products rather than being remade into equivalent items, and after a few cycles the material degrades and must be discarded. Economics also undermine recycling. Virgin plastic, made from cheap oil and gas, is often less expensive than recycled material, giving manufacturers little incentive to use recycled content unless required by law or consumer demand. When oil prices fall, recycling programs struggle financially, and some collected plastic is exported, landfilled, or incinerated rather than actually recycled. These limitations do not mean recycling is worthless. It saves energy compared with producing new plastic, reduces landfill waste, and provides jobs in collection and processing. But treating recycling as the primary solution can be misleading, as it places responsibility on consumers to sort waste while allowing producers to continue making disposable products. A more effective approach prioritizes reduction and reuse: designing products with fewer materials, standardizing packaging, encouraging refill systems, and holding manufacturers responsible for the lifecycle of their products. Policies such as recycled-content mandates, deposit-return schemes, and bans on problematic single-use items can create markets for recycled materials and reduce overall plastic use. Recycling should therefore be understood as one tool among many, valuable when properly supported, but insufficient on its own to address a problem rooted in how much plastic society produces and consumes.", "title": "Why Plastic Recycling Falls Short"}]}, {"no": 46, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了VR治疗既有前景也有局限的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中临床证据相反；D 与文中可及性论述相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Virtual reality therapy shows promise for some conditions but has limits and risks.", "VR therapy cures all mental disorders.", "VR therapy has no scientific evidence.", "VR therapy is too expensive for any patient."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中暴露疗法相反；B 与在安全可控环境中逐步暴露一致；C 与治疗机制相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["VR therapy avoids all feared situations.", "VR allows gradual exposure to feared situations in a safe, controlled setting.", "VR therapy never involves therapists.", "VR therapy replaces all medication."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how does VR help with phobias?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中晕动症相反；C 与晕动症、成本和数据隐私一致；B 与技术局限相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["VR has no side effects.", "All patients tolerate VR perfectly.", "Motion sickness, cost, and data privacy are concerns.", "VR headsets are free for everyone."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about limitations of VR therapy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张辅助而非替代相反；D 与VR作为辅助工具、需临床监督的建议一致；B 与文中证据要求相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Replacing all therapists with VR.", "Using VR without any clinical guidance.", "Ignoring patient safety.", "Using VR as a supervised tool alongside human therapy."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与讨论风险不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely descriptive.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "Virtual reality, once associated mainly with gaming, is increasingly being used as a therapeutic tool for conditions ranging from phobias and post-traumatic stress disorder to chronic pain and rehabilitation after stroke. In exposure therapy, for example, a patient afraid of heights can gradually confront virtual high places in a safe, controlled environment, learning that feared outcomes do not occur and building confidence before facing real situations. For veterans with PTSD, VR can recreate triggering environments in a clinical setting, allowing therapists to guide patients through traumatic memories at a manageable pace. The immersive quality of VR appears to enhance engagement compared with imagining scenarios, and therapists can precisely control stimuli, repeat exercises, and monitor physiological responses. Early studies suggest promising results, particularly for specific phobias, social anxiety, and pain distraction during medical procedures. Yet VR therapy is not a panacea. Some patients experience motion sickness, eye strain, or discomfort from headsets, while others may find virtual environments unconvincing or distressing in unexpected ways. Cost and access remain barriers, as high-quality systems and trained therapists are not widely available, particularly in low-resource settings. Data privacy also raises concerns, as VR systems collect detailed information about eye movements, physical reactions, and emotional responses. There is also a risk that technology could be marketed as a quick fix, diverting attention from the therapeutic relationship that remains central to mental health care. The most responsible approach treats VR as a tool that extends, rather than replaces, human expertise, used under clinical supervision, tailored to individual needs, and evaluated through rigorous research. As the technology improves steadily and costs fall further and becomes more affordable, VR may become a valuable addition to the therapist's toolkit, but its success will depend on careful implementation, ethical safeguards, and a recognition that healing involves more than immersive simulation.", "title": "Virtual Reality as Therapy"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中CSR不只是慈善相反；B 与CSR既可创造价值也有漂绿风险的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中利益相关者论述相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["CSR is only about charity donations.", "Corporate social responsibility can create value but also risks becoming empty public relations.", "CSR always increases profits.", "Companies have no responsibilities beyond profit."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中员工和消费者期望相反；C 与人才、声誉和消费者信任一致；B 与CSR动机相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Companies ignore social issues entirely.", "CSR is legally required in all cases.", "Talent attraction, reputation, and consumer trust motivate CSR.", "CSR has no effect on business."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why do companies adopt CSR?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中漂绿定义相反；D 与宣传社会责任而实际做法不变一致；B 与CSR批评相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["CSR always improves the environment.", "Companies never exaggerate their efforts.", "Greenwashing does not exist.", "A company may publicize responsibility while changing little in practice."], "prompt": "What does the author imply by greenwashing in CSR?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与可衡量目标、透明报告和问责的建议一致；B 与作者主张实质行动相反；C 与文中利益相关者相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Measurable goals, transparent reporting, and accountability make CSR meaningful.", "CSR should be only advertising.", "Companies should ignore stakeholders.", "CSR needs no evaluation."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既肯定潜力又强调真实行动的立场一致；C 与讨论漂绿相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about CSR.", "Cautiously optimistic, valuing genuine CSR while warning against greenwashing.", "Indifferent to corporate behavior.", "Hostile toward all companies."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward CSR?"}], "text": "Corporate social responsibility, the idea that businesses should contribute to society beyond generating profits, has moved from the margins to the mainstream of management thinking. Companies now publish sustainability reports, set carbon reduction targets, donate to charities, and promote diversity initiatives, often in response to pressure from consumers, employees, investors, and regulators. Supporters argue that CSR can align business interests with social good: companies that treat workers well attract talent, those that reduce environmental impact lower costs and risks, and those that earn public trust build stronger brands. In this view, responsibility is not a cost but an investment in long-term value. Critics, however, question whether CSR is genuinely transformative or merely a form of public relations. When a fossil fuel company advertises a small renewable energy project while continuing to expand oil production, or when a clothing brand promotes a charitable donation while tolerating poor labor conditions in its supply chain, CSR can function as greenwashing, distracting from harmful practices rather than addressing them. The voluntary nature of most CSR also limits its impact: companies can choose which issues to address and how much to disclose, making it difficult for outsiders to assess real progress. Some economists argue that the primary social responsibility of business is to maximize profits within the law, and that asking companies to pursue social goals confuses their purpose and may reduce economic efficiency. Others respond that businesses operate within societies and depend on public infrastructure, educated workers, and stable environments, and therefore have obligations that extend beyond shareholders. The debate is increasingly shifting from whether companies should be responsible to how responsibility should be defined, measured, and enforced. Transparent reporting, independent audits, measurable targets, and stakeholder engagement can distinguish genuine commitment from empty slogans. Ultimately, CSR is most meaningful when it is integrated into core business strategy, rather than treated as a separate charitable activity, and when it is subject to the same scrutiny and accountability as financial performance.", "title": "Corporate Social Responsibility"}]}, {"no": 47, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中物种灭绝加速相反；C 与生物多样性丧失既威胁生态也有经济与伦理原因的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中栖息地破坏相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Biodiversity loss is not happening.", "Only one species is at risk.", "Biodiversity loss threatens ecosystems, economies, and human wellbeing.", "Extinction is natural and harmless."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中栖息地破坏相反；D 与栖息地丧失、过度开发、污染和气候变化一致；B 与多重原因相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Biodiversity loss has a single cause.", "Only climate change matters.", "Human activity has no effect on species.", "Habitat loss, overexploitation, pollution, and climate change drive extinction."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what drives biodiversity loss?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与传粉、净水和气候调节等生态系统服务一致；B 与文中经济价值相反；C 与生态功能相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Ecosystems provide pollination, clean water, and climate regulation.", "Biodiversity has no economic value.", "Species loss never affects humans.", "Ecosystem services are free forever."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about ecosystem services?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张保护与可持续利用相反；B 与保护区、可持续农业和国际合作的建议一致；C 与文中恢复可能相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Abandoning all conservation efforts.", "Combining protected areas, sustainable farming, and international cooperation.", "Ignoring habitat loss.", "Relying only on zoos."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于悲观；C 与既承认危机严重又强调可行动的立场一致；B 与讨论灭绝相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Biodiversity loss is unimportant.", "Biodiversity loss cannot be slowed.", "Biodiversity loss is serious but can be slowed with action.", "Nature will recover without help."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward biodiversity loss?"}], "text": "The Earth is experiencing a biodiversity crisis, with species disappearing at rates far above natural background levels, driven largely by human activity. Habitat destruction is the primary cause: forests are cleared for agriculture, wetlands are drained for development, and coral reefs are damaged by pollution and warming seas. Overexploitation, including overfishing, hunting, and illegal wildlife trade, further depletes populations, while invasive species, pollution, and climate change compound the pressure. The loss is not merely aesthetic or sentimental. Biodiversity underpins ecosystem services that humans depend on: pollinators enable food production, forests store carbon and regulate rainfall, wetlands filter water, and diverse genetic resources provide medicines and crops resilient to disease. When species disappear, ecosystems become less stable and less able to recover from disturbances, increasing the risk of cascading failures. The economic costs are substantial, affecting agriculture, fisheries, tourism, and pharmaceuticals, while the cultural and ethical dimensions are equally profound, as the extinction of a species represents an irreversible loss of unique evolutionary history. Some argue that conservation competes with development, particularly in poor countries where land and resources are needed for livelihoods. Yet evidence shows that conservation and development can be compatible when local communities benefit from protecting nature, through ecotourism, sustainable harvesting, and payments for ecosystem services. International agreements, such as the Convention on Biological Diversity, set targets for protecting habitats and restoring degraded ecosystems, but implementation has often fallen short. Protected areas, while essential, are insufficient on their own if surrounding landscapes remain hostile to wildlife. A more integrated approach combines protected areas with sustainable agriculture, urban planning that preserves green spaces, and policies that address the underlying drivers of habitat loss. The challenge is urgent, because extinction is permanent, but it is not hopeless: species can recover when threats are removed, as shown by the return of wolves, whales, and other animals after targeted protection.", "title": "The Biodiversity Crisis"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中图书馆转型相反；D 与图书馆既面临数字化挑战也有社区新角色的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中数字资源论述相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Libraries are obsolete.", "Libraries should only lend books.", "Libraries have no role in digital literacy.", "Libraries are evolving into community hubs for learning and access."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与电子书和在线资源减少实体借阅一致；B 与图书馆转型相反；C 与文中服务扩展相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["E-books and online resources have reduced physical borrowing.", "People no longer read at all.", "Libraries have no internet.", "All books have disappeared."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how has digitization affected libraries?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中数字鸿沟作用相反；B 与为无网络者提供接入和培训一致；C 与社区服务相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Libraries only serve wealthy readers.", "Libraries provide internet access and digital training to those without it.", "Libraries exclude disadvantaged groups.", "Libraries ignore technology."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about libraries and equity?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张投资图书馆相反；C 与资助、培训和社区合作的建议一致；B 与文中图书馆价值相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Closing all public libraries.", "Removing all digital services.", "Funding libraries, training staff, and partnering with communities.", "Turning libraries into shops."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既承认挑战又肯定图书馆适应力的立场一致；B 与讨论转型相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Libraries are doomed.", "Libraries should never change.", "Libraries are irrelevant.", "Libraries remain valuable by adapting to new needs."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward libraries?"}], "text": "When the internet made vast amounts of information freely available, many predicted the end of public libraries, yet libraries have proved remarkably resilient by reinventing themselves as community hubs rather than mere repositories of books. Physical borrowing has declined in many places, but libraries now offer far more than lending: free internet access, computer training, study spaces, children's programs, job search assistance, and meeting rooms for local groups. For people without reliable internet at home, libraries provide an essential point of access, helping to narrow the digital divide. They also serve as trusted, neutral spaces in increasingly divided societies, where people of different backgrounds can meet, learn, and participate in civic life. Librarians have evolved from custodians of collections into guides, educators, and community organizers, helping users navigate information overload, evaluate sources, and develop digital skills. The challenges facing libraries are nonetheless real. Funding is often precarious, with local governments cutting budgets during economic downturns, forcing reductions in hours, staff, and services. The shift to digital resources raises questions about licensing, as e-books are often more expensive for libraries than print books and come with restrictive terms. Some critics argue that public money should not support institutions whose original function has been superseded by technology. Supporters respond that libraries have always adapted to new media, from printed books to films to computers, and that their core mission, providing free access to knowledge and opportunity, is more important than ever in an age of misinformation and inequality. The future of libraries likely lies in partnership: with schools, health services, and community organizations, libraries can reach people who might never walk through their doors. Their survival will depend on demonstrating value, securing stable funding, and continuing to evolve in response to community needs, proving that a centuries-old institution can remain relevant in a digital age.", "title": "The Future of Libraries"}]}, {"no": 48, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了网络安全既是技术也是人为与组织问题的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中人为因素相反；D 与文中勒索软件论述相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Cybersecurity is a technical, human, and organizational challenge.", "Technology alone can guarantee security.", "Human error plays no role in breaches.", "Ransomware is no longer a threat."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中钓鱼攻击相反；B 与员工点击钓鱼链接和弱密码一致；C 与人为因素相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Hackers never target employees.", "Employees clicking phishing links and using weak passwords cause many breaches.", "Security training is useless.", "All breaches are caused by insiders."], "prompt": "According to the passage, why do many breaches succeed?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中勒索软件危害相反；C 与加密数据并索要赎金一致；B 与攻击模式相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Ransomware only affects individuals.", "Ransomware never demands payment.", "Ransomware encrypts data and demands payment for its release.", "Ransomware is easy to prevent completely."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about ransomware?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张多层防御相反；D 与技术、培训、备份和合作相结合的建议一致；B 与文中人为因素相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Relying only on firewalls.", "Ignoring employee training.", "Paying every ransom immediately.", "Combining technology, training, backups, and cooperation."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which strategy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合严肃关切的语气；B 与网络威胁论述不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Serious and concerned.", "Humorous and light-hearted.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "As societies become increasingly dependent on digital systems for banking, healthcare, energy, and communication, cybersecurity has emerged as a critical challenge that is as much human and organizational as it is technical. High-profile breaches regularly expose millions of personal records, disrupt hospitals and businesses, and cost economies billions of dollars. Ransomware attacks, in which criminals encrypt an organization's data and demand payment for its release, have targeted schools, local governments, and critical infrastructure, revealing how vulnerable essential services can be. While sophisticated hacking techniques attract attention, many breaches succeed through simpler means: employees click on phishing emails, reuse weak passwords, or fail to update software, providing attackers with an easy entry point. This human dimension means that technology alone cannot guarantee security. Firewalls and encryption are essential, but they must be complemented by training, clear procedures, and a culture in which security is treated as everyone's responsibility rather than solely the IT department's concern. Small businesses are particularly vulnerable, as they often lack dedicated security staff and resources, yet they hold valuable customer data and may serve as entry points to larger partners. Governments face their own dilemmas, balancing the need to protect critical infrastructure with concerns about privacy, surveillance, and the regulation of technology companies. International cooperation is complicated by the fact that attackers can operate across borders, often from jurisdictions where prosecution is difficult. There is no perfect security, as new vulnerabilities are constantly discovered and attackers continuously adapt. The goal, therefore, is resilience: the ability to prevent most attacks, detect those that succeed quickly, and recover with minimal damage. This requires layered defenses, regular backups, incident response plans, and information sharing between organizations. Ultimately, cybersecurity is not a one-time investment but an ongoing process of adaptation, reflecting the reality that in a connected world, security depends on the weakest link in a complex chain of people, technology, and institutions.", "title": "Cybersecurity in a Connected World"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中极简主义不只是丢东西相反；B 与极简主义既带来清晰也有社会与经济局限的主旨一致；C 过于绝对；D 与文中消费反思相悖。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Minimalism means owning nothing at all.", "Minimalism can bring clarity and freedom but has social and economic limits.", "Minimalism is only for wealthy people.", "Minimalism requires buying expensive products."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中决策疲劳相反；C 与减少物品可降低决策负担和压力一致；B 与心理研究相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Owning more always reduces stress.", "Minimalism has no psychological effects.", "Fewer possessions can reduce decision fatigue and stress.", "Minimalists never feel anxious."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what psychological benefit is associated with minimalism?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中贫困与自愿简朴的区别相反；D 与被迫匮乏不同于主动选择一致；B 与社会背景相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Minimalism is identical to poverty.", "Poor people choose minimalism freely.", "Minimalism ignores social inequality.", "Forced scarcity differs from voluntary simplicity."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about minimalism and poverty?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与有意识消费、重视体验和关系的建议一致；B 与作者主张反思消费相反；C 与文中极简主义价值相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Intentional consumption, valuing experiences and relationships over accumulation.", "Buying more to feel happy.", "Discarding everything immediately.", "Judging people by their possessions."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which view?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 过于悲观；B 与既肯定极简价值又承认局限的立场一致；C 与讨论社会背景相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about minimalism.", "Thoughtfully balanced, seeing both value and limits.", "Indifferent to lifestyle choices.", "Hostile toward minimalists."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward minimalism?"}], "text": "In a world saturated with advertising and endless consumer choice, minimalism, the deliberate pursuit of living with less, has attracted a growing following. Minimalists argue that possessions, far from bringing happiness, often create clutter, debt, and stress, distracting people from relationships, experiences, and personal growth. By owning fewer but more meaningful things, they claim, individuals can gain time, freedom, and clarity, focusing on what truly matters rather than maintaining and managing an accumulation of objects. Research offers some support for this view: cluttered environments are associated with higher stress hormones, while the constant decision-making required by excess choice can lead to decision fatigue. The practice of decluttering, popularized by books and documentaries, has helped many people reconsider their consumption habits and discover satisfaction in simplicity. Yet minimalism also faces criticism. For affluent people, choosing to own less can be a lifestyle aesthetic, involving expensive, beautifully designed objects and spacious homes, while for poor people, having few possessions is not a choice but a condition imposed by necessity. The movement can therefore appear privileged, overlooking the fact that many people would welcome more, not less, material security. Minimalism also risks becoming another form of consumerism, as people buy specialized organizers, courses, and books about owning less. Moreover, the emphasis on individual lifestyle change can distract from structural issues, such as overproduction, planned obsolescence, and inequality, that drive excessive consumption at a societal level. Despite these limitations, minimalism raises valuable questions about the relationship between possessions and wellbeing. Its most useful insight may not be a specific number of items to own, but the practice of intentionality: asking whether each purchase adds genuine value, whether time spent shopping could be better spent elsewhere, and whether a fulfilling life depends on accumulation or on experiences, relationships, and purpose. In that sense, minimalism is less a rigid doctrine than a prompt to reflect on what enough means in a culture that constantly encourages more.", "title": "Minimalism: Living with Less"}]}, {"no": 49, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中老龄劳动力趋势相反；C 与老龄劳动力既带来经验也有健康与技能挑战的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；D 与文中灵活工作论述相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Older workers cannot learn new skills.", "Older workers should all retire at sixty.", "An aging workforce brings experience but also health and skill challenges.", "Age has no effect on work."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中经验价值相反；D 与经验、判断和指导作用一致；B 与老龄工人贡献相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Older workers have no useful skills.", "Older workers are always slower.", "Experience is worthless in modern work.", "Older workers bring experience, judgment, and mentoring ability."], "prompt": "According to the passage, what do older workers contribute?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与年龄歧视和刻板印象一致；B 与文中招聘偏见相反；C 与年龄歧视证据相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Age discrimination and stereotypes limit older workers' opportunities.", "Employers never discriminate by age.", "Older applicants are always preferred.", "Training is equally available to all ages."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about age discrimination?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与作者主张延长工作生涯相反；B 与灵活工作、培训和健康支持的建议一致；C 与文中包容政策相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "B", "options": ["Forcing all older workers to retire early.", "Offering flexible work, training, and health support.", "Ignoring older workers' needs.", "Banning older people from employment."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which policy?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于悲观；C 与既承认挑战又肯定老年人贡献的立场一致；B 与讨论歧视相反；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Older workers are a burden.", "Older workers have no future.", "An aging workforce is a manageable challenge with proper policy.", "Age should determine all employment."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward an aging workforce?"}], "text": "As populations age and retirement ages rise, workforces in many countries are becoming older, a trend that challenges assumptions about productivity, learning, and the stages of a career. Older workers bring valuable assets: decades of experience, institutional knowledge, judgment honed by facing varied situations, and the ability to mentor younger colleagues. In many professions, from medicine to craftsmanship to management, expertise deepens with time, and the loss of experienced workers through early retirement can deprive organizations of knowledge that is difficult to replace. Yet aging workforces also face real challenges. Physical demands may become harder, health conditions more common, and technological change can require continuous learning that some employers are reluctant to support. Age discrimination remains widespread, with older applicants facing stereotypes about being slower, less adaptable, or more expensive, despite evidence that performance varies more within age groups than between them. Training opportunities are often directed toward younger employees, reinforcing a cycle in which older workers are seen as less capable because they have received less investment. The structure of work itself may need to change. Flexible hours, part-time roles, phased retirement, and ergonomic adjustments can allow older workers to continue contributing while managing health and caring responsibilities. Lifelong learning programs, designed for adults with experience rather than school leavers, can help workers update skills and transition to new roles. Employers who value age diversity often find that mixed-age teams combine the fresh perspectives of younger workers with the seasoned judgment of older colleagues, improving decision-making and innovation. Societies must also reconsider the idea of a single career followed by abrupt retirement, moving toward more fluid life courses in which work, learning, and leisure are interspersed across decades. An aging workforce is not a problem to be solved by pushing people out, but an opportunity to rethink how work is organized, valued, and shared across generations.", "title": "The Aging Workforce"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中AI法律应用相反；D 与AI既提高效率也有偏见与责任问题的主旨一致；B 过于绝对；C 与文中预测性警务争议相悖。", "answer": "D", "options": ["AI has no role in law.", "AI will replace all lawyers immediately.", "AI in law has no risks.", "AI can improve legal efficiency but raises bias and accountability concerns."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与文档审查和法律研究自动化一致；B 与AI应用相反；C 与效率提升相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "A", "options": ["AI can automate document review and legal research.", "AI cannot process legal texts.", "Lawyers never use technology.", "AI always understands legal nuance."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how is AI used in law?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中历史数据偏见相反；B 与偏见风险相悖；C 与训练数据反映历史不平等一致；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["AI systems are always neutral.", "Bias never occurs in legal AI.", "Training data reflecting historical inequalities can produce biased outcomes.", "Historical data is irrelevant."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about bias in legal AI?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与作者主张人类监督相反；C 与人类判断、透明度和问责的建议一致；B 与文中责任讨论相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Letting AI decide all cases alone.", "Hiding how AI systems work.", "Keeping human judgment, transparency, and accountability central.", "Ignoring AI errors."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which approach?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 过于悲观；D 与既承认效率收益又强调公平保障的立场一致；B 与讨论偏见相反；C 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["AI in law is purely harmful.", "AI in law has no problems.", "Lawyers should never use AI.", "AI in law needs careful oversight to protect fairness."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward AI in law?"}], "text": "Artificial intelligence is entering courtrooms and law firms, promising to make legal work faster, cheaper, and more accessible, while also raising profound questions about fairness and accountability. AI systems can review thousands of documents in hours, identify relevant precedents, draft contracts, and predict the likely outcome of cases based on historical data. For large firms, this efficiency reduces costs; for individuals who cannot afford a lawyer, AI-powered legal advice could expand access to justice, helping people understand their rights and navigate complex procedures. Yet the application of AI to law is fraught with risks. Predictive policing tools, which use historical crime data to forecast where offenses are likely to occur, have been criticized for reinforcing existing biases, as data reflecting past discriminatory practices can lead to over-policing of minority communities. Similarly, risk assessment algorithms used in bail and sentencing decisions may reproduce inequalities embedded in training data, with serious consequences for liberty. The opacity of many AI systems compounds the problem: when a model recommends a particular outcome, it may be difficult to explain why, undermining the transparency and reasoning that are central to legal legitimacy. Questions of responsibility are also unresolved. If an AI system provides incorrect legal advice or a flawed prediction, it is unclear whether the software developer, the law firm, or the individual lawyer should be held accountable. Some legal scholars argue that AI should be treated strictly as an assistant, with human lawyers retaining final judgment and responsibility, while others warn that economic pressures may push firms to defer excessively to algorithmic recommendations. Regulation is beginning to emerge, requiring disclosure when AI is used, auditing for bias, and standards for accuracy. The future of AI in law will likely involve a partnership between human judgment and machine efficiency, but that partnership must be designed to protect fairness, transparency, and the rule of law, rather than sacrificing them for speed.", "title": "Artificial Intelligence in the Law"}]}, {"no": 50, "passages": [{"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 概括了碳税既激励减排也有分配与竞争力挑战的主旨；B 过于绝对；C 与文中减排激励相反；D 与文中退税论述相悖。", "answer": "A", "options": ["A carbon tax can encourage emission cuts but raises fairness and competitiveness concerns.", "A carbon tax has no effect on emissions.", "A carbon tax always harms the economy.", "A carbon tax is the same as a ban."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 与文中价格信号相反；B 与提高化石燃料成本、激励清洁替代一致；C 与碳税机制相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "B", "options": ["A carbon tax makes fossil fuels cheaper.", "A carbon tax raises fossil fuel prices, encouraging cleaner alternatives.", "A carbon tax bans all energy use.", "A carbon tax only affects households."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how does a carbon tax work?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中低收入家庭负担相反；C 与能源支出占比更高一致；B 与分配效应相悖；D 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["A carbon tax affects everyone equally.", "Low-income families never use energy.", "Low-income households spend a larger share of income on energy.", "Wealthy families bear the heaviest burden."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about the distributional impact?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与作者主张退税或补贴相反；D 与退还收入、补贴清洁技术和边境调节的建议一致；B 与文中竞争力讨论相悖；C 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Ignoring fairness concerns.", "Keeping all revenue without returning it.", "Banning carbon taxes entirely.", "Returning revenue, subsidizing clean technology, and using border adjustments."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which design?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 符合平衡分析的语气；B 与讨论公平不符；C 过于极端；D 未表现出乐观。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Balanced and analytical.", "Purely descriptive.", "Completely dismissive.", "Uncritically optimistic."], "prompt": "What is the author's tone?"}], "text": "A carbon tax, which charges emitters for each tonne of carbon dioxide they release, is widely regarded by economists as one of the most efficient tools for addressing climate change. By making fossil fuels more expensive, it creates a continuous incentive to reduce emissions, improve energy efficiency, and invest in cleaner alternatives, while allowing businesses and households to choose the cheapest ways to cut pollution. Unlike prescriptive regulations that mandate specific technologies, a carbon tax harnesses market forces, encouraging innovation across sectors. Several countries and regions have implemented carbon pricing, with evidence suggesting that it can reduce emissions without causing major economic disruption when designed carefully. Yet the policy faces significant political and social challenges. A carbon tax can be regressive, as low-income households spend a larger share of their income on energy and transport, and may struggle to afford higher prices or to invest in energy-efficient alternatives. Competitiveness concerns also arise: if one country imposes a carbon tax while others do not, energy-intensive industries may relocate to jurisdictions with weaker regulations, a phenomenon known as carbon leakage, which undermines environmental goals without protecting jobs. Design choices therefore matter greatly. Returning revenue to households through rebates, investing in public transport and clean technology, and providing targeted support for vulnerable groups can address fairness concerns. Border carbon adjustments, which impose charges on imports from countries without equivalent pricing, can reduce leakage and encourage broader adoption. The level of the tax also matters: too low, and it will have little effect; too high, too quickly, and it may cause economic hardship and political backlash. Ultimately, a carbon tax is not a silver bullet, but a powerful instrument that works best as part of a broader package of policies, including regulation, investment, and international cooperation, that together can steer economies toward a low-carbon future.", "title": "The Carbon Tax Debate"}, {"questions": [{"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中注意力成为稀缺资源相反；B 过于绝对；C 与注意力经济既创造价值也有认知与民主风险的主旨一致；D 与文中平台设计论述相悖。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Attention is unlimited and free.", "Attention has no economic value.", "The attention economy creates value but also cognitive and democratic risks.", "Only advertisers care about attention."], "prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 与文中免费服务商业模式相反；C 与用户注意力出售给广告商一致；B 与平台盈利模式相悖；D 文中未提及。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Free platforms earn money from nothing.", "Users pay directly for all services.", "Free services sell user attention to advertisers.", "Attention cannot be measured."], "prompt": "According to the passage, how do free platforms make money?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 与文中碎片化注意力相反；D 与持续切换和通知削弱深度专注一致；B 与认知研究相悖；C 与文中论述不符。", "answer": "D", "options": ["Constant notifications improve concentration.", "Multitasking always increases productivity.", "Attention is unaffected by design.", "Constant switching and notifications undermine deep focus."], "prompt": "What does the author imply about attention and focus?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 与设计更健康、保护隐私和培养注意力的建议一致；B 与作者主张改革相反；C 与文中民主担忧相悖；D 与作者立场不符。", "answer": "A", "options": ["Designing healthier platforms, protecting privacy, and cultivating attention.", "Maximizing engagement at any cost.", "Ignoring the effects on democracy.", "Banning all digital media."], "prompt": "It can be inferred that the author favors which response?"}, {"analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 过于悲观；B 与讨论风险相反；C 与既承认数字服务价值又强调注意力保护的立场一致；D 与全文分析不符。", "answer": "C", "options": ["Completely pessimistic about technology.", "Indifferent to attention.", "Concerned but constructive, valuing attention as a resource to protect.", "Hostile toward all media."], "prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward the attention economy?"}], "text": "In the digital age, attention has become one of the most valuable commodities, traded, measured, and fought over by companies whose business models depend on capturing as much of it as possible. Social media platforms, search engines, and streaming services offer free content in exchange for time, collecting data about user behavior and selling advertisers access to that attention. The more time users spend scrolling, clicking, and watching, the more revenue platforms generate, creating powerful incentives to design products that are difficult to put down. Notifications, autoplay, infinite scrolling, and personalized recommendations are not accidental features but deliberate techniques rooted in psychology, exploiting curiosity, social validation, and the fear of missing out. The consequences extend beyond wasted time. Constant switching between tasks and notifications fragments attention, making deep focus, sustained reading, and reflective thinking more difficult. Research links heavy use of attention-capturing technologies to reduced concentration, poorer sleep, and increased anxiety, particularly among young people whose brains are still developing. At a societal level, the attention economy shapes public discourse: content that provokes outrage or surprise spreads more easily than nuanced analysis, potentially distorting democratic debate and reinforcing polarization. Yet attention is also essential for learning, creativity, and relationships, and the ability to direct it intentionally is a key skill in an information-rich world. Some designers and policymakers advocate for healthier alternatives: platforms that prioritize user wellbeing over engagement metrics, tools that help people manage screen time, and education that teaches attention as a skill to be cultivated rather than a resource to be harvested. Regulation may also play a role, through limits on manipulative design, greater transparency about algorithms, and stronger privacy protections. Ultimately, the attention economy raises a fundamental question about what kind of digital environment societies want: one that exploits human psychology for profit, or one that respects attention as a finite and precious resource, supporting focus, reflection, and genuine human connection.", "title": "The Attention Economy"}]}];
